window.PLAZA_TERRITORY = [{"state":"BCN","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The Tijuana corridor was run as a plaza of Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo's Guadalajara federation; the Arellano Félix brothers arrived in Tijuana in the mid-1980s under local boss Jesús Labra. Violence was low and trafficking centred on marijuana and Colombian cocaine through San Ysidro and Mexicali."},{"from":1989,"to":2001,"primary":"tijuana","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"After Félix Gallardo's arrest (April 8, 1989) the Arellano Félix Organization took Tijuana, Tecate, Mexicali and Ensenada and fought El Chapo's Sinaloa network, a feud behind the November 1992 Christine disco attack in Puerto Vallarta and the May 24, 1993 Guadalajara airport shooting. The AFO's use of San Diego gang hitmen and 'narcojuniors' and the September 17, 1998 El Sauzal massacre near Ensenada (19 dead) marked the era."},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"primary":"tijuana","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Ramón Arellano Félix was killed in Mazatlán (February 10, 2002) and Benjamín captured in Puebla (March 9, 2002); Javier was captured at sea on August 14, 2006, leaving Eduardo and nephew Luis Fernando Sánchez Arellano 'El Ingeniero' in charge. Sinaloa pressed on Mexicali and Tijuana while AFO cells such as Teodoro García Simental's turned to kidnapping."},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"primary":"tijuana","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"An April 26, 2008 shootout in Tijuana exposed the split between 'El Teo' García Simental and Sánchez Arellano; after Eduardo's arrest (October 25, 2008) El Teo allied with the Sinaloa Cartel and waged open war in Tijuana, Rosarito and Tecate. Tijuana logged roughly 840 homicides in 2008, and El Teo's associate Santiago Meza 'El Pozolero' admitted in January 2009 to dissolving some 300 bodies."},{"from":2010,"to":2015,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["tijuana"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"El Teo was arrested in La Paz on January 12, 2010 and Sinaloa operators became dominant across Tijuana, Mexicali, Tecate and Ensenada, while the diminished AFO under 'El Ingeniero' kept a toll-collecting role until his June 2014 arrest. Homicides fell to their lowest level in a decade (364 in Tijuana in 2012)."},{"from":2016,"to":2017,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","tijuana"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"CJNG entered Tijuana allied with AFO remnants rebranded as the Cártel de Tijuana Nueva Generación (2016), contesting Sinaloa's retail methamphetamine and heroin markets in the east of the city after El Chapo's extradition; the CTNG-CJNG alignment was short-lived. Tijuana homicides jumped from 910 in 2016 to about 1,780 in 2017."},{"from":2018,"to":2020,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","tijuana"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"Tijuana was ranked the world's most violent city for 2018 (2,519 homicides, about 138 per 100,000) and 2019 (about 2,367), driven by cell-level fights over street sales and crossings. From 2019 the Sinaloa structure itself split between Los Chapitos and El Mayo's Los Rusos in Mexicali and San Luis Río Colorado, and the CTNG-CJNG alliance frayed by 2020."},{"from":2021,"to":2023,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["chapitos","mayiza","cjng"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"Tijuana stayed close to 2,000 homicides a year (2,058 in the CCSPJP tally for 2022, about 105 per 100,000) as Chapitos cells consolidated Ensenada and much of Tijuana, Los Rusos held the Mexicali Valley, and CJNG-AFO cells fought in eastern Tijuana and Tecate. Ensenada and Tecate saw their worst years as fentanyl production and coastal cocaine landings grew."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["mayiza","chapitos","cjng"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"El Mayo's capture (July 25, 2024) turned the Chapitos-Rusos rivalry into open war; Tijuana homicides fell from 1,809 (2024) to 1,219 (2025) by the municipal SSPCM count, yet the Mexico Peace Index 2026 named the state the main CJNG-Sinaloa battleground. On April 21, 2026 state security secretary Laureano Carrillo described an Arellano Félix-CJNG-Chapitos alliance against La Mayiza (Los Rusos dominant in Mexicali, Los Mayos in Rosarito, Chapitos in Ensenada and San Felipe, Tecate and San Quintín disputed); after El Mencho's death (February 22, 2026) the CJNG-Chapitos pact broke, which the same official reported on May 28, 2026, and Los Rusos were blamed for killing two state police (FESC) officers on June 26 (Tijuana) and June 29, 2026 (Mexicali) after operations against the cell."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Tijuana","lat":32.5149,"lng":-117.0382,"why":"San Ysidro and Otay Mesa are the busiest land crossings into California and the city is a major retail market for meth, heroin and fentanyl.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2009,"cartel":"tijuana"},{"from":2010,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Mexicali","lat":32.6245,"lng":-115.4523,"why":"Calexico crossings and Mexicali Valley airstrips and farm roads make it the eastern gateway, held by El Mayo's Los Rusos (Juan José Ponce Félix 'El Ruso'), a cell descended from El Macho Prieto's network and aligned with Los Chapitos' rivals since about 2019.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2007,"cartel":"tijuana"},{"from":2008,"to":2018,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2019,"to":2026,"cartel":"mayiza"}]},{"city":"Tecate","lat":32.5764,"lng":-116.6266,"why":"Small crossing with mountain smuggling trails and tunnels between Tijuana and Mexicali, fought over by Mayo-aligned cells and CJNG-Chapitos cells in 2025-2026 with no clear winner.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2009,"cartel":"tijuana"},{"from":2010,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Ensenada","lat":31.8667,"lng":-116.5964,"why":"Pacific port for maritime cocaine and precursor chemicals, with meth labs in the valleys and a Chapitos stronghold since about 2019; Los Rusos were probing it via Valle de la Trinidad in 2026.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2009,"cartel":"tijuana"},{"from":2010,"to":2018,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2019,"to":2026,"cartel":"chapitos"}]},{"city":"Playas de Rosarito","lat":32.3661,"lng":-117.0611,"why":"Coastal landing point for panga boats and a retail and extortion market on the Tijuana-Ensenada corridor, held by Los Mayos according to state security officials in April 2026.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2009,"cartel":"tijuana"},{"from":2010,"to":2023,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"cartel":"mayiza"}]},{"city":"San Quintín","lat":30.4833,"lng":-115.95,"why":"Farm belt and coastline where cocaine arriving from Baja California Sur is landed and moved north; disputed by Mayo and Chapo factions.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2009,"cartel":"tijuana"},{"from":2010,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]}],"facts":["Tijuana recorded 2,519 homicides in 2018 (about 138 per 100,000), making it the Citizens' Council (CCSPJP) most violent city in the world for 2018, and it topped the ranking again for 2019 with about 2,367 killings.","The Arellano Félix Organization ran Tijuana from 1989: Ramón was killed in Mazatlán on February 10, 2002, Benjamín arrested on March 9, 2002, Javier captured at sea off La Paz on August 14, 2006 and Eduardo arrested in Tijuana on October 25, 2008.","On September 17, 1998 gunmen tied to the AFO killed 19 members of one family at El Sauzal near Ensenada, one of the worst massacres of the pre-2006 era.","Teodoro 'El Teo' García Simental broke with the AFO in 2008, allied with the Sinaloa Cartel and was arrested in La Paz on January 12, 2010; his associate Santiago Meza 'El Pozolero' confessed in January 2009 to dissolving about 300 bodies in caustic soda.","Tijuana's municipal security secretariat (SSPCM) counted 1,809 homicides in 2024 and 1,219 in 2025, a 590-case drop, yet the city still ranked 18th among the world's 50 most violent cities in the CCSPJP 2025 ranking.","Los Rusos, the Mayo-aligned Sinaloa cell based in the Mexicali Valley and San Luis Río Colorado, have fought Los Chapitos since about 2019; on August 20, 2026 the federal security cabinet, working with DEA intelligence, seized about 120 kg of fentanyl pills (some 4 million doses) at a Mexicali property linked to the group.","According to the Mexico Peace Index 2026 (IEP), about 55% of the roughly 30,000 homicides attributed nationally to the CJNG-Sinaloa Cartel conflict over the past decade occurred in Baja California, chiefly in Tijuana, Mexicali and Ensenada.","Baja California's security secretary reported on May 28, 2026 that the CJNG-Chapitos alliance in the state had broken after El Mencho was killed in a military operation in Tapalpa, Jalisco on February 22, 2026, opening a new fight over Tijuana, Mexicali, Tecate and Ensenada."],"sources":["https://www.telemundo20.com/noticias/local/tijuana-cierra-homicidios-menos-2024-spcm/2498700/","https://zetatijuana.com/2026/04/caf-cjng-y-chapitos-aliados-en-bc-carrillo/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2026/05/28/rompen-cjng-y-cartel-de-sinaloa-en-baja-california-comienzan-nueva-guerra-por-el-control-del-estado/","https://www.kqed.org/news/11735361/in-tijuana-police-grapple-with-worlds-worst-homicide-rate","https://guillermoortega.com/pais/de-los-rusos-al-cjng-los-grupos-criminales-que-disputan-mexicali","https://www.borderlandbeat.com/2026/06/los-rusos-retaliate-for-operations.html","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2026/08/20/un-golpe-mas-a-los-rusos-aseguran-120-kilos-de-pastillas-de-fentanilo-en-predio-de-mexicali/","https://www.sinaloahoy.com.mx/portal/indice-de-paz-mexico-2026-ubica-a-baja-california-como-la-entidad-mas-afectada-por-cjng-y-cartel-de-sinaloa/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/02/12/mexico-lidera-ranking-de-ciudades-mas-violentas-en-2025-con-17-urbes-en-la-lista/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tijuana_Cartel"],"verified":true},{"state":"BCS","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The peninsula served the Guadalajara federation as a transit zone of clandestine airstrips and boat landings for Colombian cocaine and Sinaloa marijuana moving toward Baja California. No local organization existed and violence was negligible."},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["tijuana"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Sinaloa Federation operators used the Mazatlán-La Paz ferry, the Comondú farm belt and the Pacific coast, while the Arellano Félix family kept homes and logistics in La Paz and Los Cabos; Javier Arellano Félix was captured by the US Coast Guard off La Paz on August 14, 2006. Baja California Sur was among Mexico's least violent states, with 25-50 murders a year in the 2000s."},{"from":2008,"to":2013,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["tijuana","beltran_leyva"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"El Teo García Simental was captured in La Paz's Fidepaz complex on January 12, 2010, ending the AFO splinter's refuge there. Sinaloa consolidated the state, though Beltrán Leyva remnants from Sinaloa (Los Mazatlecos) began appearing in La Paz; homicides stayed in the dozens per year."},{"from":2014,"to":2016,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["beltran_leyva","cjng"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"La Paz became a battleground between the Sinaloa structure run locally by Dámaso López's people and Beltrán Leyva/Los Mazatlecos cells, with CJNG probing Los Cabos. Homicides rose from 70 (2014) to 151 (2015) and 192 (2016)."},{"from":2017,"to":2017,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","beltran_leyva"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"After El Chapo's extradition the war between Chapitos loyalists and Dámaso López's faction, which courted CJNG, exploded in La Paz and Los Cabos: 560 homicide victims in SESNSP data (state and death-certificate counts run near 790), a rate InSight Crime put at 78.6 per 100,000, the second-highest state rate in Mexico; on December 19-20, 2017 six bodies were found hanging from bridges in Los Cabos and La Paz. The CCSPJP ranked Los Cabos the most violent city in the world for 2017."},{"from":2018,"to":2019,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Dámaso López's arrest (May 2, 2017) and a heavy federal and Gendarmerie deployment in Los Cabos let Sinaloa-aligned cells consolidate; homicides fell to 185 in 2018 (SESNSP) and roughly 100 in 2019, and Los Cabos and La Paz dropped out of the world ranking."},{"from":2020,"to":2024,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["chapitos","cjng"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"One of the calmest states in the country: Sinaloa cells led locally by René Bastidas Mercado '00' (La Chapiza) ran the tourist retail markets of Los Cabos and La Paz with little open conflict, and 2024 closed with roughly 55 homicide investigations."},{"from":2025,"to":2026,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["mayiza","chapitos"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"Spillover from the Sinaloa war: 2025's 112-113 homicide investigations roughly doubled 2024 and were the most since 2018 as cells in all five municipalities realigned between Los Mayos (the freed operator Abraham Cervantes 'El Babay', reported allied with them in April 2025, was blamed by the governor for narcomantas and the April 22, 2025 killing of police commander Ulises Cota) and Los Chapitos under '00'. A May 31, 2026 shootout in San José del Cabo killed a US citizen and wounded two soldiers and four civilians, one of whom died on June 3."}],"plazas":[{"city":"La Paz","lat":24.1426,"lng":-110.3128,"why":"State capital and ferry port to Mazatlán and Topolobampo, the main local drug market and epicentre of the 2014-2017 killings.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Cabo San Lucas","lat":22.8905,"lng":-109.9167,"why":"Resort retail market and marina landing point that made Los Cabos the world's most violent city in 2017.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"San José del Cabo","lat":23.0631,"lng":-109.7019,"why":"International airport and hotel corridor; scene of renewed Sinaloa-faction shootings in 2025-2026, including the May 31, 2026 firefight that killed a US citizen.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Ciudad Constitución","lat":25.0322,"lng":-111.6656,"why":"Comondú farm belt with airstrips and marijuana and meth production feeding the peninsula route north.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Santa Rosalía","lat":27.3389,"lng":-112.2669,"why":"Gulf ferry link to Guaymas, Sonora, tying the peninsula to the Sonoran corridor.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]}],"facts":["Baja California Sur homicides climbed from 70 in 2014 to 151 in 2015, 192 in 2016 and 560 in 2017 (SESNSP victims), a rate InSight Crime put at 78.6 per 100,000, the second highest of any Mexican state that year; the state's own tally for 2017 is about 788 killings.","The CCSPJP ranked Los Cabos the most violent city in the world for 2017 (365 homicides, about 111 per 100,000), with La Paz also on the list; both fell off the ranking for 2018.","Javier Arellano Félix, then head of the Tijuana Cartel, was captured by the US Coast Guard aboard a boat off La Paz on August 14, 2006.","Teodoro 'El Teo' García Simental was arrested by Federal Police in La Paz on January 12, 2010 after fleeing the Tijuana war.","The 2017 bloodshed grew out of the split between El Chapo's sons and Dámaso López 'El Licenciado', who sought CJNG as an ally; Dámaso was arrested in Mexico City on May 2, 2017.","On December 19-20, 2017 six bodies were found hanging from bridges in Los Cabos and La Paz, with narcomantas left beside some of them, emblematic of that year's fight over the resort market.","State prosecutors opened 113 homicide investigations in 2025 (112 victims in SESNSP data), roughly double 2024 and the most since 2018; the surge followed the April 2025 return of 'El Babay', a Mayo-aligned operator released from the Villa Aldama federal prison.","On May 31, 2026 a shootout in San José del Cabo killed a California man uninvolved in the dispute and wounded two soldiers and four civilians; troops seized a grenade launcher and five rifles, and the US Embassy issued a security alert."],"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/news/analysis/expanding-mexico-violence-baja-california-sur/","https://justiceinmexico.org/violence-baja-california-sur/","https://peninsulardigital.com/2026/05/27/bcs-entre-los-estados-con-mas-delitos-por-habitante-en-mexico-durante-2026/","https://oem.com.mx/elsudcaliforniano/policiaca/2025-el-periodo-mas-violento-en-siete-anos-en-bcs-repuntan-homicidios-y-delitos-de-alto-impacto-27482064","https://zetatijuana.com/2025/05/narcoguerra-abarca-los-5-municipios/","https://www.razon.com.mx/estados/2025/04/24/quien-es-el-babay-supuesto-aliado-de-la-mayiza/","https://insightcrime.org/special-series-sinaloa-cartel-internal-war/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teodoro_Garc%C3%ADa_Simental"],"verified":true},{"state":"SON","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Rafael Caro Quintero's wing of the Guadalajara Cartel used Caborca, Sonoyta and Agua Prieta to move Sinaloa marijuana into Arizona, with his brother Miguel based in Agua Prieta. The February 1985 murder of DEA agent Enrique Camarena brought a crackdown but little local violence."},{"from":1989,"to":2001,"primary":"sonora","secondary":["sinaloa","juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Miguel Caro Quintero's Sonora cartel ran marijuana and cocaine through Agua Prieta, Caborca and Hermosillo, coexisting with El Chapo's network (the first known cross-border drug tunnel was found at Agua Prieta-Douglas in May 1990) and Amado Carrillo's Juárez organization. Miguel was arrested in Los Mochis on December 20, 2001."},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"The Sinaloa Federation absorbed the Sonora routes, with the Beltrán Leyva brothers overseeing the state and Los Salazar (Navojoa-Álamos) as enforcers, joined by Gente Nueva from about 2007. On May 16, 2007 some 50 gunmen attacked Cananea, and the raid and pursuit left more than 20 dead including five police officers."},{"from":2008,"to":2010,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["beltran_leyva","juarez"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"The January 2008 Beltrán Leyva split turned Nogales, Hermosillo and Agua Prieta into battlegrounds between Chapo and Mayo cells (Gente Nueva, Los Salazar, El Macho Prieto in San Luis Río Colorado and Puerto Peñasco) and Beltrán Leyva allies. A July 1, 2010 gun battle near Tubutama left 21 gunmen dead."},{"from":2011,"to":2017,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["beltran_leyva"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Sinaloa consolidated: Adán Salazar Zamorano was arrested in November 2011 but his sons expanded Los Salazar into Hermosillo and Cajeme; El Macho Prieto was killed by marines in Puerto Peñasco on December 18, 2013; Chapo Isidro's Beltrán Leyva remnants kept a foothold around Guaymas and Empalme. Rafael Caro Quintero, freed on August 9, 2013, began rebuilding a network in Caborca."},{"from":2018,"to":2021,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["chapitos","caborca","cjng"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"Chapitos-aligned Los Salazar, Gente Nueva, Los Cazadores and Los Deltas fought Caro Quintero's Caborca Cartel (Los Páez, Los Paleteros, with La Línea and Chapo Isidro as allies) for Caborca, Altar, Pitiquito and Sonoyta, while La Línea battled Los Salazar in the Sierra (LeBarón massacre, November 4, 2019). Cajeme became one of Mexico's most violent municipalities and Sonora set a record of 1,765 homicides in 2021 (up 20% on 2020); CJNG kept a limited presence in San Luis Río Colorado, Empalme and the south."},{"from":2022,"to":2024,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["chapitos","caborca","mayiza"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"Caro Quintero was captured in San Simón, Choix, Sinaloa on July 15, 2022 and his nephew Rodrigo Páez 'El R' near Guadalajara on April 30, 2023, yet the Caborca Cartel held its western border municipalities against the Chapitos. Around 2024 Iván Archivaldo Guzmán's attempt to monopolise fentanyl drove Los Salazar, Los Cazadores and Los Paredes to break with Los Chapitos, spreading fighting to Cajeme (324 homicides in 2024), San Luis Río Colorado (238) and Hermosillo (153)."},{"from":2025,"to":2026,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["mayiza","chapitos","caborca"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"The state split into blocs: Mayiza cells (Los Rusos in San Luis Río Colorado, Sonoyta, Puerto Peñasco and Caborca; Los Gigios in Nogales; Los Licenciados in Hermosillo and the south) allied with the Salazar-led 'Cártel Independiente de Sonora' (Los Salazar, Los Cazadores, Los Paredes) and Beltrán Leyva remnants in Cajeme-Guaymas-Empalme, against Chapitos cells (Los Deltas in Caborca and Altar, Los Memos in Sonoyta, La Plaza in Ciudad Obregón, Mata Salas in Hermosillo) and CJNG; the Caborca Cartel kept about 13 western municipalities. Caro Quintero was sent to the US on February 27, 2025; state homicides fell about 20% in 2025 (1,138 vs 1,417 by the citizens' security committee count) but Hermosillo rose 66% to 254 and Cajeme 13% to 367, and Ciudad Obregón ranked ninth in the world for 2025 (90.81 per 100,000) with Hermosillo also entering the CCSPJP top 50."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Nogales","lat":31.3186,"lng":-110.9459,"why":"Largest Arizona crossing (Mariposa and DeConcini) with a long history of tunnels; El Mayo's people (now Los Gigios) have held it since the 2010s, with CJNG probing in 2026.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2001,"cartel":"sonora"},{"from":2002,"to":2021,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2022,"to":2026,"cartel":"mayiza"}]},{"city":"Agua Prieta","lat":31.3258,"lng":-109.5489,"why":"Douglas crossing and site of the first known drug tunnel (1990); the Caro Quintero brothers' original Sonora base, now run by Los Paredes of the Salazar-led Cártel Independiente de Sonora.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2001,"cartel":"sonora"},{"from":2002,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"San Luis Río Colorado","lat":32.4767,"lng":-114.7622,"why":"Crossing to Yuma and the Mexicali Valley, stronghold of El Mayo's Los Rusos and one of Sonora's most violent cities in 2024 (238 homicides).","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2001,"cartel":"sonora"},{"from":2002,"to":2018,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2019,"to":2026,"cartel":"mayiza"}]},{"city":"Caborca","lat":30.7167,"lng":-112.15,"why":"Rafael Caro Quintero's home base and hub of desert routes to Arizona, disputed by the Caborca Cartel, the Chapitos' Los Deltas and Mayo-aligned Los Rusos and Los Cazadores since 2018.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2001,"cartel":"sonora"},{"from":2002,"to":2018,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2019,"to":2026,"cartel":"caborca"}]},{"city":"Sonoyta","lat":31.8619,"lng":-112.8506,"why":"Lukeville crossing and the Organ Pipe desert corridor toward Phoenix, a constant flashpoint between the Caborca Cartel, Los Rusos and the Chapitos' Los Memos.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2001,"cartel":"sonora"},{"from":2002,"to":2018,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2019,"to":2026,"cartel":"caborca"}]},{"city":"Ciudad Obregón","lat":27.4828,"lng":-109.9304,"why":"Southern hub of the Yaqui Valley where Los Salazar, the Chapitos' La Plaza and Beltrán Leyva remnants collide; Cajeme is repeatedly among Mexico's most violent municipalities (367 homicides in 2025).","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2001,"cartel":"sonora"},{"from":2002,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]}],"facts":["Sonora's homicides peaked at 1,765 in 2021, up 20% on 2020, with Cajeme (Ciudad Obregón) the most violent municipality; Ciudad Obregón was second in the world in the CCSPJP ranking for 2021 (about 156 per 100,000), stayed in the top three for 2022-2023 (about 138 per 100,000) and ranked ninth for 2025 (90.81 per 100,000).","On November 4, 2019 gunmen from La Línea, fighting Los Salazar for smuggling routes, killed three women and six children of the LeBarón and Langford families at La Mora, Bavispe.","Rafael Caro Quintero, figurehead of the Caborca Cartel, was captured in San Simón, Choix, Sinaloa on July 15, 2022 and sent to the United States with 28 other prisoners on February 27, 2025; his nephew Rodrigo Páez Quintero 'El R' was arrested in the Guadalajara area on April 30, 2023.","Miguel Caro Quintero's Sonora cartel, based in Agua Prieta, dominated the state through the 1990s until his arrest in Los Mochis on December 20, 2001; the first known cross-border drug tunnel was discovered between Agua Prieta and Douglas, Arizona in May 1990.","Gonzalo Inzunza 'El Macho Prieto', El Mayo Zambada's chief enforcer who ran the Mexicali, San Luis Río Colorado, Sonoyta, Puerto Peñasco and Caborca routes, was killed by marines in Puerto Peñasco on December 18, 2013.","On May 16, 2007 about 50 gunmen attacked Cananea; the raid and the pursuit that followed left more than 20 dead, including five police officers, and a July 1, 2010 gun battle near Tubutama killed 21 gunmen.","State homicides fell about 20% in 2025 (1,138 versus 1,417 by the Sonora citizens' public security committee count), but Hermosillo rose 66% to 254 and Cajeme 13% to 367, while San Luis Río Colorado logged 184 (down from 238), Nogales 63 and Guaymas 15.","A February 2026 mapping put the Mayiza-Cártel Independiente de Sonora-Beltrán Leyva bloc in 56 of Sonora's 72 municipalities, the Chapitos-CJNG bloc in 26, the Caborca Cartel in 13 and La Línea in 7 along the Chihuahua border."],"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/news/three-criminal-fronts-behind-violence-sonora-mexico/","https://grupoanimal.mx/opinion/la-disputa-criminal-por-sonora","https://www.elimparcial.com/son/sonora/2026/01/23/homicidios-dolosos-en-sonora-bajan-20-en-2025-pero-hermosillo-y-ciudad-obregon-registran-aumento/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/02/12/mexico-lidera-ranking-de-ciudades-mas-violentas-en-2025-con-17-urbes-en-la-lista/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LeBar%C3%B3n_and_Langford_families_massacre","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caborca_Cartel","https://www.cbsnews.com/news/mexico-extradites-drug-traffickers-us-rafael-caro-quintero/"],"verified":true},{"state":"SIN","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Badiraguato and the Golden Triangle supplied the opium poppy and marijuana that Sinaloa-born bosses Félix Gallardo, Caro Quintero and Fonseca Carrillo ran from Guadalajara after Operation Condor (1977) pushed them out. The February 1985 murder of DEA agent Camarena brought arrests that ended with Félix Gallardo's capture on April 8, 1989."},{"from":1989,"to":1993,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["tijuana","juarez"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"The federation's heirs (El Chapo, El Mayo, Héctor Palma, the Carrillo Fuentes family in Navolato) took Sinaloa, while Arellano Félix hitmen hunted Chapo and Palma allies in Culiacán and Mazatlán. Chapo was arrested in Guatemala on June 9, 1993 after the May 24, 1993 Guadalajara airport shooting."},{"from":1994,"to":2003,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"El Mayo, Juan José Esparragoza, the Beltrán Leyva brothers and the Carrillo Fuentes family shared the state under a working truce; Amado Carrillo died on July 4, 1997 and Chapo escaped Puente Grande on January 19, 2001 to rebuild from the Sinaloa sierra."},{"from":2004,"to":2007,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["juarez"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"The September 11, 2004 killing of Rodolfo Carrillo Fuentes in Culiacán broke the Sinaloa-Juárez alliance and violence rose in Culiacán and Navolato, where the Carrillo family is from. The Beltrán Leyva brothers managed Culiacán's protection network for the Federation."},{"from":2008,"to":2010,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["beltran_leyva","juarez"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"Alfredo Beltrán Leyva's arrest in Culiacán (January 21, 2008) triggered the Beltrán Leyva war; Édgar Guzmán, Chapo's son, was killed in Culiacán on May 8, 2008 and Culiacán, Navolato, Guamúchil and Mazatlán became daily battlegrounds. Sinaloa exceeded 2,200 homicides in 2010 (about 60 per 100,000), then its worst year; Arturo Beltrán Leyva was killed in Cuernavaca on December 16, 2009."},{"from":2011,"to":2016,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["beltran_leyva"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"Fausto Isidro Meza 'Chapo Isidro' and Los Mazatlecos held Guasave and much of the north and fought for Los Mochis and Sinaloa de Leyva; the state still averaged roughly 1,000-1,900 killings a year (about 30-65 per 100,000). Chapo was captured in Mazatlán on February 22, 2014, escaped on July 11, 2015 and was recaptured in Los Mochis on January 8, 2016; a rift between Dámaso López and Chapo's sons opened in 2016."},{"from":2017,"to":2019,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["chapitos","mayiza","beltran_leyva"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"After Chapo's extradition (January 19, 2017) the Dámaso López faction clashed with Los Chapitos in Culiacán, Eldorado and the south until Dámaso's arrest on May 2, 2017; Sinaloa logged about 1,500 homicides in 2017. The Culiacanazo of October 17, 2019 forced the government to release Ovidio Guzmán after Chapitos gunmen seized the city."},{"from":2020,"to":2023,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["chapitos","mayiza"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"The fentanyl boom enriched Los Chapitos while El Mayo's structure coexisted with them under an uneasy pact; state homicides fell to their lowest in more than a decade (about 20 per 100,000). Ovidio's recapture in Jesús María on January 5, 2023 cost the lives of 10 soldiers, one police officer and 19 gunmen."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["chapitos","mayiza","beltran_leyva"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"Joaquín Guzmán López's delivery of El Mayo to US agents (July 25, 2024) ignited open war from September 9, 2024: the state security council (CESP) counted 1,828 killed and 2,390 disappeared in the first year, and by July 2026 tallies exceeded 3,000 homicides and about 4,000 disappearances, with Culiacán the epicentre and Navolato, Elota, Mazatlán and the south drawn in. Los Chapitos allied with CJNG and La Mayiza with Chapo Isidro's Guasave-based Beltrán Leyva remnant (both announced May 2025); by mid-2026, per Crisis Group, La Mayiza held the south and east of Culiacán and had taken Navolato (October 2025), Los Chapitos had lost their advantage in the capital and were concentrated in Mazatlán, and Chapo Isidro's network controlled the north (Guasave, Sinaloa de Leyva, Ahome, El Fuerte). Culiacán ranked sixth in the world for 2025 (103.91 per 100,000)."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Culiacán","lat":24.8091,"lng":-107.394,"why":"Cartel capital and command centre; the epicentre of the 2008-2010 Beltrán Leyva war and of the Chapitos-Mayiza war since September 2024, in which La Mayiza gained the upper hand by 2026.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2025,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2026,"to":2026,"cartel":"mayiza"}]},{"city":"Mazatlán","lat":23.2494,"lng":-106.4111,"why":"Pacific port and tourist market where Ramón Arellano was killed (2002) and Chapo captured (2014); the Chapitos' main redoubt after losing ground in Culiacán, with fighting intensifying there in 2026.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2025,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2026,"to":2026,"cartel":"chapitos"}]},{"city":"Los Mochis","lat":25.7933,"lng":-108.9967,"why":"Northern hub with the Topolobampo port and rail line to Chihuahua; Chapo was recaptured here in January 2016, and since 2025 Ahome has been consolidated by Chapo Isidro's Mayiza-allied network.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2025,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2026,"to":2026,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"}]},{"city":"Badiraguato","lat":25.3644,"lng":-107.5508,"why":"Golden Triangle birthplace of Félix Gallardo, Caro Quintero and El Chapo; historic poppy and marijuana heartland and family redoubt of the Guzmán clan.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Navolato","lat":24.7653,"lng":-107.7019,"why":"Home ground of the Carrillo Fuentes family and a coastal municipality fought over in 2008-2010 and again since 2024; taken from Los Chapitos by La Mayiza in October 2025.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2025,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2026,"to":2026,"cartel":"mayiza"}]},{"city":"Guasave","lat":25.5667,"lng":-108.4667,"why":"Stronghold of Chapo Isidro's Los Mazatlecos, the Beltrán Leyva remnant that has contested northern Sinaloa since 2010 and allied with La Mayiza in May 2025.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1988,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1989,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2026,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"}]}],"facts":["The Chapitos-Mayiza war that began on September 9, 2024 produced 1,828 homicides and 2,390 disappearances in its first year according to the state security council (CESP), with June 2025 (207 killings) the deadliest month of the war; by July 2026 tallies exceeded 3,000 dead and about 4,000 disappeared.","The CCSPJP ranked Culiacán the sixth most violent city in the world for 2025 with 103.91 homicides per 100,000 inhabitants; the Mexico Peace Index 2026 recorded Sinaloa's homicides rising from 1,022 in 2024 to 1,732 in 2025.","Ovidio Guzmán was captured and released during the Culiacanazo of October 17, 2019; his recapture in Jesús María on January 5, 2023 left 10 soldiers, one police officer and 19 gunmen dead, and he was extradited on September 15, 2023.","Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada was arrested near El Paso on July 25, 2024 after Joaquín Guzmán López flew him out of Sinaloa; he pleaded guilty in Brooklyn on August 25, 2025 and was sentenced to life in prison on July 20, 2026.","The Beltrán Leyva split began with Alfredo Beltrán Leyva's arrest in Culiacán on January 21, 2008; Chapo's son Édgar Guzmán was killed in Culiacán on May 8, 2008 and the state surpassed 2,200 homicides in 2010.","El Chapo was captured in Mazatlán on February 22, 2014, escaped the Altiplano prison on July 11, 2015, was recaptured in Los Mochis on January 8, 2016 and extradited on January 19, 2017.","The Golden Triangle municipalities of Badiraguato, Sinaloa de Leyva and Choix have been Mexico's main opium poppy and marijuana zone since the 1940s; since the 2010s clandestine labs around Culiacán have made the state a leading producer of methamphetamine and fentanyl.","In May 2025 Ismael Zambada Sicairos 'El Mayito Flaco' and Fausto Isidro Meza 'Chapo Isidro' announced an alliance against Los Chapitos, who in turn aligned with CJNG; by July 2026 Crisis Group reported La Mayiza in control of southern and eastern Culiacán, Los Chapitos concentrated in Mazatlán and Chapo Isidro's group holding the north."],"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/special-series-sinaloa-cartel-internal-war/","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/sinaloa-cartel-profile/","https://www.milenio.com/estados/cuantos-muertos-ha-dejado-la-guerra-en-sinaloa","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/07/25/las-cifras-de-la-guerra-en-sinaloa-y-el-efecto-mayo-zambada-mas-de-mil-800-muertos-y-casi-2-mil-desaparecidos/","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/2026/7/25/la-guerra-que-devora-sinaloa-3-mil-homicidios-4-mil-desapariciones-y-1-gobernador-en-casa-376778.html","https://www.razon.com.mx/mexico/2026/07/07/mayos-ganan-terreno-en-culiacan-y-reordenan-mapa-criminal-de-sinaloa/","https://riodoce.mx/2026/06/16/sinaloa-lejos-de-la-paz-anhelada/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ismael_Zambada_Garc%C3%ADa","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_Sinaloa_unrest"],"verified":true},{"state":"CHH","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":["juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Pablo Acosta ran the Ojinaga corridor for the Guadalajara Cartel (moving tens of tons of Colombian cocaine plus marijuana and heroin) until federal police killed him at Santa Elena on 24 April 1987; ex-DFS agent Rafael Aguilar Guajardo held the Ciudad Juárez plaza and Amado Carrillo Fuentes apprenticed under Acosta. The Juárez organization became independent as Guadalajara broke up in 1989."},{"from":1990,"to":1997,"primary":"juarez","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Amado Carrillo Fuentes had Aguilar Guajardo killed (Cancún, 12 April 1993) and turned Ciudad Juárez into the world's largest cocaine gateway, flying jetliners of Colombian cocaine north (the 'Señor de los Cielos'). Sinaloa's Guzmán-Zambada network operated as junior partners in the Federation; Amado died on 4 July 1997 after plastic surgery."},{"from":1998,"to":2007,"primary":"juarez","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Vicente Carrillo Fuentes inherited the cartel; La Línea (built from state and municipal police) and the Barrio Azteca prison gang became its enforcers in Juárez. The Federation alliance with Sinaloa broke after Rodolfo Carrillo was killed in Culiacán on 11 September 2004, setting up the war."},{"from":2008,"to":2011,"primary":"juarez","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"From January 2008 Sinaloa (Gente Nueva plus the Artistas Asesinos and Mexicles gangs) fought La Línea and Barrio Azteca for Ciudad Juárez; Operativo Conjunto Chihuahua (March 2008) and a federal-police takeover failed to stop it. Juárez was the world's most violent city with about 2,600 homicides in 2009 and 3,111 in 2010 (state count 3,622); the Creel (16 August 2008, 13 dead) and Villas de Salvárcar (31 January 2010, 15 dead) massacres marked the period, and fighting spread to Chihuahua city, Cuauhtémoc and the sierra. The state homicide rate reached about 111 per 100,000 in 2010."},{"from":2012,"to":2015,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":3,"note":"Sinaloa won the city: Juárez homicides fell to roughly 750 in 2012 and about 300 in 2015. La Línea retreated to the northwest (Namiquipa, Madera, Cuauhtémoc, Nuevo Casas Grandes) under Carlos Arturo Quintana 'El 80'; Vicente Carrillo Fuentes was arrested in Torreón on 9 October 2014."},{"from":2016,"to":2019,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["juarez"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"La Línea resurged after El Chapo's January 2016 recapture, fighting Gente Nueva and Los Salazar for the Sierra Tarahumara (Madera, Guadalupe y Calvo, Bocoyna, Cuauhtémoc) while a Mexicles/Artistas Asesinos versus Aztecas retail war pushed Juárez homicides from about 540 in 2016 to roughly 1,500 in 2019. The LeBarón-Langford massacre of 4 November 2019 (nine dead, blamed on La Línea) drew international attention to the northwest corridor."},{"from":2020,"to":2023,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["juarez"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"The Madera ambush (3 April 2020, 19 dead) showed the scale of the La Línea-Gente Nueva sierra war; the state logged about 2,300 homicide cases in 2020 (SESNSP), its worst year since 2011. Sinaloa-linked boss José Noriel Portillo 'El Chueco' murdered two Jesuit priests in Cerocahui (Urique) on 20 June 2022, and the Mexicles' 'Black Thursday' (11 August 2022) and the 1 January 2023 assault on Cereso 3 (17 killed inside the prison plus two gunmen; 'El Neto' freed then killed) kept Juárez above 1,100 homicides a year."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"juarez","secondary":["mayiza","chapitos","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"After El Mayo's July 2024 arrest the Sinaloa bloc fractured into the Mayiza (allied with the Mexicles, Gente Nueva del Jaguar and Los Cabrera), the Chapitos (with the Artistas Asesinos, Gente Nueva del Tigre and Los Salgueiro) and an autonomous Los Salazar; La Línea/Nuevo Cartel de Juárez, reportedly supplied by CJNG, exploited the split and by June 2026 Lantia rated it dominant in 26 of 67 municipalities (Mayiza 17, Chapitos 14, Salazar 10), including the Juárez valley, the state capital and the Jiménez-Camargo-Delicias corridor. Flashpoints were the Aldama-Coyame del Sotol-Ojinaga desert (Los Cabrera versus La Línea from September 2024, with heavy fighting on 27 May 2025 and repeated multi-fatality clashes through late 2025) and Guadalupe y Calvo (La Línea against Los Salgueiro/Gente Nueva del Tigre, with displacement); statewide SESNSP victims fell from 2,040 (2024) to 1,791 (2025) yet Chihuahua remained the second most violent state in 2025-2026."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Ciudad Juárez","lat":31.7389,"lng":-106.4869,"why":"Largest border crossing to El Paso; historic cocaine gateway and the epicenter of the 2008-2011 war and later retail-market gang wars.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2011,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2012,"to":2023,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"cartel":"juarez"}]},{"city":"Ojinaga","lat":29.5644,"lng":-104.4164,"why":"Remote Big Bend crossing to Presidio, Texas; Pablo Acosta's 1980s base and a 2024-2025 battleground between La Línea/NCDJ and Los Cabrera-Mayiza.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2011,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2012,"to":2023,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"cartel":"juarez"}]},{"city":"Chihuahua","lat":28.6353,"lng":-106.0889,"why":"State capital and highway hub linking the sierra production zones to Juárez and Ojinaga; heavy fighting 2008-2011.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2011,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2012,"to":2023,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"cartel":"juarez"}]},{"city":"Cuauhtémoc","lat":28.405,"lng":-106.8667,"why":"Gateway between the Chihuahua plateau and the Sierra Tarahumara; La Línea stronghold under 'El 80' contested by Sinaloa cells.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2011,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2012,"to":2016,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2017,"to":2026,"cartel":"juarez"}]},{"city":"Guadalupe y Calvo","lat":26.0975,"lng":-106.9631,"why":"Chihuahua's corner of the Golden Triangle (opium, marijuana, meth labs, timber); Sinaloa heartland invaded by La Línea in 2025-2026.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Nuevo Casas Grandes","lat":30.4167,"lng":-107.9167,"why":"Northwest corridor to Puerto Palomas and New Mexico; La Línea's rear base (Janos, Ascensión, Madera) since 2016.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2011,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2012,"to":2015,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2016,"to":2026,"cartel":"juarez"}]}],"facts":["Ciudad Juárez recorded 3,111 homicides in 2010 by the federal/AP count (3,622 by the state count), a rate around 270 per 100,000, the highest in the world; Chihuahua led all Mexican states in homicides from 2008 to 2011 and its 2010 statewide rate was about 111 per 100,000.","Pablo Acosta Villarreal, who moved an estimated 60 tons of cocaine a year through Ojinaga, was killed by Mexican federal police with FBI support at Santa Elena on 24 April 1987; Amado Carrillo Fuentes died on 4 July 1997 and Vicente Carrillo Fuentes was arrested in Torreón on 9 October 2014.","On 3 April 2020 some 40 La Línea gunmen (about 60 by the National Guard's estimate) ambushed a Gente Nueva del Jaguar (Sinaloa) column near Chuhuichupa, Madera, killing 19; another 18 people died in the Madera area the same day.","The LeBarón-Langford massacre of 4 November 2019 killed three women and six children of the dual-national Mormon colonies (based in Galeana, Chihuahua) at La Mora, Bavispe, on the Sonora-Chihuahua line; investigators blamed La Línea, then at war with Los Salazar.","On 1 January 2023 the Mexicles gang stormed Cereso 3 in Ciudad Juárez, killing 10 guards and 7 inmates (two attackers also died), freeing about 30 prisoners including leader Ernesto Piñón 'El Neto', who was shot dead by police on 5 January.","Ciudad Juárez homicides fell from 1,172 (2023) to about 1,104 (2024) and 926 (2025); statewide SESNSP victims dropped from 2,040 to 1,791 over 2024-2025, still the second-highest total in Mexico behind Guanajuato.","A June 2026 Lantia analysis (published by Noroeste) rated La Línea dominant in 26 municipalities (present in 45 of 67), the Mayiza in 17 (present in 53), the Chapitos in 14 (present in 30) and Los Salazar in 10 (present in 26), with CJNG present sporadically in four."],"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/news/analysis/ciudad-juarez-records-record-murders/","https://acleddata.com/report/how-sinaloa-cartel-rift-redrawing-mexicos-criminal-map","https://politica.expansion.mx/estados/2026/04/28/chihuahua-el-segundo-estado-mas-violento-y-en-disputa-entre-el-cartel-de-juarez-y-el-de-sinaloa","https://www.noroeste.com.mx/colaboraciones/chihuahua-una-entidad-acosada-por-el-crimen-organizado-HE23050034","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madera_ambush","https://diario.mx/juarez/2025/dec/31/incrementan-aqui-los-asesinatos-a-golpes-1099924.html","https://eldiariodechihuahua.mx/local/2026/jan/03/fue-2025-el-de-menos-asesinatos-en-10-anos-761331.html","https://puentelibre.mx/local/chihuahua-cerro-el-2025-con-mil-791-ejecutados/","https://lahoguera.mx/entre-2024-y-2025-zacatecas-y-chiapas-bajan-homicidios-pero-crecen-en-sinaloa-y-veracruz/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_Ciudad_Ju%C3%A1rez_prison_attack","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/12/03/disputa-entre-los-cabrera-sarabia-y-la-linea-seria-la-causante-de-la-violencia-en-ojinaga-chihuahua/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infighting_in_the_Sinaloa_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mexican_states_by_homicides"],"verified":true},{"state":"DUR","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":["other"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"The Durango sierra (Tamazula, Canelas, Topia, Guanaceví) supplied opium and marijuana to the Guadalajara Cartel, while the home-grown Herrera family organization (Jaime Herrera Nevares) ran a heroin pipeline from Durango to Chicago. Violence stayed local and rural."},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"The Golden Triangle municipalities became the core production zone and refuge of the Sinaloa Federation (Guzmán, Zambada, the Beltrán Leyva brothers), while Amado Carrillo's Juárez Cartel handled La Laguna (Gómez Palacio-Lerdo) through the 1990s. Durango remained one of the quieter northern states (about 16 homicides per 100,000 in 2007)."},{"from":2008,"to":2012,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["zetas","beltran_leyva","gulf"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"Los Zetas (the Gulf Cartel's armed wing until their 2010 split) pushed into La Laguna and Durango city while the Beltrán Leyva split and an internal Sinaloa purge (the Cabrera Sarabia cell against a rival faction) filled clandestine graves: 383 bodies were exhumed in Durango city, Lerdo and Santiago Papasquiaro between April 2011 and 2012. Gómez Palacio prison inmates were let out at night to massacre rivals in Torreón in 2010, and the state homicide rate passed 60 per 100,000 in 2010 (about 61)."},{"from":2013,"to":2015,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["zetas"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Los Cabrera, run from Santiago Papasquiaro, drove the Zetas out of Gómez Palacio-Lerdo as the Treviño Morales brothers fell (July 2013, March 2015). Marines nearly captured El Chapo in Tamazula in October 2015, confirming the sierra as his redoubt."},{"from":2016,"to":2023,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Undisputed Sinaloa hegemony through Los Cabrera, who also policed common crime, made Durango one of Mexico's lowest-homicide states (about 8 per 100,000 in 2020); CJNG made only brief incursions into Durango city and Mezquital. Opium, marijuana and synthetic-drug production continued in the Golden Triangle."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["mayiza","chapitos"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"Los Cabrera sided with Ismael Zambada Sicairos 'Mayito Flaco', sending Grupo Flechas gunmen into Sinaloa from September 2024, while Aureliano Guzmán 'El Guano' anchors the Chapitos in Tamazula. A light aircraft reportedly linked to Los Chapitos dropped explosives on Vascogil (a Cabrera stronghold) on 28 October 2024 and on Tamazula on 2 November 2024, drone-bomb attacks followed, Gente del Guano blockaded El Durazno (Tamazula) in August 2025 and families were displaced from El Durazno and nearby communities in 2025-2026, but La Laguna and the capital stayed calm."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Durango","lat":24.0277,"lng":-104.6532,"why":"State capital and highway node between the sierra and the north-center; site of the 2011-2012 mass graves.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Gómez Palacio","lat":25.5694,"lng":-103.4958,"why":"Durango half of the La Laguna conurbation with Torreón; Sinaloa's base for the 2008-2013 war with the Zetas.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2006,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2007,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Tamazula","lat":24.9667,"lng":-106.9667,"why":"Golden Triangle municipality bordering Sinaloa; El Chapo's refuge, now the Gente del Guano (Chapitos) stronghold fought over with the Mayiza.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2023,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"cartel":"chapitos"}]},{"city":"Santiago Papasquiaro","lat":25.0433,"lng":-105.4192,"why":"Home base of Los Cabrera (Cabrera Sarabia), the Sinaloa cell that governs most of Durango and backs the Mayiza.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2023,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"cartel":"mayiza"}]},{"city":"Mezquital","lat":23.4761,"lng":-104.3917,"why":"Southern sierra route toward Nayarit, Jalisco and Zacatecas; the one area where CJNG probed Durango.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]}],"facts":["Between April 2011 and 2012 authorities exhumed 383 bodies (357 men, 26 women) from clandestine graves in Durango city (Las Fuentes, San Vicente, Valle del Guadiana), Lerdo, Santiago Papasquiaro, Cuencamé and San Juan del Río; backhoes mangled the remains and only 124 had been identified by 2019.","Durango's homicide rate peaked at about 61 per 100,000 in 2010, among the five highest in Mexico, fell to 36 by 2012 and to about 8 per 100,000 by 2020.","In April 2009 Archbishop Héctor González said El Chapo was living openly in Guanaceví; in October 2015 Marines raided Tamazula and Guzmán escaped injured into the sierra.","In July 2010 federal authorities revealed that inmates of the Gómez Palacio prison were released at night with guards' weapons to carry out massacres in Torreón, including the 18 July 2010 attack that killed 17 people at a party.","Los Cabrera (Cabrera Sarabia family) of Santiago Papasquiaro emerged from the 2011 intra-Sinaloa purge and the war with the Zetas as the dominant cell in all 39 municipalities; Felipe Cabrera Sarabia was arrested in December 2011.","On 28 October 2024 a light aircraft reportedly linked to Los Chapitos dropped four explosive devices on Vascogil in the Durango sierra (a Cabrera Sarabia stronghold and former El Mayo hideout), and four more were dropped on Tamazula on 2 November 2024; the army and state police later seized two aircraft, and drone-borne explosive attacks followed in the sierra.","In 2025-2026 residents of El Durazno and other Tamazula communities were displaced by fighting between Gente del Guano (Chapitos) and Mayiza-aligned groups over the Golden Triangle; Gente del Guano set up a blockade at El Durazno on 15 August 2025 and displacement was still being reported in May 2026."],"sources":["https://www.infobae.com/america/mexico/2020/11/11/el-drama-de-las-fosas-clandestinas-hallaron-383-cadaveres-en-durango-pero-las-retroexcavadoras-los-desmembraron-e-imposibilitaron-su-identificacion/","https://insightcrime.org/news/analysis/durango-ground-zero-for-zetas-sinaloa-cartel-battle/","https://www.noroeste.com.mx/colaboraciones/los-cabrera-los-duenos-de-durango-FD23644357","https://www.unotv.com/nacional/no-solo-es-chilapa-narco-desplaza-a-familias-en-tamazula-durango/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infighting_in_the_Sinaloa_Cartel","https://www.jornada.com.mx/2011/12/20/politica/005n2pol","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2024/10/30/avioneta-arroja-explosivos-en-vascogil-durango-el-sitio-que-fue-escondite-del-mayo-zambada/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2024/11/09/fuerzas-armadas-aseguran-dos-avionetas-en-durango-tras-bombardeos-en-tamazula-y-vascogil/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/04/05/chapitos-o-mayiza-quien-va-ganando-la-guerra-asi-se-ve-el-mapa-de-los-territorios-dominados-por-ambos-bandos/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mexican_states_by_homicides"],"verified":true},{"state":"COA","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":["gulf"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Coahuila was a low-profile transit state: Guadalajara-linked traffickers used the Piedras Negras and Acuña crossings and the Gulf organization reached in from Nuevo Laredo. No cartel maintained an armed presence."},{"from":1990,"to":2004,"primary":"juarez","secondary":["gulf","sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Amado Carrillo's Juárez Cartel worked Torreón-La Laguna and the Coahuila crossings while the Gulf Cartel expanded along the border. The state stayed among Mexico's safest, with a homicide rate under 7 per 100,000 as late as 2008."},{"from":2005,"to":2009,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["zetas","sinaloa","juarez"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"The Gulf Cartel, through Los Zetas (still its armed wing), turned Coahuila into a rear base, capturing police in Piedras Negras, Saltillo and Monclova and converting the Piedras Negras prison into an operations center from 2008. Sinaloa, operating from Gómez Palacio, began contesting Torreón in 2007-2008 as the Juárez Cartel's La Laguna presence faded."},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"After the February 2010 Gulf-Zetas split the Treviño Morales brothers ruled Coahuila outright: the Allende massacre (18-20 March 2011, dozens to as many as 300 victims), more than 150 people killed and incinerated inside the Piedras Negras prison, and a roughly 130-inmate tunnel escape on 17 September 2012. Torreón, fought over with Sinaloa's Laguna cells, ranked among the world's most violent cities and the state homicide rate peaked at about 27 per 100,000 in 2012."},{"from":2013,"to":2015,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":3,"note":"Z-40's capture (July 2013) and Z-42's (March 2015) broke Zetas command while Governor Rubén Moreira's state-led 'Coahuila model' (GATE special forces, purge of municipal police, later Fuerza Coahuila) took hold. Homicides fell by more than half from the 2012 peak."},{"from":2016,"to":2019,"primary":"cdn","secondary":["zetas_vieja_escuela","sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"The Cártel del Noreste, founded in Nuevo Laredo in 2015, inherited the Zetas' Coahuila border (Piedras Negras, Acuña, Guerrero, Hidalgo) while Zetas Vieja Escuela remnants lingered and Sinaloa's Cabrera cells kept La Laguna. CDN's 30 November 2019 raid on Villa Unión (23 dead) was the era's one large battle."},{"from":2020,"to":2026,"primary":"cdn","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"CDN (Tropa del Infierno) trafficks fentanyl, meth and migrants through Piedras Negras and Acuña and, per Lantia, has cells in Monclova and Saltillo, but keeps a low profile under heavy state policing; Sinaloa remains in Torreón. Coahuila became one of Mexico's safest states with 84 homicides in 2025 (the lowest in 30 years and the second-lowest state rate after Yucatán), and the US designated CDN a Foreign Terrorist Organization on 20 February 2025."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Piedras Negras","lat":28.7003,"lng":-100.5236,"why":"Border crossing to Eagle Pass; Zetas' prison-based killing center 2008-2012 and CDN's main Coahuila plaza today.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2004,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2005,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"cdn"}]},{"city":"Ciudad Acuña","lat":29.3242,"lng":-100.9314,"why":"Crossing to Del Rio and the Amistad reservoir; smuggling and migrant corridor held by Zetas then CDN.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2004,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2005,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"cdn"}]},{"city":"Saltillo","lat":25.4232,"lng":-101.0053,"why":"State capital on the Monterrey-Zacatecas axis; Zetas command hub in 2010-2012, now a CDN retail and extortion market.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2004,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2005,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"cdn"}]},{"city":"Torreón","lat":25.5428,"lng":-103.4068,"why":"La Laguna metropolis straddling Durango; the Sinaloa-Zetas battleground of 2008-2013 and Sinaloa's foothold in Coahuila.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2006,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2007,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Monclova","lat":26.9103,"lng":-101.4222,"why":"Steel city on the Saltillo-Piedras Negras highway; Zetas logistics node, now a CDN command post per Lantia.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2004,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2005,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"cdn"}]}],"facts":["On 18-20 March 2011 Zetas convoys razed homes in Allende and nearby towns after a DEA phone-tracking operation was leaked; the state investigation confirmed 28 deaths while researchers led by Sergio Aguayo and the National Security Archive found the dead and missing could exceed 100 and approach 300, most of them incinerated in fuel drums.","The Piedras Negras state prison served as a Zetas operations center from 2008 to 2012; more than 150 people were killed there and burned or dissolved in acid, and about 130 inmates escaped through a tunnel on 17 September 2012.","Coahuila's homicide rate rose from about 6.6 per 100,000 in 2008 to 23 in 2011 and a peak of 27 in 2012, then fell back below 8 by 2018; Governor Rubén Moreira acknowledged about 1,600 people disappeared to organized crime.","Vicente Carrillo Fuentes, head of the Juárez Cartel, was arrested in Torreón on 9 October 2014.","On 30 November 2019 a CDN convoy attacked the town hall of Villa Unión; the state reported 23 dead (17 gunmen, four state police, two civilians), with further gunmen killed in the pursuit on 1 December.","The US State Department designated the Cártel del Noreste a Foreign Terrorist Organization on 20 February 2025, and on 6 August 2025 Treasury sanctioned CDN figures including Antonio Romero Sánchez 'Romeo', who led operations in Piedras Negras.","Coahuila's prosecutor reported 84 intentional homicides in 2025 (down from 122 in 2024, a 31 percent drop), the lowest in 30 years, giving the state the second-lowest homicide rate in Mexico after Yucatán."],"sources":["https://nsarchive.gwu.edu/briefing-book/mexico/2021-03-18/allende-massacre-decade-impunity","https://www.fidh.org/en/region/americas/mexico/mexico-murders-disappearances-and-torture-in-coahuila-de-zaragoza-are","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019_Villa_Uni%C3%B3n_shootout","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/estados/balacera-en-villa-union-suman-23-muertos/","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0219","https://vanguardia.com.mx/coahuila/fue-el-2025-con-la-menor-cifra-de-homicidios-dolosos-en-30-anos-en-coahuila-fiscal-general-DI18545512","https://www.milenio.com/estados/coahuila-el-segundo-estado-con-menos-homicidios-en-mexico-en-2025","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/coahuila/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mexican_states_by_homicides"],"verified":true},{"state":"ZAC","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Zacatecas was a marijuana-growing and transit backwater feeding Guadalajara Cartel routes north; no organization maintained an armed presence."},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["gulf","juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The state's highways linking the Pacific and the Bajío to the northeastern border made it a shared corridor for Sinaloa and Juárez traffickers, and from the mid-2000s the Gulf Cartel and its Zetas began moving in. Homicide rates were among Mexico's lowest (about 4.5 per 100,000 in 2007)."},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["zetas","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":1,"note":"The Gulf Cartel's armed wing, Los Zetas, made Zacatecas a stronghold centered on Fresnillo and the Zacatecas-Saltillo highway, freeing 53 inmates from Cieneguillas prison on 16 May 2009 and expanding extortion and kidnapping; the homicide rate was still only about 5 per 100,000."},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["gulf","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"After the February 2010 Gulf-Zetas split the now-independent Zetas held the state; the Gulf-Zetas war and the 2012 revolt of Iván Velázquez 'El Talibán' (who had aired his grievances against the Treviño Morales brothers since late 2011) brought running battles, but the homicide rate stayed moderate (about 13 per 100,000 in 2012)."},{"from":2013,"to":2016,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["talibanes","gulf","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"After Z-40's 2013 capture the Zetas splintered; Los Talibanes (Velázquez Caballero family, allied first with the Gulf Cartel) held the Fresnillo-San Luis Potosí corridor while Sinaloa's network expanded from the north and west. Violence was diffuse rather than a full plaza war."},{"from":2017,"to":2019,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","talibanes","cdn"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"With the Zetas gone, Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada's network became the leading power while CJNG pushed in from the Jalisco border (Valparaíso, Jerez) and CDN remnants held mining districts; the homicide rate climbed to about 36 per 100,000 in 2018 (roughly 560 SESNSP investigations). The Cieneguillas prison riot of 31 December 2019 (16 dead) signaled the coming war."},{"from":2020,"to":2023,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","talibanes","cdn"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"A full CJNG-Sinaloa war for the corridors made Zacatecas Mexico's most violent state: INEGI counted 1,776 homicides in 2021 (109 per 100,000), with Fresnillo, Jerez, Valparaíso and the capital as epicenters, entire villages emptied around Jerez and Valparaíso, and police killed by the dozen. Ten bodies were hung from a Cuauhtémoc overpass (18 November 2021) and ten dumped outside the governor's palace (6 January 2022); Operativo Zacatecas II (November 2021) and the killing of General José Silvestre Urzúa (24 November 2022) framed a period that still produced 981 SESNSP homicide cases in 2022 and roughly 1,000 in 2023."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["mayiza","cjng","talibanes"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"The Mayiza with Los Cabrera and Grupo Flechas consolidated the north and center (Fresnillo, Sombrerete, Río Grande, Pánuco, Zacatecas-Guadalupe) while CJNG held the south (Jerez, Valparaíso, Villanueva, Pinos) with Los Metros' help and Los Talibanes kept the San Luis Potosí corridor; homicide victims collapsed from 500 in 2024 to 144 in 2025 (114 cases) and about 0.2 a day in 2026. A reported 'pax narca' broke on 16-18 July 2026 (ten men killed, five hung from a bridge on the Zacatecas-Saltillo highway at Pozo de Gamboa, Pánuco), CJNG declared war on the Mayiza on 24 August 2026, and a roughly 200 kg car bomb hit the Ojocaliente police station on 30 August 2026 as explosive attacks rose from 1 (2024) to 6 (2025) and 10 (2026 to early September); extortion and disappearances persist despite the low homicide count."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Zacatecas","lat":22.7709,"lng":-102.5833,"why":"State capital and Guadalupe conurbation on the Mexico City-Saltillo-Torreón axis; scene of the 2022 governor's-palace body dump.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Fresnillo","lat":23.175,"lng":-102.8675,"why":"Largest city and silver-mining hub at the crossroads to Durango and Torreón; the most fought-over plaza in the state.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Jerez","lat":22.6489,"lng":-102.9906,"why":"Gateway from the Jalisco highlands; CJNG's entry point and the site of mass rural displacement in 2021.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2019,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2020,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Valparaíso","lat":22.7717,"lng":-103.57,"why":"Sierra municipality on the Jalisco-Durango border; CJNG stronghold repeatedly patrolled by Mayiza's Grupo Flechas.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2019,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2020,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Ojocaliente","lat":22.5686,"lng":-102.25,"why":"Junction on the Zacatecas-San Luis Potosí-Aguascalientes corridor; contested Mayiza-CJNG ground and target of the 30 August 2026 car bomb.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2019,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2020,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Sombrerete","lat":23.6333,"lng":-103.64,"why":"Northwestern mining town on the road to Durango; anchor of the Mayiza-Cabrera northern zone.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]}],"facts":["INEGI recorded 1,776 homicides in Zacatecas in 2021, a rate of 109 per 100,000, the highest of any Mexican state and nearly four times the national 28.","SESNSP homicide investigations ran about 561 (2018), 510 (2019), 789 (2020), 1,134 (2021), 981 (2022), roughly 1,050 (2023), 369 (2024) and 114 (2025); victim counts fell from 500 in 2024 to 144 in 2025, a 71 percent drop that led the country.","On 16 May 2009 a Zetas commando disguised as federal police freed 53 inmates from the Cieneguillas prison; a riot there on 31 December 2019 killed 16.","Los Talibanes were founded in 2012 when Zetas finance chief Iván Velázquez Caballero 'El Talibán' (Z-50) broke with the Treviño Morales brothers after months of public banners; the group, long allied with the Gulf Cartel, holds the Zacatecas-San Luis Potosí corridor under Raúl Velázquez Caballero 'El Talibancito'.","Ten bodies were hung from an overpass in Cuauhtémoc on 18 November 2021 and ten more left in an SUV outside the state government palace in Zacatecas city on 6 January 2022; General José Silvestre Urzúa Padilla, head of the state's public security, was killed by gunmen during an operation in Pinos on 24 November 2022.","A car bomb with about 200 kg of industrial explosives detonated outside the Ojocaliente police headquarters on 30 August 2026, injuring at least 11 and damaging more than 60 homes (10 severely) and about 13-15 vehicles; authorities blamed CJNG, which had declared war on the Mayiza on 24 August 2026 after the July truce collapse.","Between 2021 and 2026 the daily homicide average fell from 4.5 to 0.2 (a 96 percent decline) per SESNSP figures presented by Marcela Figueroa in Guadalupe on 4 September 2026, even as extortion and disappearances kept rising."],"sources":["https://ljz.mx/27/07/2022/registro-zacatecas-la-mayor-tasa-de-homicidios-del-pais-durante-2021-inegi/","https://insightcrime.org/news/violence-zacatecas-mexico-surges-cjng-mayiza-clash/","https://politica.expansion.mx/estados/2026/09/02/zacatecas-disputa-cjng-cartel-sinaloa","https://lahoguera.mx/entre-2024-y-2025-zacatecas-y-chiapas-bajan-homicidios-pero-crecen-en-sinaloa-y-veracruz/","https://www.milenio.com/policia/narcotrafico/violencia-en-zacatecas-mapa-y-claves-disputa-entre-carteles","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Talibanes","https://smallwarsjournal.com/2026/09/07/mexican-cartel-tactical-note-55-car-bombing-against-the-comandancia-police-headquarters-in-ojocaliente-zacatecas/","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/estados/un-coche-bomba-11-heridos-y-una-investigacion-que-apunta-al-cjng-lo-que-se-sabe-del-ataque-en-ojocaliente-zacatecas/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/07/18/hallan-10-cuerpos-en-zacatecas-cinco-colgaban-de-un-puente/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/09/04/homicidios-en-zacatecas-disminuyen-96-en-cinco-anos-gobernador-agradece-a-las-nobles-instituciones-de-las-fuerzas-armadas/","https://ljz.mx/08/01/2026/zacatecas-encabeza-la-mayor-reduccion-de-homicidios-del-pais-bajan-71-1-entre-2024-y-2025/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mexican_states_by_homicides"],"verified":true},{"state":"NLE","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1996,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Monterrey served as a business, laundering and residential base for Gulf Cartel figures rather than a battleground; Juan García Ábrego was captured on a ranch outside Monterrey on 14 January 1996. Street violence was negligible."},{"from":1997,"to":2004,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["zetas","sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Osiel Cárdenas' Gulf Cartel and its Zetas enforcers (formed 1997-99) used the wealthy suburbs (San Pedro Garza García, Guadalupe) as a rear base, while Sinaloa Federation operators, including the Beltrán Leyva wing, arrived around 2003-2004 to stage their push on the Nuevo Laredo corridor."},{"from":2005,"to":2009,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["zetas","beltran_leyva","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"The Zetas ran the Monterrey plaza on the Gulf's behalf, planting cells across Apodaca, Escobedo, Santa Catarina and Guadalupe and capturing municipal police forces; skirmishes with Sinaloa cells, and from 2008 with the now-independent Beltrán Leyva Organization, led to the 2007 joint federal operation in Nuevo León-Tamaulipas. Homicide rates stayed near 6 per 100,000."},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["gulf","beltran_leyva"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"The Gulf-Zetas split made the Monterrey metro a war zone: the Zetas held most of the metro and the northern highway to Nuevo Laredo while the Gulf attacked from the Reynosa side (Cadereyta, Juárez, Guadalupe). Zetas burned the Casino Royale on 25 August 2011 (52 dead), killed 44 inmates in the Apodaca prison (19 February 2012) and dumped 49 dismembered bodies near Cadereyta (13 May 2012); INEGI recorded 2,003 homicides in 2011 (41.75 per 100,000) and about 1,459 in 2012, and the state created the Fuerza Civil police in 2011."},{"from":2013,"to":2016,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["gulf","cdn","beltran_leyva"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Arrests of Z-40 near Anáhuac (15 July 2013) and of Omar Treviño 'Z-42' in San Pedro Garza García (4 March 2015) plus Fuerza Civil deployments cut homicides to about 450 in 2015. Zetas remnants in the north (Anáhuac, Sabinas Hidalgo) became the Cártel del Noreste in 2015 while Gulf cells held the eastern municipalities."},{"from":2017,"to":2020,"primary":"cdn","secondary":["gulf","cjng","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"CDN pushed south along Highway 85 (Anáhuac, Sabinas Hidalgo, Salinas Victoria) into the metro area while Gulf Ciclones cells held Juárez and Cadereyta and CJNG and Sinaloa cells appeared. By 2019 twelve municipalities including Monterrey, Guadalupe, Santa Catarina, Cadereyta and Linares were disputed and homicide investigations rose from 282 (first half 2018) to 486 (first half 2019)."},{"from":2021,"to":2023,"primary":"cdn","secondary":["gulf","cjng","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"CDN fought the Gulf Cartel, Sinaloa-linked Los Rodos and CJNG cells for control of metro police forces and retail drug markets in San Pedro, Guadalupe, Apodaca, Escobedo and Santa Catarina. Homicides reached about 1,430 in 2022 (state prosecutor figures reported to SESNSP), the highest since 2012, with 17 police officers killed in 2022 and 16 in the first half of 2023; 842 homicides were logged from January to August 2023, about 1,400 by 27 December 2023, and bodies were dumped across seven metro municipalities."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"cdn","secondary":["gulf","cjng","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Homicides rose again to about 1,539 in 2024 (SESNSP preliminary), the most since 2012, before falling sharply in 2025. The state ran Operativo Muralla to slow CDN's advance toward the metro; a CJNG-CDN gunfight in Rayones on the night of 5 May 2025 reportedly left five gunmen dead (bodies removed by the gangs, more than 2,000 casings recovered) and state police killed two CDN gunmen in Los Aldama in late October 2025. Gulf faction boss 'La Kena' was arrested in Monterrey (January 2024) and veteran Gulf capo Zeferino Peña Cuéllar 'Don Zefe' died in a shootout with state investigators in Santiago on 23 December 2025 while allegedly setting up an armed cell; the US designated CDN and the Gulf Cartel as FTOs in February 2025."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Monterrey","lat":25.6866,"lng":-100.3161,"why":"Industrial capital and laundering hub; retail market and municipal police forces fought over by CDN, Gulf, Sinaloa and CJNG cells.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2016,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2017,"to":2026,"cartel":"cdn"}]},{"city":"Cadereyta Jiménez","lat":25.5883,"lng":-99.9915,"why":"Pemex refinery and Highway 40 gateway to Reynosa; site of the May 2012 dumping of 49 bodies and a Gulf Ciclones foothold.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"gulf"}]},{"city":"Anáhuac","lat":27.2408,"lng":-100.1327,"why":"Highway 85 corridor town between Monterrey and Nuevo Laredo where Z-40 was captured in July 2013; CDN's logistics link to its border stronghold.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"cdn"}]},{"city":"Apodaca","lat":25.7817,"lng":-100.1886,"why":"Airport and industrial suburb; February 2012 prison massacre and a long-running Zetas/CDN cell base.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2016,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2017,"to":2026,"cartel":"cdn"}]}],"facts":["INEGI recorded 2,003 homicides in Nuevo León in 2011 (41.75 per 100,000), about ten times the 2006 rate, and about 1,459 in 2012; homicides fell to roughly 450 in 2015 and climbed back to about 1,430 in 2022 and 1,539 in 2024 (SESNSP), the most since 2012.","On 25 August 2011 Zetas gunmen set fire to the Casino Royale in Monterrey, killing 52 people, after the owners resisted extortion.","On 19 February 2012, 44 inmates linked to the Gulf Cartel were killed by Zetas prisoners during a riot at the Apodaca prison, and around 30 Zetas escaped.","On 13 May 2012, 49 dismembered bodies were dumped on Highway 40 near Cadereyta Jiménez; the Zetas claimed the massacre in a banner.","Nuevo León created the Fuerza Civil state police in 2011 after purging municipal forces infiltrated by the Zetas; Zetas leader Miguel Treviño 'Z-40' was captured near Anáhuac on 15 July 2013 and his brother Omar 'Z-42' in San Pedro Garza García on 4 March 2015.","Police killings rose 143 percent between 2019 and 2022 (17 officers killed in 2022) as the Cártel del Noreste expanded south from Nuevo Laredo toward Monterrey.","Gulf Cartel veteran Zeferino Peña Cuéllar 'Don Zefe', a former Miguel Alemán police chief credited with helping recruit the first Zetas in the late 1990s, was killed in a shootout with state investigators in Santiago, Nuevo León on 23 December 2025; authorities said he was trafficking drugs and trying to form an armed cell in the state."],"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/news/is-northeast-cartel-expansion-driving-killings-of-police-in-nuevo-leon-mexico/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2023/10/01/por-que-la-violencia-en-monterrey-y-cual-es-el-interes-del-cartel-del-noreste-en-la-sultana-del-norte/","https://www.excelsior.com.mx/nacional/disputa-de-carteles-azota-a-nuevo-leon/1329889","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2025/10/28/choque-entre-cartel-del-noreste-y-policias-deja-dos-muertos-y-siete-detenidos/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2011_Monterrey_casino_attack","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mexican_states_by_homicides","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/estados/nuevo-leon-alcanza-mil-400-homicidios-dolosos-en-2023/","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/estados/enfrentamiento-en-rayones-nuevo-leon-balacera-entre-carteles-deja-cinco-muertos-y-vehiculos-incendiados/","https://www.milenio.com/politica/comunidad/fiscalia-nuevo-leon-descarta-muertos-enfrentamiento-rayones","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/12/23/abaten-en-nuevo-leon-a-zeferino-pena-alias-don-zefe-lider-de-fundador-de-los-zetas/","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anexo:Entidades_federativas_de_M%C3%A9xico_por_tasa_de_homicidio"],"verified":true},{"state":"TAM","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1995,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Juan N. Guerra's Matamoros smuggling clan became the cocaine-era Gulf Cartel under Juan García Ábrego, moving Cali cocaine through Matamoros and Reynosa with political and police protection. Nuevo Laredo was run by allied local bosses (Los Chachos, tied to the Juárez network). García Ábrego was captured on 14 January 1996."},{"from":1996,"to":2002,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["zetas","juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Succession fights followed García Ábrego's arrest; Osiel Cárdenas seized command by 1999 after killing partner Salvador Gómez and recruited GAFE army deserters as Los Zetas (1997-99), an armed wing still inside the Gulf Cartel, based in Nuevo Laredo and Matamoros. The Zetas wiped out Los Chachos in Nuevo Laredo by 2002."},{"from":2003,"to":2006,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["zetas","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Osiel Cárdenas was captured in Matamoros on 14 March 2003; the Sinaloa Federation, using the Beltrán Leyva brothers' wing and Édgar Valdez 'La Barbie' (then still part of the Federation), pushed into Nuevo Laredo in 2004-05 and was repelled by the Zetas. Police chief Alejandro Domínguez was killed hours after taking office on 8 June 2005, and Nuevo Laredo became one of Mexico's most violent cities, though the statewide homicide rate (about 11 per 100,000 in 2006) stayed far below the 2010-12 peak."},{"from":2007,"to":2009,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["zetas"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"After Cárdenas' extradition (19 January 2007) the Gulf-Zetas 'Compañía' ran the state jointly: Jorge Costilla and Antonio Cárdenas 'Tony Tormenta' in Matamoros-Reynosa, while Heriberto Lazcano and Miguel Treviño's Zetas ran Nuevo Laredo, Ciudad Victoria and the Tampico region with growing autonomy. Homicide rates (roughly 8-10 per 100,000) were the lowest of the period before the 2010 war."},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["gulf"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"The February 2010 split triggered open war: the Zetas held Nuevo Laredo, Ciudad Victoria, San Fernando and the Tampico area while the Gulf kept Reynosa-Matamoros, with the Frontera Chica (Mier, Camargo) as the front line. The Zetas killed 72 migrants at San Fernando (August 2010) and 193 bodies were exhumed there in April 2011; Ciudad Mier emptied in November 2010; 2012 was the state's worst homicide year on record (INEGI, about 1,550 deaths)."},{"from":2013,"to":2014,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["gulf"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"Lazcano was killed (October 2012) and Miguel Treviño 'Z-40' arrested on 15 July 2013 on a back road near Anáhuac, Nuevo León, outside Nuevo Laredo, but the Zetas kept Nuevo Laredo and Victoria. The Gulf fractured after Costilla's arrest (September 2012) into Metros, Rojos and Ciclones, turning Reynosa, Tampico and Matamoros violent and prompting the federal Tamaulipas operation of May 2014."},{"from":2015,"to":2016,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["cdn","zetas_vieja_escuela"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Omar Treviño 'Z-42' was captured in the Monterrey area in March 2015 and the Zetas split into the Cártel del Noreste (Treviño family, Nuevo Laredo) and Zetas Vieja Escuela (Ciudad Victoria and the south, later allied with the Gulf). The Gulf factions, holding Reynosa, Matamoros and Río Bravo, became the largest bloc by default while fighting each other; homicide rates dipped to about 15-17 per 100,000."},{"from":2017,"to":2020,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["cdn","zetas_vieja_escuela","cjng"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"Marines killed Metros boss Julián Loza 'El Toro' in Reynosa (April 2017), unleashing years of Metros infighting; Ciclones/Escorpiones held Matamoros; CDN fought Zetas Vieja Escuela in Ciudad Victoria and the Army in Nuevo Laredo. Homicides hit a second peak of about 1,437 in 2018 (INEGI); CJNG-linked cells appeared in the Tampico-Altamira zone."},{"from":2021,"to":2023,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["cdn","cjng"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Violence fell to 739 homicides in 2021 (lowest in a decade) even as 19 charred bodies, mostly Guatemalan migrants, were found at Camargo (January 2021) and Gulf faction gunmen killed 15 bystanders in Reynosa on 19 June 2021 (19 dead including four attackers). Ciclones leader 'El Contador' was arrested (February 2022), CDN's 'El Huevo' fell in Nuevo Laredo on 13 March 2022 amid a city-wide firefight, and the Escorpiones kidnapped four Americans in Matamoros on 3 March 2023, killing two. INEGI counted 680 homicides in 2023."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["cdn","cjng"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"Ciclones/Escorpiones chief 'La Kena' was arrested in Monterrey (19 January 2024); the Metros kidnapped and killed the five Grupo Fugitivo musicians in Reynosa (25 May 2025, nine arrested) and gunmen killed the FGR's state delegate in Reynosa in August 2025. CDN's 'El Ricky' was captured on 3 February 2025, triggering gunfights that shut Nuevo Laredo's airport; on 20 February 2025 the US designated the Gulf Cartel and CDN as terrorist organizations; the Tampico 'huachicol fiscal' fuel-smuggling scandal (tanker seized March 2025, Navy officers arrested September 2025) exposed port corruption. Recorded homicides fell to 331 in 2024 (SESNSP). CJNG cells staged roadblocks in the state after El Mencho's death (22 February 2026), the US sanctioned two Tamaulipas casinos tied to CDN (14 April 2026) and 57 PepsiCo delivery vehicles were torched in Matamoros on 27 July 2026."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Nuevo Laredo","lat":27.4763,"lng":-99.5164,"why":"Busiest land port on the US-Mexico border (World Trade Bridge); Zetas birthplace and CDN stronghold.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"cdn"}]},{"city":"Reynosa","lat":26.0922,"lng":-98.2778,"why":"Largest drug and migrant crossing opposite McAllen; Gulf Cartel's Metros faction base.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2026,"cartel":"gulf"}]},{"city":"Matamoros","lat":25.869,"lng":-97.5027,"why":"Historic Gulf Cartel headquarters opposite Brownsville; Ciclones/Escorpiones base.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2026,"cartel":"gulf"}]},{"city":"Tampico","lat":22.2331,"lng":-97.8611,"why":"Gulf port and oil hub (with Altamira); extortion, fuel smuggling and the 2025 huachicol fiscal tanker seizure.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2026,"cartel":"gulf"}]},{"city":"Ciudad Victoria","lat":23.7369,"lng":-99.1411,"why":"State capital and Highway 85/101 junction; Zetas rear base later fought over by Zetas Vieja Escuela and CDN.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2015,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2016,"to":2019,"cartel":"zetas_vieja_escuela"},{"from":2020,"to":2026,"cartel":"cdn"}]}],"facts":["Homicides peaked in 2012 during the Gulf-Zetas war, the state's worst year on record (INEGI, about 1,550 deaths); a second spike reached about 1,437 in 2018 (INEGI) before falling to 739 in 2021, the lowest in a decade, 680 in 2023 (INEGI) and 331 in 2024 (SESNSP).","Los Zetas massacred 72 migrants (58 men, 14 women) at San Fernando in August 2010; in April 2011, 193 bodies were exhumed from clandestine graves in the same municipality.","Osiel Cárdenas was captured in Matamoros on 14 March 2003 and extradited to the US on 19 January 2007; his GAFE-deserter bodyguard unit, Los Zetas, broke with the Gulf Cartel in February 2010 and Ciudad Mier was abandoned by most residents in November 2010.","In January 2021, 19 charred bodies, most of them Guatemalan migrants, were found at Camargo and 12 state police officers were charged; on 19 June 2021 Gulf Cartel gunmen killed 15 bystanders in Reynosa (19 dead in total including four attackers).","On 3 March 2023 the Escorpiones faction kidnapped four US citizens in Matamoros, killing two; their boss José Alberto García Vilano 'La Kena' was arrested in Monterrey on 19 January 2024 and sent to the US on 27 February 2025 with 28 other capos.","The US designated the Gulf Cartel and the Cártel del Noreste as Foreign Terrorist Organizations on 20 February 2025; the 3 February 2025 arrest of CDN chief Ricardo González Sauceda 'El Ricky' set off gunfights that closed Nuevo Laredo's airport.","In March 2025 the Navy seized the tanker Challenge Procyon in Tampico with about 10 million litres of diesel declared as additives, exposing the 'huachicol fiscal' network; 14 people were arrested in September 2025 and Vice Admiral Manuel Roberto Farías Laguna and his brother Rear Admiral Fernando Farías Laguna, nephews of the former Navy secretary, were charged.","On 25 May 2025 the five members of Grupo Fugitivo were abducted in Reynosa and killed; prosecutors arrested nine members of the Gulf Cartel's Metros faction and named its leader 'M-40' as the mastermind."],"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/news/matamoros-symptom-organized-crime-mexico-tamaulipas/","https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/02/20/2025-02873/foreign-terrorist-organization-designations-of-tren-de-aragua-mara-salvatrucha-cartel-de-sinaloa","https://www.odni.gov/wp-content/uploads/dlm_uploads/CT-Guide-Gulf-Cartel_508.pdf","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0146","https://smallwarsjournal.com/2026/07/16/hybrid-violence-in-mexico-cartel-attacks-tactical-evolution-and-the-impact-of-2025-terrorist-designations/","https://elgraficotam.com.mx/2022/07/27/en-2021-tamaulipas-registro-el-menor-numero-de-homicidios-de-los-ultimos-10-anos/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2025/05/29/asesinato-de-integrantes-de-grupo-fugitivo-detienen-a-9-personas-del-cartel-del-golfo/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huachicol_fiscal","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_in_Mexico","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anexo:Entidades_federativas_de_M%C3%A9xico_por_tasa_de_homicidio"],"verified":true},{"state":"SLP","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1996,"primary":"gulf","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"A transit state on Highway 57 (Mexico City-Nuevo Laredo) and Highway 85 through the Huasteca, inside Juan García Ábrego's Gulf Cartel sphere; no home-grown cartel and little violence."},{"from":1997,"to":2006,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["zetas"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Osiel Cárdenas' Gulf Cartel and its Zetas enforcers policed the corridor from the capital, Matehuala and Ciudad Valles; the 2004-2006 Nuevo Laredo war barely touched the state."},{"from":2007,"to":2009,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["zetas"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"From 2007 the Gulf Cartel's Zetas wing, still formally part of the Gulf organization, set up a regional command in the capital and Ciudad Valles, adding extortion, kidnapping and retail drug sales to trafficking; several organizations began establishing cells that year and the homicide rate rose to about 8.5 per 100,000 in 2008."},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["gulf"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"After the February 2010 split the Gulf-Zetas war spilled into the Huasteca (Ciudad Valles, Tamuín) and the capital; Zetas plaza chief Alfredo Alemán Narváez 'El Alemán' was arrested in Fresnillo, Zacatecas in November 2011 (and later freed) and Matehuala's mayor-elect Edgar Morales was assassinated on 12 August 2012. Homicide rates peaked near 15-16 per 100,000 in 2010-2012."},{"from":2014,"to":2016,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["cdn","zetas","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"As the Zetas fragmented, Gulf cells (Operativa Espartano, Grupo Squalo) took the Huasteca and Zona Media while Zetas remnants and, from 2015, the Cártel del Noreste held the Highway 57/Altiplano corridor around Matehuala. Homicides bottomed out at 266 in 2015 (INEGI)."},{"from":2017,"to":2023,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["gulf","cdn","talibanes"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"CJNG arrived from Guanajuato and Querétaro in 2017 (homicide rate back to about 16.7 per 100,000) and with its local franchise Cártel San Luis Potosí Nueva Generación pushed into the capital, Soledad de Graciano Sánchez and the Huasteca against Gulf-aligned cells and Zetas remnants, with Los Talibanes on the Zacatecas border; no single group dominated. INEGI homicides hit records of 803 (2020), 797 (2021) and 759 (2022), a 2022 Sedena report leaked by Guacamaya mapped six cartels, and Los Alemanes (CDN allies) kidnapped more than 100 migrants on Highway 57 in April 2023."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["gulf","cdn","talibanes"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"Homicides fell to 511 in 2024 and 369 in 2025 (INEGI), the lowest since 2015, though CDN/Los Alemanes and the Gulf Cartel traded interrogation videos and killings in Charcas, Huehuetlán and the Huasteca in September 2025. Seven CFE subcontractor electricians abducted on Highway 57 near Matehuala on 21 March 2026 were found alive on 23 March and Matehuala's municipal police director was arrested over the kidnapping; the DEA's 2025 assessment lists CJNG and Sinaloa activity in the state."}],"plazas":[{"city":"San Luis Potosí","lat":22.1565,"lng":-100.9855,"why":"State capital and Highway 57 logistics hub; largest retail market, contested by CJNG, Gulf, CDN and Sinaloa cells.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2017,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2018,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Ciudad Valles","lat":21.9931,"lng":-99.013,"why":"Capital of the Huasteca on Highway 85 toward Tampico; Gulf Cartel and Talibanes base disputed by CJNG.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2026,"cartel":"gulf"}]},{"city":"Matehuala","lat":23.6434,"lng":-100.6445,"why":"Altiplano crossroads on Highway 57 toward Nuevo Laredo; migrant kidnappings and CDN/Los Alemanes corridor.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"cdn"}]},{"city":"Tamazunchale","lat":21.26,"lng":-98.79,"why":"Southern Huasteca gateway to Hidalgo and Veracruz on Highway 85; CJNG entry point into the region.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2018,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2019,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]}],"facts":["INEGI homicides peaked at 803 in 2020, 797 in 2021 and 759 in 2022, then fell to about 560 (2023), 511 (2024) and 369 (2025), the lowest since 2015, when 266 were recorded.","INEGI's preliminary 2025 count (369) was almost double the 185 homicide victims reported by the SESNSP for the same year, a gap of 99 percent.","San Luis Potosí borders nine states and is crossed by Highway 57 (Mexico City-Nuevo Laredo) and Highway 85 (Huasteca to Tamaulipas), making it a corridor for drugs, fuel theft and migrant smuggling rather than a production zone.","Matehuala's PRI mayor-elect Edgar Morales Pérez and his campaign manager were assassinated on 12 August 2012; Zetas plaza chief Alfredo Alemán Narváez 'El Alemán' was arrested in Fresnillo in November 2011, later regained his freedom, and his Los Alemanes cell, now allied with CDN, kidnapped more than 100 migrants on Highway 57 in April 2023.","A Sedena report leaked in 2022 mapped six criminal organizations in the state (Zetas remnants, Gulf Cartel cells Espartano and Squalo, CJNG, Cártel San Luis Potosí Nueva Generación, Cártel Unido de la Huasteca and La Familia Michoacana); homicides rose 60 percent (753 vs 420) in Governor Gallardo's first 17 months.","In September 2025 two videos circulated: one of a woman from Charcas, described as a Gulf Cartel informant, interrogated by gunmen, and one (21 September) of two alleged Los Alemanes lookouts interrogated by presumed Gulf Cartel members, evidence of a CDN-Gulf dispute over the capital's outskirts, the Huasteca and Matehuala.","Seven electrical workers who vanished on Highway 57 near Matehuala on 21 March 2026 were found alive on 23 March; Matehuala's municipal police director was detained over the kidnapping and the DEA's 2025 National Drug Threat Assessment records CJNG and Sinaloa Cartel activity in the state."],"sources":["https://laorquesta.mx/slp-cerro-2025-con-369-homicidios-el-menor-registro-en-diez-anos/","https://pulsoslp.com.mx/slp/inegi-homicidios-san-luis-potosi-2025-casi-duplica-snsp/2072963","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2023/04/08/san-luis-potosi-dominado-por-6-carteles-cuales-son-y-en-que-zonas-operan/","https://www.milenio.com/policia/carteles-en-san-luis-potosi-cuales-son-y-por-que-se-establecieron-ahi","https://sanluispotosidigital.com/2025/09/24/el-cartel-del-noreste-vs-cartel-del-golfo-en-la-disputa-por-san-luis-potosi/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/03/24/cuales-son-los-grupos-criminales-que-operan-en-san-luis-potosi-donde-desaparecio-un-grupo-de-electricistas/","https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2026/03/23/estados/localizan-con-vida-a-trabajadores-subcontratados-por-la-cfe-en-matehuala-slp","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/estados/2026/3/25/detienen-al-jefe-de-seguridad-de-matehuala-lo-investigan-por-el-secuestro-de-siete-electricistas-370959.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mexican_states_by_homicides"],"verified":true},{"state":"VER","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1995,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The Gulf coast was a maritime and air corridor for Colombian cocaine handled by García Ábrego's Gulf Cartel, with Juárez (Carrillo Fuentes) networks also using Veracruz ports and airstrips; violence was rare and politically contained."},{"from":1996,"to":2003,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["juarez","zetas"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Juárez-linked Albino Quintero Meraz ran the Veracruz-Boca del Río plaza until his arrest in May 2002, while Osiel Cárdenas' Gulf Cartel and its Zetas wing extended south to Coatzacoalcos and into the Tuxpan-Poza Rica oil belt."},{"from":2004,"to":2007,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["zetas","sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Under Governor Fidel Herrera (2004-2010) the Zetas took over day-to-day control of the Gulf plaza, extorting Pemex contractors and businesses around Coatzacoalcos, Poza Rica and Córdoba, with occasional Sinaloa probes."},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["zetas","sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"The Zetas, still the Gulf Cartel's armed wing, ran Veracruz as their most important rear base outside Tamaulipas, with the lowest homicide rates of the period (about 4.5 per 100,000 in 2009) masking entrenched extortion and kidnapping under Herrera's government."},{"from":2010,"to":2011,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["gulf","cjng","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"After the February 2010 split the now-independent Zetas dominated the state; the war with the Gulf hit northern Veracruz (Pánuco, Tuxpan), and on 20 September 2011 the CJNG 'Matazetas' dumped 35 bodies in Boca del Río, prompting Operación Veracruz Seguro in October 2011. Javier Duarte took office on 1 December 2010."},{"from":2012,"to":2015,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["cjng","gulf"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Duarte-era collusion let the Zetas run extortion and kidnapping with state police, burying victims at sites such as Colinas de Santa Fe while at least a dozen journalists were killed. CJNG steadily took Córdoba-Orizaba and the south; on 11 January 2016 state police handed five youths from Tierra Blanca to CJNG killers."},{"from":2016,"to":2019,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["zetas_vieja_escuela","gulf","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"The Zetas splintered into Zetas Vieja Escuela and Sangre Nueva Zeta while CJNG became dominant in the centre and south; the Gulf-linked Grupo Sombra emerged in Tuxpan and Poza Rica in 2017. Duarte fled (October 2016) and was arrested in Guatemala on 15 April 2017; the state logged a record 1,722 homicides in 2017 (state figures reported to SESNSP), 13 died at a Minatitlán party on 19 April 2019 and around 30 in the Caballo Blanco bar arson in Coatzacoalcos on 27 August 2019."},{"from":2020,"to":2023,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["gulf","zetas_vieja_escuela"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Homicides fell to 854 in 2022 (state figures) and 666 in 2023 (INEGI) as CJNG consolidated the Veracruz-Boca del Río, Córdoba-Orizaba and Coatzacoalcos-Minatitlán corridors against Zetas Vieja Escuela remnants, while Grupo Sombra (Gulf) held Tuxpan, Pánuco, Álamo and Tantoyuca and fuel theft flourished on the Tierra Blanca-Amatlán pipelines."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["gulf","sinaloa","zetas_vieja_escuela"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"The Grupo Sombra-CJNG war escalated from January 2024 across Poza Rica, Papantla, Tuxpan and Tantoyuca, with Sinaloa's Gente Nueva cells also present; recorded homicides rose to 772 in 2024 (SESNSP). Retired teacher Irma Hernández was kidnapped in Álamo and killed in July 2025, three dismembered bodies with a Grupo Sombra message were left on a highway near Poza Rica in early August 2025 and a Tuxpan prison riot against Grupo Sombra extortion killed seven inmates on 2 August 2025. Journalist Carlos Castro was shot dead in Poza Rica on 8 January 2026, journalist Roxana Guzmán was abducted in Nanchital on 2 June 2026 and her remains identified in Moloacán on 3 July 2026, with Grupo Sombra operatives and four Ixhuatlán del Sureste police arrested; Rocío Nahle has governed since 1 December 2024."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Veracruz","lat":19.1738,"lng":-96.1342,"why":"Mexico's main Gulf container port for cocaine and precursor chemicals; site of the 2011 Boca del Río body dump and the Colinas de Santa Fe graves.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Coatzacoalcos","lat":18.1345,"lng":-94.4589,"why":"Southern petrochemical port and Isthmus gateway; extortion economy and the 2019 Caballo Blanco bar massacre.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Poza Rica","lat":20.5328,"lng":-97.4593,"why":"Northern oil city; birthplace of Grupo Sombra and front line of its war with CJNG since 2024.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2016,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2017,"to":2026,"cartel":"gulf"}]},{"city":"Tuxpan","lat":20.9568,"lng":-97.4013,"why":"Northern port and Grupo Sombra stronghold; the 2 August 2025 prison riot left seven dead.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2016,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2017,"to":2026,"cartel":"gulf"}]},{"city":"Córdoba","lat":18.885,"lng":-96.9275,"why":"Córdoba-Orizaba industrial corridor linking the port to Puebla; early CJNG conquest and fuel-theft zone.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]}],"facts":["Veracruz recorded a record 1,722 intentional homicides in 2017 (state figures reported to SESNSP), the year after Duarte fled, and 854 in 2022; the homicide rate peaked at about 20 per 100,000 in 2017 and INEGI counted 666 homicides in 2023.","On 20 September 2011, 35 bodies were dumped from two trucks in Boca del Río by the CJNG-linked 'Matazetas', opening the CJNG-Zetas war for the state.","Mothers of the Colectivo Solecito located the Colinas de Santa Fe burial site outside Veracruz port in August 2016; more than 250 skulls had been recovered by March 2017 and close to 300 by 2018, making it Mexico's largest clandestine grave.","Governor Javier Duarte (1 December 2010 to 12 October 2016) fled and was arrested in Panajachel, Guatemala on 15 April 2017; at least twelve journalists were killed and three disappeared during his term by 2015.","On 19 April 2019, 13 people including a one-year-old were killed at a party in Minatitlán, and on 27 August 2019 the Caballo Blanco bar in Coatzacoalcos was set ablaze, killing around 30, both amid CJNG-Zetas fighting.","Grupo Sombra (also 'Mafia Veracruzana'), a Gulf Cartel arm that began operating in Tuxpan and Poza Rica in 2017, has fought CJNG openly since January 2024 across northern Veracruz; a Tuxpan prison riot against its extortion killed seven inmates on 2 August 2025 and journalist Roxana Guzmán was murdered after her abduction on 2 June 2026, with Grupo Sombra operatives and municipal police arrested.","The ports of Veracruz, Coatzacoalcos and Tuxpan handle cocaine and precursor chemicals, and the state is among Mexico's leading zones for pipeline fuel theft."],"sources":["https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/07/04/grupo-sombra-el-cartel-que-domina-veracruz-tras-el-asesinato-de-roxana-guzman/","https://politica.expansion.mx/estados/2025/08/06/grupo-sombra-el-brazo-del-cartel-del-golfo-que-siembra-terror-en-veracruz","https://elciudadano.mx/nacional/estos-son-los-tres-carteles-que-campean-en-veracruz-en-agosto-de-2025/","https://www.veracruz.gob.mx/2023/07/05/de-2017-a-2022-cerca-de-mil-homicidios-dolosos-menos-en-veracruz/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Javier_Duarte_de_Ochoa","https://insightcrime.org/news/2017-deadliest-year-organized-crime-related-homicides-mexico/","https://cpj.org/2026/07/remains-of-abducted-mexican-journalist-roxana-guzman-identified-in-veracruz/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2025/08/03/motin-en-el-penal-de-tuxpan-veracruz-asesinato-maestra-irma-hernandez/","https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2025/08/03/estados/confirma-ssp-de-veracruz-siete-muertos-por-motin-en-penal-de-tuxpan","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_in_Mexico","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anexo:Entidades_federativas_de_M%C3%A9xico_por_tasa_de_homicidio"],"verified":true},{"state":"JAL","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1984,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Guadalajara was the headquarters of the Guadalajara Cartel (Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo, Rafael Caro Quintero, Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo), which centralized Colombian cocaine transshipment and Mexican marijuana under DFS protection. The metro area served as a safe haven and business center rather than a battlefield."},{"from":1985,"to":1989,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":["sinaloa","tijuana"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"The kidnapping and murder of DEA agent Enrique Camarena in Guadalajara in February 1985 triggered Operation Leyenda; Caro Quintero and Fonseca Carrillo were arrested in April 1985 and Félix Gallardo in Guadalajara on 8 April 1989. The subsequent division of plazas turned the Guadalajara metro into shared ground for the emerging Sinaloa (Guzmán, Zambada, Coronel) and Tijuana (Arellano Félix) networks."},{"from":1990,"to":1999,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["tijuana","milenio","colima"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"Guadalajara became a base for several successor organizations at once: the Sinaloa network of El Chapo and Nacho Coronel, the Arellano Félix organization, the Valencia family's Milenio Cartel and the Amezcua brothers' Colima meth cartel (arrested in Guadalajara in June 1998). The 24 May 1993 killing of Cardinal Posadas Ocampo at Guadalajara airport during an Arellano Félix attempt on El Chapo epitomized the Sinaloa-Tijuana feud."},{"from":2000,"to":2009,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["milenio","beltran_leyva"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Ignacio 'Nacho' Coronel ran Jalisco for the Sinaloa Federation and built a methamphetamine empire ('King of Crystal'), with the Milenio Cartel (Valencias, Nava Valencias) as junior partner supplying Michoacán and the Guadalajara metro. Homicide rates stayed at 6-8 per 100,000, but the capture of Óscar Nava Valencia 'El Lobo' in Tlajomulco on 28 October 2009 began the unraveling."},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["sinaloa","milenio","zetas"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Nacho Coronel was killed by the Army in Zapopan on 29 July 2010 and the Milenio Cartel split into La Resistencia and Los Torcidos, the latter becoming the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación under Nemesio Oseguera 'El Mencho' and Érick Valencia 'El 85', initially as a Sinaloa affiliate. Los Zetas backed La Resistencia; in November 2011, 26 bodies were dumped at the Arcos del Milenio in Guadalajara in retaliation for the CJNG's 'Mata Zetas' massacres in Veracruz."},{"from":2013,"to":2016,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":3,"note":"CJNG broke from Sinaloa and turned Jalisco into its command and laundering center. In 2015 it ambushed Gendarmerie in Ocotlán (19 March), killed 15 state police in Soyatlán (6 April) and on 1 May shot down an Air Force helicopter in Villa Purificación while setting 39 blockades across 25 municipalities; in August 2016 it kidnapped El Chapo's son from a Puerto Vallarta restaurant."},{"from":2017,"to":2021,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["other","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"The Sinaloa-backed Cártel Nueva Plaza (Carlos Enrique Sánchez 'El Cholo', an ex-CJNG lieutenant) fought CJNG across the Guadalajara metro (Tlaquepaque, Tonalá, Zapopan), pushing Jalisco to record homicide rates of about 24-25 per 100,000 in 2018-2019 and a wave of disappearances and clandestine graves; a refrigerated trailer holding 273 unclaimed bodies surfaced in September 2018. Former governor Aristóteles Sandoval was assassinated in Puerto Vallarta on 18 December 2020 and El Cholo was killed in December 2021."},{"from":2022,"to":2025,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":3,"note":"CJNG held uncontested dominance while homicides fell (1,196 victims in 2025, 13.4 per 100,000, down 33.5%), but Jalisco led the national disappeared registry (15,036 people as of March 2025) and Los Altos became a hotspot: five youths were abducted and killed in Lagos de Moreno in August 2023. Rancho Izaguirre in Teuchitlán, raided in September 2024 and exposed by the Guerreros Buscadores collective on 5 March 2025, revealed a CJNG forced-recruitment and killing camp."},{"from":2026,"to":2026,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":4,"note":"El Mencho was killed on 22 February 2026 in a military operation in Tapalpa; CJNG answered with about 250 blockades in 20 states, a car bomb in San Juan de los Lagos, a prison break in Puerto Vallarta and the deaths of 25 National Guard members nationwide. His stepson Juan Carlos Valencia González 'El 03' emerged as leader by March, and rival commander Audias Flores 'El Jardinero' was arrested on 27 April 2026, leaving CJNG dominant in Jalisco but with an unresolved internal succession."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Guadalajara","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"why":"Mexico's second city: cartel headquarters, financial and laundering center since the Guadalajara Cartel era","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Puerto Vallarta","lat":20.6534,"lng":-105.2253,"why":"Pacific resort used for cocaine landings, tourism-sector laundering and timeshare fraud; site of high-profile kidnappings and killings","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Lagos de Moreno","lat":21.3564,"lng":-101.9297,"why":"Los Altos crossroads on the routes to Guanajuato, Aguascalientes and San Luis Potosí; epicenter of disappearances and clandestine graves","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Ocotlán","lat":20.3553,"lng":-102.7736,"why":"Ciénega corridor linking Guadalajara with Michoacán; Valencia-family turf and site of the March 2015 CJNG ambush on the Gendarmerie","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2009,"cartel":"milenio"},{"from":2010,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Tapalpa","lat":19.945,"lng":-103.7597,"why":"Sierra hideout south of Guadalajara where El Mencho was killed on 22 February 2026","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]}],"facts":["DEA agent Enrique 'Kiki' Camarena was kidnapped in Guadalajara on 7 February 1985 and murdered; Guadalajara Cartel boss Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo was arrested in Guadalajara on 8 April 1989.","Cardinal Juan Jesús Posadas Ocampo was shot dead at Guadalajara airport on 24 May 1993 during an Arellano Félix attack aimed at Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán.","Ignacio 'Nacho' Coronel, the Sinaloa Cartel's Jalisco chief and methamphetamine kingpin, was killed by the Army in Zapopan on 29 July 2010; the CJNG emerged from the Milenio Cartel's Los Torcidos faction in 2009-2010.","On 1 May 2015 CJNG gunmen shot down a Mexican Air Force helicopter with an RPG in Villa Purificación, killing nine security personnel, and set 39 blockades in 25 municipalities during Operación Jalisco.","Jalisco ranks first in Mexico for disappeared persons: 15,036 people were registered missing on the RNPDNO as of 18 March 2025, 6,727 of them disappeared during the 2018-2024 Alfaro administration; 2,633 new reports were filed in 2025.","Rancho Izaguirre in Teuchitlán, exposed on 5 March 2025 after a September 2024 raid, held human remains and more than 400 items of clothing from CJNG forced-recruitment victims; in 2025 ten defendants were convicted of murder.","CJNG leader Nemesio Oseguera 'El Mencho' was killed in Tapalpa on 22 February 2026; the reprisal included about 250 blockades in 20 states and 25 National Guard deaths. Jalisco recorded 1,196 homicide victims in 2025 (13.4 per 100,000), down 33.5% from 2024."],"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/news/whats-next-for-mexicos-cjng-after-the-killing-of-el-mencho/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Jalisco_operation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1_May_2015_Jalisco_attacks","https://www.milenio.com/policia/jalisco-lidera-registro-nacional-de-personas-desaparecidas-en-2025","https://insightcrime.org/news/killing-camp-in-mexico-shows-horrors-of-cjng-forced-recruitment/","https://www.latinometrics.com/homicides/mexico"],"verified":false},{"state":"NAY","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Nayarit's Sierra Madre (El Nayar, Huajicori, La Yesca) grew marijuana and opium poppy for the Guadalajara Cartel network, and the San Blas coast served as a landing zone; the state was a quiet production and transit backwater."},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["beltran_leyva"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"After the 1989 plaza division Nayarit fell to Sinaloa traffickers, with the Beltrán Leyva brothers managing the state and its Pacific corridor as part of the Sinaloa Federation. Homicide rates hovered around 9-10 per 100,000 in 2006-2007."},{"from":2008,"to":2011,"primary":"beltran_leyva","secondary":["sinaloa","zetas"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"The January 2008 Beltrán Leyva-Sinaloa rupture turned Tepic and Xalisco into a war zone between the BLO (allied with Los Zetas) and El Chapo's and El Mayo's gunmen; homicides climbed from a rate of about 14 in 2009 to 33 in 2010 and 40 per 100,000 in 2011 (465 killings), among the highest in Mexico. The BLO faction of Juan Francisco Patrón Sánchez 'H2', loyal to Héctor Beltrán Leyva, prevailed."},{"from":2012,"to":2016,"primary":"beltran_leyva","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Under Governor Roberto Sandoval (2011-2017) and prosecutor Édgar Veytia (Tepic security chief 2008-2011, state attorney general 2013-2017), H2's organization enjoyed official protection and a 'pax narca': recorded homicides fell from 465 in 2011 to 36 in 2016, while rivals were disappeared or jailed by the state apparatus itself."},{"from":2017,"to":2018,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["beltran_leyva","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Marines killed H2 and seven others in Tepic on the night of 9-10 February 2017 and his nephew 'H9' the next day; Veytia was arrested in San Diego on 27 March 2017. The CJNG moved in from Jalisco to absorb the BLO structure and its officials' networks, and homicide rates rebounded to 20 per 100,000 in 2017 and 25 in 2018."},{"from":2019,"to":2023,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"CJNG consolidated Tepic, the Bahía de Banderas resort strip (Nuevo Vallarta, Bucerías) and the coastal highway, using the Puerto Vallarta-Nayarit tourism economy for laundering and timeshare fraud, while the Sinaloa Cartel kept the northern border municipalities (Huajicori, Acaponeta, Tecuala). Homicide rates fell to 12-13 per 100,000."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["mayiza","chapitos"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"The Sinaloa Cartel's September 2024 split spilled into the northern sierra: La Mayiza and the CJNG (aligned with Los Chapitos) fight over the Huajicori corridor linking Sinaloa with Zacatecas, displacing thousands of Naayeri, O'dam and mestizo villagers (57 families in October 2024; over 12,000 residents from 170 communities by local counts), and in September 2025 the governor denounced groups testing explosive devices on Indigenous children. Statewide homicides remained moderate (193 in 2025, 14.6 per 100,000, up 6%), and CJNG blockades hit the state after El Mencho's death in February 2026."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Tepic","lat":21.5041,"lng":-104.8942,"why":"State capital and BLO/H2 command center; site of the February 2017 Navy operation that ended Beltrán Leyva rule","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2016,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2017,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Nuevo Vallarta (Bahía de Banderas)","lat":20.6989,"lng":-105.2933,"why":"Resort strip adjoining Puerto Vallarta used for laundering, timeshare fraud and cocaine logistics","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2016,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2017,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"San Blas","lat":21.5403,"lng":-105.2855,"why":"Historic Pacific port and estuary coast used for maritime cocaine landings","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2016,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2017,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Huajicori","lat":22.6414,"lng":-105.3133,"why":"Sierra municipality on the Sinaloa-Durango border: poppy zone and corridor to Zacatecas contested by La Mayiza and CJNG since 2024","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2016,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2017,"to":2023,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"cartel":"mayiza"}]}],"facts":["Nayarit's homicide count peaked at 465 in 2011 (about 40 per 100,000) during the Beltrán Leyva-Zetas war against the Sinaloa Cartel, then fell to 36 in 2016 under prosecutor Édgar Veytia's protection racket.","Beltrán Leyva leader Juan Francisco Patrón Sánchez 'H2' was killed by Navy Marines, including helicopter fire, in Tepic on the night of 9-10 February 2017 along with seven others; his nephew Daniel Isaac Silva Gárate 'H9' was killed in Tepic the following day.","Édgar Veytia, Nayarit attorney general 2013-2017, was arrested at the San Diego border on 27 March 2017, pleaded guilty in New York in January 2019 and was sentenced to 20 years on 26 September 2019; a US court found he amassed about US$250 million. Former governor Roberto Sandoval was arrested in Nuevo León in June 2021.","The Sierra del Nayar (Huajicori, El Nayar, La Yesca) is one of Mexico's main opium poppy zones; since 2024 fighting between La Mayiza and the CJNG has displaced Naayeri and O'dam communities, and on 5 September 2025 Governor Miguel Ángel Navarro denounced criminal groups testing explosives on Indigenous children.","Nayarit recorded 193 homicide victims in 2025 (14.6 per 100,000, up 6% from 2024), below the national rate of 17.7.","The U.S. Treasury has sanctioned CJNG-linked timeshare-fraud and money-laundering networks operating in the Puerto Vallarta-Bahía de Banderas resort corridor in successive actions between 2023 and 2025."],"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/news/brief/death-blo-leader-illustrates-marines-decisive-role-underworld-fight/","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/estados/quien-es-edgar-veytia-el-diablo-que-protegia-al-cartel-el-h2/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edgar_Veytia","https://acleddata.com/report/how-sinaloa-cartel-rift-redrawing-mexicos-criminal-map","https://icmagazine.org/flee-or-die-the-naayeri-peoples-impossible-choice/","https://www.latinometrics.com/homicides/mexico"],"verified":false},{"state":"COL","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1987,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Manzanillo's port and Colima's coastal highway were part of the Guadalajara Cartel's Pacific logistics, but the small state saw little violence."},{"from":1988,"to":1998,"primary":"colima","secondary":["sinaloa","milenio"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The Amezcua Contreras brothers (Jesús, Luis, Adán) founded the Colima Cartel in 1988, importing ephedrine from India and Thailand through Manzanillo and becoming the hemisphere's leading methamphetamine producers. Adán was arrested in November 1997 and Luis and Jesús in Guadalajara on 1 June 1998."},{"from":1999,"to":2009,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["colima","milenio"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The Amezcua network was absorbed into the Sinaloa Federation; Nacho Coronel and the Valencias' Milenio Cartel managed Manzanillo for cocaine and precursor imports. Homicide rates stayed at 5-8 per 100,000."},{"from":2010,"to":2015,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["sinaloa","zetas"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Colima became a core territory of the newborn CJNG, still allied with Sinaloa, while Zetas-backed La Resistencia cells fought for Manzanillo and Tecomán; the homicide rate jumped to 24.5 in 2011 and 41.9 per 100,000 in 2012 before easing to 14 in 2014 and 23 in 2015."},{"from":2016,"to":2021,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"Open war between CJNG and the Sinaloa Cartel for Manzanillo, Mexico's busiest container port and gateway for Asian precursor chemicals, drove the homicide rate from 23 (2015) to 71 (2016), 97 (2017), 81 (2018) and 87 per 100,000 (2019), the highest in Mexico. CJNG emerged dominant in the port, Colima city and Tecomán."},{"from":2022,"to":2025,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["mezcales","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"Los Mezcales, a CJNG cell based in Colima city's Mezcalito neighborhood led by José Bernabé Brizuela 'La Vaca', broke away after the 25 January 2022 Cereso prison riot (9 dead) and declared war on CJNG in Colima, Villa de Álvarez, Tecomán and Manzanillo. Colima city was ranked the world's most violent city in 2022, and the state posted Mexico's highest rate for three straight years (690 homicides, 101 per 100,000 in 2024; 622 and 81 per 100,000 in 2025); La Vaca was arrested in August 2022 and the Navy detained 54 Mezcales in November 2025."},{"from":2026,"to":2026,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["mezcales","mayiza"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"In January 2026 banners in Villa de Álvarez, Coquimatlán, Tecomán and Manzanillo announced that La Mayiza ('Mayito Flaco') was backing Los Mezcales against CJNG; the state was hit by blockades after El Mencho's death in February and still had Mexico's highest homicide rate by mid-2026."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Manzanillo","lat":19.0522,"lng":-104.3158,"why":"Mexico's busiest container port: entry point for cocaine and Asian precursor chemicals for methamphetamine and fentanyl","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1987,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1988,"to":1998,"cartel":"colima"},{"from":1999,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Colima","lat":19.2433,"lng":-103.725,"why":"State capital; Mezcalito neighborhood is the Los Mezcales stronghold and the state prison a contested asset","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1987,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1988,"to":1998,"cartel":"colima"},{"from":1999,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Tecomán","lat":18.9089,"lng":-103.8747,"why":"Lime-growing hub on the coastal highway to Michoacán; extortion and the Coahuayana crossing","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1987,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1988,"to":1998,"cartel":"colima"},{"from":1999,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Villa de Álvarez","lat":19.2667,"lng":-103.7333,"why":"Colima's metro twin city and main battleground of the CJNG-Mezcales war since 2022","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1987,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1988,"to":1998,"cartel":"colima"},{"from":1999,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]}],"facts":["The Amezcua Contreras brothers founded the Colima Cartel in 1988 and became Mexico's largest methamphetamine producers by importing ephedrine through Manzanillo; Luis and Jesús were arrested in Guadalajara on 1 June 1998.","Manzanillo is Mexico's busiest container port, handling more than 3 million TEU a year, and the principal entry point for precursor chemicals from China and India used in synthetic drug production.","Colima's homicide rate rose from 23 per 100,000 in 2015 to 71 in 2016 and 97 in 2017 as CJNG fought the Sinaloa Cartel for Manzanillo, making it the deadliest state in Mexico in most years since 2016.","Los Mezcales split from the CJNG after a riot at the Colima Cereso on 25 January 2022 that killed nine inmates; their founder José Bernabé Brizuela 'La Vaca' was arrested in Mexico City on 18 August 2022.","Colima city was ranked the most violent city in the world in 2022 with a homicide rate of about 182 per 100,000 (Citizens' Council for Public Security ranking).","Colima recorded 690 homicides in 2024 (101 per 100,000, INEGI), the country's highest rate for the third consecutive year, and 622 in 2025 (81 per 100,000, SESNSP), still the highest despite a 26% drop.","In January 2026 banners announced an alliance between La Mayiza and Los Mezcales; in November 2025 the Navy arrested 54 Mezcales members in Manzanillo, Tecomán, Villa de Álvarez and Colima under the Pez Vela 2025 strategy."],"sources":["https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/estados/los-mezcales-vs-cjng-la-ruptura-entre-carteles-que-genero-la-ola-de-violencia-en-colima/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/01/22/con-mantas-la-mayiza-anuncia-alianza-con-los-mezcales-en-colima/","https://lasillarota.com/lsr-en-ingles/2026/5/27/colima-the-small-state-with-mexicos-highest-homicide-rate-601303.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colima_Cartel","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/11/19/marina-detiene-a-54-personas-que-responden-a-los-mezcales-ex-socios-del-cjng-que-operan-en-colima/","https://www.latinometrics.com/homicides/mexico"],"verified":false},{"state":"MIC","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":["milenio"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Tierra Caliente and the Sierra-Costa grew marijuana and opium for the Guadalajara Cartel network; the Valencia family of Apatzingán and Uruapan, later the Milenio Cartel, began as growers and brokers within that system."},{"from":1990,"to":2000,"primary":"milenio","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"The Valencias' Milenio Cartel, led by Armando Valencia Cornelio, dominated Apatzingán, Uruapan and the emerging Lázaro Cárdenas port route with Colombian cocaine ties, aligned with Sinaloa's Nacho Coronel. Michoacán's rural homicide rate was already among Mexico's highest, but as feuds rather than cartel war."},{"from":2001,"to":2003,"primary":"milenio","secondary":["gulf","zetas"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"The Gulf Cartel sent Los Zetas to Michoacán around 2001-2002 to back local ally Carlos Rosales 'El Tísico' (La Empresa) against the Valencias; Armando Valencia was arrested in Guadalajara on 15 August 2003, breaking Milenio's hold."},{"from":2004,"to":2006,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["gulf","milenio","familia_michoacana"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Los Zetas and La Empresa imposed extortion and methamphetamine production in Tierra Caliente after Rosales' October 2004 arrest. Local lieutenants under Nazario Moreno and José de Jesús Méndez rebelled as La Familia Michoacana, rolling five severed heads onto a Uruapan dance floor on 6 September 2006 and prompting Calderón's Operación Conjunta Michoacán on 11 December 2006."},{"from":2007,"to":2010,"primary":"familia_michoacana","secondary":["zetas","milenio"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"La Familia expelled Los Zetas, ran industrial meth labs and a quasi-religious protection racket, threw grenades into Morelia's Independence Day crowd on 15 September 2008 (8 dead) and killed 12 federal police in July 2009. Nazario Moreno was reported dead in Apatzingán on 9 December 2010, triggering a split."},{"from":2011,"to":2013,"primary":"templarios","secondary":["familia_michoacana","autodefensas","cjng"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"The Caballeros Templarios under Servando Gómez 'La Tuta' seized Lázaro Cárdenas, exported illegally mined iron ore to China and extorted the lime and avocado industries; the Hurtado brothers' Familia remnant held the east. Autodefensas rose in Tepalcatepec and La Ruana on 24 February 2013 with quiet CJNG support, and federal forces took over the port on 4 November 2013."},{"from":2014,"to":2015,"primary":"autodefensas","secondary":["templarios","cjng","carteles_unidos"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Federal commissioner Alfredo Castillo legalized militias as Fuerza Rural (May 2014) while Nazario Moreno was killed on 9 March 2014, Enrique Plancarte on 31 March and La Tuta was captured on 27 February 2015. Self-defense fronts fragmented into Los Viagras (Sierra Santana), Juan José Farías 'El Abuelo' in Tepalcatepec and H3, while federal police killed 42 people in Tanhuato on 22 May 2015."},{"from":2016,"to":2019,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["carteles_unidos","nueva_familia_michoacana","templarios"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"CJNG pushed in from the Jalisco border (Zamora, Sahuayo, La Piedad), Uruapan and Lázaro Cárdenas, while Los Viagras, El Abuelo's Cártel de Tepalcatepec and Blancos de Troya coalesced as Cárteles Unidos in Tierra Caliente and La Nueva Familia Michoacana held Zitácuaro and the east. Homicide rates climbed from 28 per 100,000 in 2016 to 36 in 2019; CJNG hung nine bodies from a Uruapan bridge in August 2019 and began the siege of Aguililla."},{"from":2020,"to":2023,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["carteles_unidos","nueva_familia_michoacana"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"The CJNG-Cárteles Unidos war in Aguililla, Tepalcatepec, Buenavista and Apatzingán introduced armed drones, landmines and IEDs to Mexico, displaced more than 3,000 people from Tepalcatepec alone, and produced massacres in San José de Gracia (27 February 2022) and the Las Tinajas palenque in Zinapécuaro (27 March 2022, 20 dead). The homicide rate peaked near 44 per 100,000 in 2020, and autodefensa founder Hipólito Mora was killed in La Ruana on 29 June 2023."},{"from":2024,"to":2025,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["carteles_unidos","nueva_familia_michoacana"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"Extortion of the avocado and lime economies and the Tierra Caliente war continued; Uruapan mayor Carlos Manzo, an outspoken anti-cartel figure, was shot dead at a Day of the Dead festival on 1 November 2025 in an attack authorities attributed to CJNG. Nationwide protests followed and Sheinbaum presented the 57-billion-peso Plan Michoacán on 10 November 2025 with 10,506 troops under Plan Paricutín; Michoacán recorded 1,267 homicides in 2025 (25.1 per 100,000, down 15%)."},{"from":2026,"to":2026,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["carteles_unidos","nueva_familia_michoacana"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"CJNG blockades hit Michoacán after El Mencho's death on 22 February 2026; the cartel's drone unit kept bombing Cárteles Unidos positions in Tepalcatepec and security forces kept clearing hundreds of IEDs in Tierra Caliente. Ramón Álvarez Ayala 'R1', accused of ordering Manzo's killing, was arrested on 30 July 2026, and arrests in August 2026 triggered new blockades, with no side able to dominate the state."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Lázaro Cárdenas","lat":17.9583,"lng":-102.2,"why":"Mexico's largest Pacific cargo port: precursor chemicals, cocaine and iron-ore smuggling; taken over by federal forces in November 2013","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2003,"cartel":"milenio"},{"from":2004,"to":2006,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2007,"to":2010,"cartel":"familia_michoacana"},{"from":2011,"to":2013,"cartel":"templarios"},{"from":2014,"to":2015,"cartel":"government"},{"from":2016,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Apatzingán","lat":19.0886,"lng":-102.3508,"why":"Capital of Tierra Caliente and historic headquarters of the Valencias, La Familia and the Templarios; today a Los Viagras stronghold besieged by CJNG","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2003,"cartel":"milenio"},{"from":2004,"to":2006,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2007,"to":2010,"cartel":"familia_michoacana"},{"from":2011,"to":2013,"cartel":"templarios"},{"from":2014,"to":2015,"cartel":"autodefensas"},{"from":2016,"to":2026,"cartel":"carteles_unidos"}]},{"city":"Uruapan","lat":19.4208,"lng":-102.0628,"why":"World avocado capital and extortion prize; scene of La Familia's 2006 debut and the November 2025 assassination of mayor Carlos Manzo","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2003,"cartel":"milenio"},{"from":2004,"to":2006,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2007,"to":2010,"cartel":"familia_michoacana"},{"from":2011,"to":2013,"cartel":"templarios"},{"from":2014,"to":2015,"cartel":"autodefensas"},{"from":2016,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Tepalcatepec","lat":19.1875,"lng":-102.8483,"why":"Cradle of the 2013 autodefensas and El Abuelo's Cártel de Tepalcatepec; frontline of the CJNG siege from the Jalisco border since 2019","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2003,"cartel":"milenio"},{"from":2004,"to":2006,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2007,"to":2010,"cartel":"familia_michoacana"},{"from":2011,"to":2012,"cartel":"templarios"},{"from":2013,"to":2015,"cartel":"autodefensas"},{"from":2016,"to":2026,"cartel":"carteles_unidos"}]},{"city":"Zitácuaro","lat":19.4358,"lng":-100.3583,"why":"Eastern gateway to the State of Mexico and base of La Familia's Hurtado-brothers remnant (La Nueva Familia Michoacana)","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2003,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2004,"to":2006,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2007,"to":2010,"cartel":"familia_michoacana"},{"from":2011,"to":2026,"cartel":"nueva_familia_michoacana"}]},{"city":"Aguililla","lat":18.7375,"lng":-102.7869,"why":"El Mencho's birthplace in the Sierra-Costa; site of the 2019-2022 siege with drones and landmines and the El Aguaje meth corridor","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2003,"cartel":"milenio"},{"from":2004,"to":2006,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2007,"to":2010,"cartel":"familia_michoacana"},{"from":2011,"to":2013,"cartel":"templarios"},{"from":2014,"to":2015,"cartel":"autodefensas"},{"from":2016,"to":2020,"cartel":"carteles_unidos"},{"from":2021,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]}],"facts":["La Familia Michoacana announced itself by rolling five severed heads onto the dance floor of the Sol y Sombra club in Uruapan on 6 September 2006; President Calderón launched Operación Conjunta Michoacán on 11 December 2006, the opening move of the militarized drug war.","On 15 September 2008 grenades thrown into Morelia's Independence Day crowd killed 8 people and wounded more than 100, the first mass attack on civilians of the drug war.","The Knights Templar controlled Lázaro Cárdenas and shipped illegally mined iron ore to China until federal forces took over the port on 4 November 2013; Nazario Moreno was killed on 9 March 2014 and Servando Gómez 'La Tuta' was captured on 27 February 2015.","Autodefensas rose in Tepalcatepec and La Ruana on 24 February 2013 under José Manuel Mireles and Hipólito Mora; Mireles was jailed in June 2014 and Mora was murdered in La Ruana on 29 June 2023.","Michoacán's homicide rate peaked at about 44 per 100,000 in 2020; the state recorded 1,267 homicide victims in 2025 (25.1 per 100,000, down 15%), still the fifth-highest total in Mexico. On 27 March 2022 gunmen killed 20 people at a clandestine cockfight in Las Tinajas, Zinapécuaro.","Uruapan mayor Carlos Manzo was shot dead on 1 November 2025 during the Festival de las Velas; authorities blamed a CJNG cell, arrested seven of his bodyguards and on 30 July 2026 detained Ramón Álvarez Ayala 'R1' as the alleged mastermind. Sheinbaum's Plan Michoacán (10 November 2025) committed 57 billion pesos and 10,506 troops.","Since 2021 Tierra Caliente (Aguililla, Tepalcatepec, Apatzingán, Buenavista) has seen Mexico's most systematic use of landmines, IEDs and explosive-laden drones; by August 2026 InSight Crime documented a CJNG drone unit operating in five states. Cárteles Unidos and La Nueva Familia Michoacana were designated Foreign Terrorist Organizations by the United States on 20 February 2025."],"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlos_Manzo","https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/plan-michoacan-sheinbaum-12-point-strategy-pacify-state/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/08/26/cjng-perfecciona-ataques-con-drones-y-los-extiende-a-cinco-estados-de-mexico-alerta-insight-crime/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Las_Tinajas_massacre","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milenio_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Nueva_Familia_Michoacana_Organization"],"verified":false},{"state":"GUA","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Guanajuato was a quiet transit and money-laundering corridor for the Guadalajara Cartel; León's leather and shoe industry and Bajío agribusiness absorbed drug money, with no local armed groups of note."},{"from":1990,"to":2007,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["milenio","juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"After the Guadalajara Cartel split, the Sinaloa Federation (Ignacio 'Nacho' Coronel's Guadalajara-based network) controlled Highway 45 transit through León, Irapuato and Celaya, with the Milenio/Valencia organization laundering in the state; the INEGI homicide rate was 4.5 per 100,000 in 2006."},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["gulf","familia_michoacana"],"contested":true,"intensity":1,"note":"The Gulf Cartel's armed wing, Los Zetas, began pushing into the eastern Bajío (Celaya, Salamanca, Salvatierra) from San Luis Potosí and Querétaro, while La Familia Michoacana operated across the southern border in Acámbaro, Yuriria and Moroleón; the homicide rate was still 4.7 per 100,000 in 2008 (INEGI)."},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["zetas","familia_michoacana","templarios"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"After the 2010 Gulf-Zetas split the now independent Zetas ran extortion and retail cells in Celaya, Salamanca and the Laja-Bajío corridor, while La Familia and, from 2011, the Knights Templar operated in the southern municipalities; the homicide rate rose to 13.6 per 100,000 in 2012 (INEGI), still below the national average."},{"from":2013,"to":2017,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["santa_rosa_lima","sinaloa","templarios"],"contested":false,"intensity":3,"note":"The CJNG absorbed the Zetas' and Templarios' Bajío networks and made León, Irapuato and Celaya a logistics and laundering hub, while huachicol (fuel theft) around the Salamanca refinery exploded: Guanajuato led the country in illegal taps in early 2017 and was among the three most-tapped states in 2016-2018. José Antonio Yépez 'El Marro' built the Cártel de Santa Rosa de Lima from 2014 in Villagrán and in October 2017 publicly threatened the CJNG over the fuel business."},{"from":2018,"to":2020,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["santa_rosa_lima","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"The CJNG-Santa Rosa de Lima war made Guanajuato the state with the most homicides in Mexico from 2018 (INEGI: 5,370 in 2020, about 87 per 100,000 and roughly 15% of the national total). Federal Operation Golpe de Timón (3 March 2019) hit Villagrán, gunmen killed 15 in a Salamanca bar (9 March 2019) and 27 in an Irapuato rehab centre (1 July 2020); El Marro was captured at Franco Tavera, Juventino Rosas, on 2 August 2020 and successor Adán Ochoa 'El Azul' on 14 October 2020."},{"from":2021,"to":2024,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["santa_rosa_lima","sinaloa","gulf"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"CJNG became the dominant force but CSRL remnants run by El Marro's family ('La Marriza') survived in the Celaya-Villagrán-Juventino Rosas corridor through alliances with the Gulf Cartel's Escorpiones and Sinaloa's Flechas; Sinaloa cells also pushed into the south from 2022. Massacres continued (Celaya Gala Hotel 23 May 2022, 11 dead; Salvatierra posada 17 Dec 2023, 11 dead; a wave of attacks in Salamanca on 4 Oct 2024, 12 dead) and over 300 police were killed 2019-2023; INEGI counted 4,333, 4,329, 3,862 and 4,035 homicides in 2021-2024 (64 per 100,000 in 2024)."},{"from":2025,"to":2026,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["santa_rosa_lima","chapitos","gulf"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"Homicides fell sharply under the Sheinbaum-García Harfuch strategy (INEGI 3,249 in 2025, 51 per 100,000, the lowest in seven years; SESNSP 2,557 cases, -19.2%) yet Guanajuato stayed first nationally in absolute homicides for an eighth straight year (seventh by rate). The US Treasury sanctioned CSRL and El Marro on 17 December 2025; CSRL gunmen killed 11 people described as CJNG escorts at the Loma de Flores football field in Salamanca on 25 January 2026, after the San Felipe (20 May 2025, 7 dead) and Irapuato fiesta de San Juan (24 June 2025, 12 dead) massacres. CJNG roadblocks hit the state after the death of Nemesio Oseguera 'El Mencho' in an army operation on 22 February 2026, and on 24 May 2026 the FGR reported that CJNG had allied with Los Chapitos and CSRL with the Gulf Cartel over huachicol."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Celaya","lat":20.5235,"lng":-100.8157,"why":"Bajío industrial and rail hub at the heart of the CSRL-CJNG dispute; Mexico's most dangerous city for police.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2016,"cartel":"cjng"},{"from":2017,"to":2026,"cartel":"santa_rosa_lima"}]},{"city":"Salamanca","lat":20.5717,"lng":-101.1969,"why":"Pemex Ing. Antonio M. Amor refinery and pipeline hub; epicentre of huachicol and of the 2019, 2024 and 2026 massacres.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2016,"cartel":"cjng"},{"from":2017,"to":2020,"cartel":"santa_rosa_lima"},{"from":2021,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Irapuato","lat":20.6767,"lng":-101.3542,"why":"Highway 45 agro-export and logistics city; CJNG stronghold hit by the 2020 rehab-centre and 2025 fiesta massacres and mass graves.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2012,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2013,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"León","lat":21.125,"lng":-101.686,"why":"Largest city and leather-industry laundering centre; CJNG retail and finance base.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2012,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2013,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Villagrán (Santa Rosa de Lima)","lat":20.5167,"lng":-100.9917,"why":"Birthplace and fortress of the Cártel de Santa Rosa de Lima; target of Operation Golpe de Timón in March 2019.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2026,"cartel":"santa_rosa_lima"}]},{"city":"Acámbaro","lat":20.0361,"lng":-100.7217,"why":"Southern gateway from Michoacán used by La Familia, the Templarios and later CJNG and Sinaloa incursions.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2005,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2006,"to":2010,"cartel":"familia_michoacana"},{"from":2011,"to":2014,"cartel":"templarios"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]}],"facts":["Guanajuato recorded more homicides than any other Mexican state every year from 2018 to 2025; INEGI counted a peak of 5,370 in 2020 (about 87 per 100,000, roughly 15% of the national total), 4,035 in 2024 (64 per 100,000) and 3,249 in 2025 (51 per 100,000, seventh nationally by rate).","The Cártel de Santa Rosa de Lima emerged in 2014 in the Villagrán community of the same name around fuel theft; Guanajuato led the country in illegal pipeline taps in early 2017 (408 by May) and, with Hidalgo and Puebla, was among the three most-tapped states in 2016-2018, mostly around the Salamanca refinery.","José Antonio Yépez 'El Marro' was captured on 2 August 2020 in Franco Tavera, Juventino Rosas; his successor Adán Ochoa 'El Azul' fell on 14 October 2020, after which El Marro's relatives ran the group through the armed wing 'La Marriza'. The US Treasury (OFAC) sanctioned CSRL and El Marro on 17 December 2025, accusing him of running the group from prison.","The 1 July 2020 attack on a rehabilitation centre in Irapuato killed 27 people, the deadliest massacre in the state's history; in 2025 Irapuato also saw 17 bodies found in a house (26 May) and 32 bodies exhumed from a property (30 July).","Other emblematic massacres: Salamanca bar (9 March 2019, 15 dead), Celaya Gala Hotel (23 May 2022, 11 dead), Salvatierra posada (17 December 2023, 11 dead), Salamanca attacks (4 October 2024, 12 dead), San Felipe church party (20 May 2025, 7 dead), Irapuato fiesta de San Juan (24 June 2025, 12 dead) and Salamanca Loma de Flores football field (25 January 2026, 11 dead; three suspects arrested 28 January).","Guanajuato is the most dangerous state in Mexico for police: 386 officers were killed in the six years to December 2023 (about 15% of the national total), more than 300 of them between 2019 and 2023.","On 24 May 2026 the FGR delegation in Guanajuato reported that CJNG had allied with Los Chapitos and the Santa Rosa de Lima Cartel with the Gulf Cartel (Grupo Escorpiones) to control huachicol; Milenio's 3 February 2026 map placed CSRL in 33 of the state's 46 municipalities, backed by Sinaloa and Gulf cells.","CJNG leader Nemesio Oseguera 'El Mencho' died after an army operation in Tapalpa, Jalisco, on 22 February 2026; the cartel's retaliatory roadblocks and clashes reached Guanajuato and Aguascalientes."],"sources":["https://www.am.com.mx/guanajuato/2026/08/03/guanajuato-tuvo-menor-tasa-de-homicidios-en-7-anos-pero-siguio-como-puntero-en-mexico-1827258.html","https://www.milenio.com/estados/cartel-santa-rosa-de-lima-cjng-entidades-que-disputan-en-mexico-mapa","https://insightcrime.org/news/mexicos-santa-rosa-de-lima-cartel-el-marro/","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%C3%A1rtel_de_Santa_Rosa_de_Lima","https://www.seasonsofcrime.com/p/where-is-the-cjng-in-mexico","https://www.informador.mx/mexico/siete-estados-sumaron-la-mitad-del-total-de-homicidios-dolosos-en-mexico-del-2025-20260108-0123.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_in_Mexico","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_in_Mexico","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mexican_states_by_homicides","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nemesio_Oseguera_Cervantes","La Jornada, 24 May 2026: 'CJNG se alió con Los Chapitos y Cártel Santa Rosa de Lima con el del Golfo para controlar huachicol: FGR en Guanajuato' (Google News index)","El Financiero, 26 January 2026: 'Masacre en Salamanca: víctimas eran escoltas del CJNG y fueron atacados por el Cártel Santa Rosa de Lima' (Google News index)","Quadratín Bajío, 28 December 2023: 'En 6 años asesinato de policías en Guanajuato promedia 5 por mes, van 386' (Google News index)"],"verified":true},{"state":"QUE","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1999,"primary":"none","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Querétaro had no meaningful cartel structure; its role was as a discreet residence and investment haven for trafficking families close to Mexico City, with homicide rates among the lowest in the country."},{"from":2000,"to":2009,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["gulf"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The Sinaloa Federation used the Mexico City-Querétaro-Bajío highway and San Juan del Río industrial corridor for transit and laundering, while the Gulf Cartel's armed wing Los Zetas began to appear along Highway 57 from San Luis Potosí; violence stayed minimal (INEGI rate 3.0 in 2006, 3.3 in 2008)."},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["sinaloa","familia_michoacana"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Los Zetas made Querétaro a rear base and refuge during their war with the Gulf Cartel, running extortion and retail cells in the capital and San Juan del Río, while La Familia Michoacana reached Amealco and the southern border; the homicide rate stayed near 6 per 100,000 (INEGI 5.75 in 2012)."},{"from":2014,"to":2018,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["sinaloa","zetas"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"As the Zetas collapsed the CJNG took over transit and laundering through Querétaro's booming industrial parks, coexisting with Sinaloa finances; the state remained one of the five safest in Mexico."},{"from":2019,"to":2022,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["santa_rosa_lima","sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"The Santa Rosa de Lima Cartel used the Apaseo municipalities of Guanajuato as a springboard into Querétaro, Corregidora, Huimilpan, Pedro Escobedo and San Juan del Río for fuel theft and extortion; CJNG narcomantas signed 'Comandante Castro' appeared in La Lira and La Venta in November 2022 (nine people were arrested in December 2022 for placing them). The INEGI rate peaked at 8.7 per 100,000 in 2020; violence remained low but organized."},{"from":2023,"to":2026,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["santa_rosa_lima","sinaloa","gulf"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"The Guanajuato war spilled over and 2023 was a record year for violence: a video purporting to announce CJNG's arrival circulated in October 2023 and threats closed the Universidad Autónoma de Querétaro on 29 October 2023; CJNG-CSRL disputes produced the El Marqués killings (9 October 2024, 5 dead) and the Los Cantaritos bar massacre in the capital (9 November 2024, 10 dead). In early February 2026 (announced 6 February) the SSPC with naval intelligence arrested 30 members of Los Salazar (Sinaloa Cartel) in 12 simultaneous raids; Querétaro ranked seventh lowest nationally for intentional homicide in March 2026 and state figures showed year-on-year drops of 22-31% in the first half of 2026."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Santiago de Querétaro","lat":20.5888,"lng":-100.3899,"why":"State capital and industrial-financial hub; refuge and laundering centre, site of the November 2024 Los Cantaritos massacre.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1999,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2000,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"San Juan del Río","lat":20.3886,"lng":-99.9961,"why":"Highway 57 and rail corridor to Mexico City with pipelines and industrial parks; CJNG, CSRL and a local cell under 'El Genético'.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1999,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2000,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Corregidora","lat":20.545,"lng":-100.4403,"why":"Metropolitan suburb on the Guanajuato side where Santa Rosa de Lima cells entered from Apaseo.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1999,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2000,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Pedro Escobedo","lat":20.5083,"lng":-100.1408,"why":"Pipeline crossing on Highway 57 targeted for huachicol by local cells, CSRL and CJNG.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1999,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2000,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2018,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2019,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]}],"facts":["Querétaro's INEGI homicide rate stayed between roughly 3 and 9 per 100,000 from 2006 to 2020 (3.0 in 2006, 8.7 in 2020), a fraction of the national rate, and the state was ranked seventh lowest for intentional homicide in March 2026.","The state has long served as a residence, refuge and investment haven for cartel families and finances because of its proximity to Mexico City and its industrial boom; Governor Mauricio Kuri acknowledged in 2024 that people fleeing disputes in Guanajuato hide in Querétaro.","Army intelligence cited by Milenio (13 November 2024) identified CJNG and Santa Rosa de Lima cells in five municipalities: Querétaro, Corregidora, Huimilpan, Pedro Escobedo and San Juan del Río, plus a third cell in San Juan del Río led by Honorio Núñez Vargas 'El Genético'.","Threats bearing CJNG references forced the Universidad Autónoma de Querétaro to suspend classes on 29 October 2023, days after a video purporting to announce the cartel's arrival circulated; CJNG narcomantas signed 'Comandante Castro' had appeared in La Lira and La Venta in November 2022.","On 9 October 2024 five people were killed in El Marqués and on 9 November 2024 gunmen killed 10 people at the Los Cantaritos bar in Santiago de Querétaro; Security Secretary Omar García Harfuch linked the bar attack to a criminal cell involved in the CJNG-Santa Rosa de Lima dispute.","In early February 2026 (reported 6 February), 30 alleged members of Los Salazar, a Sinaloa Cartel cell led locally by Diego 'N' 'El Flaco', were arrested in 12 simultaneous raids; authorities said Sinaloa, CJNG, the Gulf Cartel and CSRL all operate in the state.","State figures reported intentional homicides down 22.4% to 31.2% year-on-year in the first half of 2026 (El Universal Querétaro, 5 June 2026; El Sol de México, 29 June 2026)."],"sources":["https://www.milenio.com/estados/guerra-entre-cjng-y-santa-rosa-de-lima-se-extiende-hasta-queretaro","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2023/11/01/entre-cjng-y-cartel-de-santa-rosa-de-lima-cuales-son-los-grupos-criminales-que-operan-en-queretaro/","https://rotativo.com.mx/queretaro-territorio-disputa-carteles-los-salazar-operativo","https://www.seasonsofcrime.com/p/where-is-the-cjng-in-mexico","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_massacres_in_Mexico","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mexican_states_by_homicides","El Sol de México, 13 March 2026: 'Querétaro, séptimo lugar entre los estados con menor homicidio doloso' (Google News index)","El Sol de México, 29 June 2026: 'Querétaro reduce 31.2% homicidios dolosos' (Google News index)"],"verified":true},{"state":"AGU","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1994,"primary":"none","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Aguascalientes was a small, calm state on the Guadalajara-Zacatecas-Torreón highway with no local cartel structure; Guadalajara Cartel and later Sinaloa loads merely passed through."},{"from":1995,"to":2007,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The Sinaloa Federation used Highway 45 through Aguascalientes city as a link between Guadalajara and the Zacatecas-Torreón-Ciudad Juárez corridor, with Juárez Cartel networks sharing the route; the INEGI homicide rate was 2.3 per 100,000 in 2006."},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["gulf"],"contested":true,"intensity":1,"note":"The Gulf Cartel's armed wing Los Zetas expanded from Zacatecas and San Luis Potosí into Aguascalientes, setting up extortion and retail cells against Sinaloa-linked operators; the homicide rate was 5.5 per 100,000 in 2008."},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["sinaloa","familia_michoacana"],"contested":true,"intensity":1,"note":"After the 2010 split the independent Zetas dominated the state's criminal market and used it as a hideout: La Familia Michoacana leader José de Jesús Méndez 'El Chango' was captured by the Federal Police at a checkpoint in Aguascalientes on 21 June 2011. Lethal violence stayed low (INEGI rate 3.6 in 2012)."},{"from":2013,"to":2017,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","zetas"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"With the Zetas fragmenting, Sinaloa reasserted the corridor while the CJNG entered from Jalisco through Calvillo and the capital's transport sector; Aguascalientes remained among the three or four least violent states."},{"from":2018,"to":2021,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["sinaloa","talibanes"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The CJNG displaced Sinaloa as the main operator, using Aguascalientes as a low-profile logistics, laundering and fuel-fraud hub, while Los Talibanes, a Zetas splinter fighting in neighbouring Zacatecas, pressed on the northern municipalities; the INEGI rate was 5.5 per 100,000 in 2020."},{"from":2022,"to":2026,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["sinaloa","talibanes"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"A September 2022 CJNG video with the cartel's logo over a map of the state asserted control; alleged plaza boss 'El Kike' was arrested with his escorts in January 2023 and indicted in June 2024 for threatening a journalist. Homicides rose nearly 20% between 2022 and 2024 as CJNG, Sinaloa and Zacatecas-based Talibanes cells disputed the highway corridors, then fell: prosecutors counted 47 intentional homicides by early July 2025 and none in November 2025. CJNG roadblocks reached the state after El Mencho's death on 22 February 2026. Proceso (3 July 2026) described the CJNG model as 'no bodies in the street' but heavy laundering, fiscal fuel theft and transport-company fronts."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Aguascalientes","lat":21.8853,"lng":-102.2916,"why":"Capital on Highway 45 between Guadalajara and Zacatecas; laundering, transport fronts and fentanyl transit.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1994,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1995,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2017,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2018,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Jesús María","lat":21.9614,"lng":-102.3436,"why":"Industrial suburb of the capital (automotive cluster) used by CJNG cells for retail and logistics.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1994,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1995,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2017,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2018,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Rincón de Romos","lat":22.2333,"lng":-102.3167,"why":"Northern corridor toward Zacatecas where CJNG faces Talibanes and Sinaloa spillover.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1994,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1995,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2017,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2018,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Calvillo","lat":21.8467,"lng":-102.7186,"why":"Western sierra border with Jalisco and Zacatecas; CJNG's earliest entry route into the state.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1994,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1995,"to":2009,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]}],"facts":["Aguascalientes has been consistently among Mexico's least violent states, with an INEGI intentional homicide rate between roughly 2.3 and 5.5 per 100,000 in 2006-2020 (2.3 in 2006, 5.5 in 2008 and 2020, 3.6 in 2012).","La Familia Michoacana leader José de Jesús Méndez Vargas 'El Chango' was captured by Federal Police at a road checkpoint in Aguascalientes on 21 June 2011, illustrating the state's role as a refuge.","In September 2022 the CJNG released a video showing its logo over a map of Aguascalientes and vowing to eliminate rivals; alleged CJNG plaza chief 'El Kike' was arrested with his escorts in January 2023 and indicted in June 2024 for threatening a journalist.","Homicides rose nearly 20% between 2022 and 2024 (Seasons of Crime, May 2024), but state prosecutors counted only 47 intentional homicides by early July 2025 and none in November 2025, with overall crime down 8.6-9.2% in 2025.","The DEA's 2025 National Drug Threat Assessment map shows both the Sinaloa Cartel and CJNG present in Aguascalientes, reflecting its position as a corridor between Guadalajara and the Zacatecas-Ciudad Juárez route, increasingly used for fentanyl.","Proceso (3 July 2026) described CJNG's low-profile model in the state: money laundering, fiscal fuel theft (huachicol fiscal) and a network of transport companies as fronts, with little visible lethal violence; CJNG roadblocks nevertheless hit the state after El Mencho's death on 22 February 2026."],"sources":["https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/2026/7/3/aguascalientes-el-corredor-discreto-del-cjng-sin-muertos-en-la-calle-pero-con-el-dinero-fluyendo-375325.html","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/05/20/narcomapa-2025-cuales-son-los-estados-donde-el-cartel-de-sinaloa-y-el-cjng-tienen-menos-presencia/","https://www.seasonsofcrime.com/p/where-is-the-cjng-in-mexico","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_de_Jes%C3%BAs_M%C3%A9ndez_Vargas","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mexican_states_by_homicides","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_in_Mexico","Infobae, 8 January 2023: 'Cayó El Kike, presunto líder del CJNG en Aguascalientes, junto con sus escoltas' (Google News index)","El Universal, 4 June 2024: 'Vinculan a proceso a El Kike, jefe de plaza del CJNG en Aguascalientes por amenazas a periodista' (Google News index)","Azteca Aguascalientes, 4 July 2025: 'Van 47 homicidios dolosos en Aguascalientes durante 2025'; El Sol de México, 22 December 2025: 'Sin homicidios en noviembre y una baja del 9.2% en delitos en 2025' (Google News index)"],"verified":true},{"state":"HID","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1999,"primary":"none","secondary":["gulf"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Hidalgo had no dominant criminal organization; the Gulf Cartel moved loads from the Huasteca (Huejutla) toward Mexico City and Pachuca served as a quiet residence for traffickers, while the Tula refinery (opened 1976) had not yet become a criminal target."},{"from":2000,"to":2009,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The Gulf Cartel, with its armed wing Los Zetas, consolidated the Huasteca and Pachuca-Tulancingo corridors for cocaine transit and began extortion and kidnapping; Heriberto Lazcano, a native of Apan, Hidalgo, made the state a Zetas rear base. INEGI homicide rates stayed at 4-5 per 100,000."},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["gulf","familia_michoacana"],"contested":true,"intensity":1,"note":"After the Gulf-Zetas split (2010) the Zetas ruled Hidalgo: in 2011 the Federal Police recognized their control of Pachuca, Mineral de la Reforma, Omitlán, Tepeapulco, Actopan, Tula, Xochiatipan and Metztitlán, adding industrial fuel theft to extortion and kidnapping; Gulf Cartel and Familia Michoacana cells fought them in the Huasteca and the Tulancingo valley. Lazcano was killed in Progreso, Coahuila, on 7 October 2012. Lethal violence stayed low (INEGI rate 3.0 in 2012)."},{"from":2013,"to":2018,"primary":"fragmented","secondary":["zetas","zetas_vieja_escuela","gulf"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"With the Zetas broken, huachicol in the Tula-Tlahuelilpan-Tepeji corridor passed to local family-based gangs protected by municipal officials, while Zetas remnants (from about 2016 as Zetas Vieja Escuela) and Gulf cells held the Huasteca; Hidalgo became the most-tapped state in Mexico with 2,121 illegal perforations in 2018."},{"from":2019,"to":2022,"primary":"fragmented","secondary":["cjng","santa_rosa_lima","gulf"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"The Tlahuelilpan pipeline explosion of 18 January 2019 killed 137 people and triggered AMLO's anti-huachicol deployment, but theft resumed as the Santa Rosa de Lima Cartel gained a foothold in Tula and the CJNG expanded along the pipeline towns; in December 2022 a CJNG faction calling itself Grupo Pantera announced its arrival by video. The INEGI rate reached 10 per 100,000 in 2020."},{"from":2023,"to":2026,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["gulf","sinaloa","other"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"The CJNG became the group with the widest presence, contesting the huachicol economy of Tula and the Mezquital valley with local gangs such as Los Hades and Gulf Cartel remnants in the Huasteca; the DEA's 2025 map lists Hidalgo among the states where CJNG predominates with a lesser Sinaloa presence, and the US Treasury (1 May 2025) sanctioned the Morfín Morfín network, including Hidalgo-based Remigio Morfín Morfín, for CJNG fuel theft. Intentional homicides stayed near 10 per 100,000 and fell 23% year-on-year to 137 cases by early August 2026."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Pachuca","lat":20.1011,"lng":-98.7591,"why":"State capital; Zetas headquarters under Lazcano 2008-2012, later CJNG retail and extortion market.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1999,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2000,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2022,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2023,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Tula de Allende","lat":20.0522,"lng":-99.3434,"why":"Pemex Miguel Hidalgo refinery and pipeline hub; the heart of Mexico's fuel-theft economy.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1999,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2000,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2022,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2023,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Tlahuelilpan","lat":20.1297,"lng":-99.2286,"why":"Site of the 18 January 2019 Tuxpan-Tula pipeline explosion that killed 137 people.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1999,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2000,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2022,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2023,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Huejutla de Reyes","lat":21.1394,"lng":-98.4197,"why":"Huasteca hub bordering Veracruz and San Luis Potosí; Gulf Cartel and Zetas route toward Mexico City.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1996,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1997,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2015,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2016,"to":2018,"cartel":"zetas_vieja_escuela"},{"from":2019,"to":2026,"cartel":"gulf"}]},{"city":"Tulancingo","lat":20.0833,"lng":-98.3667,"why":"Eastern corridor to Puebla and Veracruz on the Tuxpan pipeline; Zetas plaza turned CJNG territory.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1999,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2000,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2022,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2023,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]}],"facts":["On 18 January 2019 an illegally tapped Tuxpan-Tula Pemex pipeline exploded in Tlahuelilpan while 600-800 people were collecting gasoline, killing 137 people, the deadliest fuel-theft disaster in Mexico.","Hidalgo recorded 2,121 illegal pipeline perforations in 2018, the most of any state, and Tlahuelilpan alone at least 70 between 2016 and the explosion; Hidalgo, Puebla and Guanajuato were the three most-tapped states in 2016-2018.","In 2011 the Federal Police acknowledged that Los Zetas, led by Hidalgo native Heriberto Lazcano (born in Apan), controlled Pachuca, Mineral de la Reforma, Omitlán, Tepeapulco, Actopan, Tula, Xochiatipan and Metztitlán, and had added fuel theft to extortion and kidnapping.","The Tula refinery complex made Hidalgo the epicentre of huachicol; the Santa Rosa de Lima Cartel established a foothold in Tula (Milenio, February 2026) and a CJNG faction, Grupo Pantera, announced its arrival by video in December 2022, competing with the local gang Los Hades.","The DEA's 2025 National Drug Threat Assessment map lists Hidalgo among the 22 states where CJNG predominates, with a lesser Sinaloa Cartel presence; on 1 May 2025 OFAC sanctioned César 'Primito', Álvaro Noé and Remigio Morfín Morfín and two Tamaulipas logistics firms for CJNG fentanyl trafficking and fuel theft, with Remigio, based in Hidalgo, reported as overseeing fuel theft there.","Hidalgo's INEGI intentional homicide rate stayed between about 3 and 10 per 100,000 from 2006 to 2020 (4.0 in 2006, 3.0 in 2012, 10.0 in 2020), roughly a third of the national rate; state figures showed homicides down 18.5% by mid-June 2026 and down 23% year-on-year to 137 cases by early August 2026."],"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tlahuelilpan_pipeline_explosion","https://lasillarota.com/nacion/2019/1/21/al-huachicol-lo-controlaban-los-zetas-en-hidalgo-ahora-participan-familias-177825.html","https://lasillarota.com/hidalgo/estado/2025/5/3/huachicol-robado-en-hidalgo-por-el-cjng-era-enviado-eua-revela-departamento-del-tesoro-534320.html","https://ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20250501","https://www.seasonsofcrime.com/p/where-is-the-cjng-in-mexico","https://www.milenio.com/estados/cartel-santa-rosa-de-lima-cjng-entidades-que-disputan-en-mexico-mapa","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/05/20/narcomapa-2025-cuales-son-los-estados-donde-el-cartel-de-sinaloa-y-el-cjng-tienen-menos-presencia/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mexican_states_by_homicides","effeta.info, 11 August 2026: 'Disminuyen 23% los homicidios dolosos en Hidalgo; suma 137 casos'; Milenio, 16 June 2026: 'Registra Hidalgo disminución del 18.5 por ciento en homicidios dolosos' (Google News index)"],"verified":true},{"state":"CMX","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The DFS-protected Guadalajara network treated the capital as its political and financial hub rather than a battleground. Tepito's contraband (fayuca) market was run by local families; no organized plazas or open war."},{"from":1990,"to":1997,"primary":"juarez","secondary":["sinaloa","tijuana","gulf"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Amado Carrillo Fuentes ran much of his logistics and laundering from Mexico City (AICM air cargo, Polanco and Lomas residences) and died here on 4 July 1997 after surgery at Hospital Santa Mónica in Polanco. Rival cartels kept low-profile cells and avoided fighting in the capital."},{"from":1998,"to":2007,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["beltran_leyva","juarez","gulf"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The Sinaloa Federation's Beltrán Leyva wing (Arturo Beltrán Leyva, Édgar Valdez 'La Barbie') built the capital's wholesale distribution, protection and laundering networks. Retail narcomenudeo grew in Tepito, Iztapalapa and the Centro while the homicide rate stayed near 8-9 per 100,000."},{"from":2008,"to":2010,"primary":"beltran_leyva","secondary":["sinaloa","zetas","union_tepito"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"After the January 2008 Beltrán Leyva split, the BLO and La Barbie fought Sinaloa proxies for the capital's retail market. Tepito gangs coalesced into La Unión (2009-2012, under Francisco Hernández 'Pancho Cayagua'), initially as BLO/La Barbie distributors; Zetas cells ran kidnapping crews."},{"from":2011,"to":2016,"primary":"union_tepito","secondary":["tlahuac","sinaloa","pacifico_sur"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"La Unión consolidated narcomenudeo and extortion across Tepito, Centro Histórico and Zona Rosa; the 26 May 2013 abduction of 13 young people from the Heaven bar exposed the Tepito-Zona Rosa retail war. Felipe de Jesús Pérez Luna 'El Ojos' built the Cártel de Tláhuac (c.2011-2012) in Tláhuac, Iztapalapa, Xochimilco and Milpa Alta; Sinaloa and CPS supplied wholesale."},{"from":2017,"to":2019,"primary":"union_tepito","secondary":["anti_union","tlahuac","cjng"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Fuerza Anti-Unión split from La Unión in 2017; El Ojos was killed by marines on 20 July 2017 (8 dead, 23 arrested); Pancho Cayagua was killed in October 2017 and Roberto Moyado 'El Betito' arrested in August 2018. The 14 September 2018 Plaza Garibaldi attack by gunmen dressed as mariachis (at least five dead) marked the Unión/Anti-Unión war; the city's homicide rate peaked at about 16 per 100,000 in 2019 (roughly 1,500 killings) and CJNG entered via Gustavo A. Madero in 2019."},{"from":2020,"to":2023,"primary":"union_tepito","secondary":["cjng","anti_union","tlahuac"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"CJNG announced itself with the 26 June 2020 ambush of police chief Omar García Harfuch on Paseo de la Reforma (400+ rounds, two bodyguards and a bystander killed, 14 arrested). La Unión fragmented into cells after the arrests of Óscar Flores 'El Lunares' (January 2020) and Raúl Rojas 'Mi Jefe' (May 2022); Sinaloa cells were detected in Tlalpan (2022) and La Familia Michoacana in Milpa Alta and Tláhuac (2022); the homicide rate fell to about 13 in 2020 and kept declining."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"union_tepito","secondary":["cjng","anti_union","tlahuac"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"Eduardo Ramírez 'El Chori' was captured in 2024 but his faction kept extorting merchants in Centro and Tepito; on 20 May 2025 Clara Brugada's aides Ximena Guzmán and José Muñoz were shot dead on Calzada de Tlalpan (18 detained by May 2026, attributed to a narcomenudeo cell active in CDMX and Edomex). In July 2026 CJNG-backed Anti-Unión gunmen carried out a dozen targeted killings of Unión members in Cuauhtémoc (Centro, Morelos, Guerrero neighborhoods) and CJNG released a video threatening La Unión; INEGI put the 2024 rate at 10 per 100,000, among the country's lowest."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Tepito / Centro Histórico (Cuauhtémoc)","lat":19.4467,"lng":-99.1264,"why":"Mexico's largest contraband and retail-drug market and La Unión Tepito's birthplace; extortion of thousands of Centro merchants.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2008,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2009,"to":2026,"cartel":"union_tepito"}]},{"city":"Iztapalapa (Central de Abasto)","lat":19.3574,"lng":-99.0904,"why":"Most populous borough and the Central de Abasto wholesale market: narcomenudeo, extortion and cargo theft hub bordering Nezahualcóyotl.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2010,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2011,"to":2017,"cartel":"tlahuac"},{"from":2018,"to":2026,"cartel":"union_tepito"}]},{"city":"Tláhuac","lat":19.2857,"lng":-99.0028,"why":"Home base of the Cártel de Tláhuac, whose family leadership survived El Ojos' death in 2017 and controls the south-eastern corridor into Chalco.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2010,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2011,"to":2026,"cartel":"tlahuac"}]},{"city":"Aeropuerto Internacional (AICM, Venustiano Carranza)","lat":19.4363,"lng":-99.0721,"why":"Latin America's busiest airport: cocaine and precursor air-cargo node used by Amado Carrillo and later by Sinaloa logistics cells.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":1997,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":1998,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2010,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2011,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Polanco / Lomas de Chapultepec (Miguel Hidalgo)","lat":19.4326,"lng":-99.1919,"why":"Money-laundering, real-estate and nightlife district; Amado Carrillo died here (1997) and CJNG ambushed García Harfuch here (2020).","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":1997,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":1998,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2010,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2011,"to":2026,"cartel":"union_tepito"}]}],"facts":["Amado Carrillo Fuentes, head of the Juárez Cartel, died in Mexico City on 4 July 1997 after plastic surgery at Hospital Santa Mónica in Polanco.","On 26 May 2013, 13 young people from Tepito were abducted from the Heaven bar in the Zona Rosa; their bodies were found in a mass grave in Tlalmanalco, Estado de México, in August 2013.","Cártel de Tláhuac founder Felipe de Jesús Pérez Luna 'El Ojos' was killed by Navy marines in Tláhuac on 20 July 2017; the operation left 8 dead and 23 arrested, and his wife and daughters kept the group running.","On 26 June 2020 CJNG gunmen fired more than 400 rounds at Security Secretary Omar García Harfuch on Paseo de la Reforma, killing two bodyguards and a bystander; 14 were arrested and 12 convicted by March 2024.","Mexico City's homicide rate rose from 12.3 per 100,000 in 2017 to a peak of 16.1 in 2019 (roughly 1,500 killings), then fell to 13.1 in 2020 and 10 in 2024 (INEGI).","La Unión Tepito leaders fell in succession: Pancho Cayagua killed (October 2017), El Betito arrested (August 2018), El Lunares (January 2020), Mi Jefe (May 2022) and El Chori (2024); the group survives as semi-autonomous extortion cells present in all 16 boroughs.","Clara Brugada's private secretary Ximena Guzmán and adviser José Muñoz were shot dead on Calzada de Tlalpan on 20 May 2025; by May 2026 18 people had been detained and the hit was attributed to a narcomenudeo cell operating in CDMX and Edomex."],"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/la-union-tepito-profile/","https://politica.expansion.mx/cdmx/2023/07/04/carteles-en-cdmx","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omar_Garc%C3%ADa_Harfuch","https://www.milenio.com/policia/cartel-tlahuac-origen-legado-criminal-ojos","https://www.borderlandbeat.com/2026/07/union-tepito-members-hunted-down-across.html","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/05/14/18-detenidos-y-un-grupo-criminal-identificado-gobierno-de-la-cdmx-revela-avances-en-el-asesinato-de-ximena-guzman-y-jose-munoz/"],"verified":false},{"state":"MEX","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Estado de México was a transit ring around the capital; the southern Tierra Caliente (Tejupilco, Luvianos, Amatepec, Tlatlaya) grew marijuana and poppy for Guadalajara-era buyers linked to Guerrero and Michoacán. No open conflict."},{"from":1990,"to":1999,"primary":"juarez","secondary":["sinaloa","gulf"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Amado Carrillo's organization used Toluca airport and metro-area warehouses for logistics while kidnapping gangs such as Daniel Arizmendi 'El Mochaorejas' (arrested in Naucalpan, 1998) dominated the state's crime headlines. Cartel violence remained marginal."},{"from":2000,"to":2007,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["beltran_leyva","gulf","familia_michoacana"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"The Sinaloa Federation's Beltrán Leyva wing (La Barbie, Sergio Villarreal 'El Grande') ran metro-area distribution from residences in Lerma, Huixquilucan and Metepec; Gulf/Zetas cells appeared in northern municipalities (Ecatepec, Tultitlán). La Familia Michoacana began extorting in the south from 2006."},{"from":2008,"to":2011,"primary":"familia_michoacana","secondary":["beltran_leyva","zetas","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"La Familia Michoacana expanded across the Tierra Caliente and into metro municipalities (Naucalpan, Ecatepec, Nezahualcóyotl, Toluca) with extortion and narcomenudeo, clashing with Zetas cells in the north. The BLO-Sinaloa war played out in the metro area until La Barbie's capture in the Lerma area on 30 August 2010."},{"from":2012,"to":2016,"primary":"nueva_familia_michoacana","secondary":["templarios","guerreros_unidos","tlahuac"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"After the falls of Nazario Moreno and José de Jesús Méndez, brothers Johnny 'El Pez' and José Alfredo 'El Fresa' Hurtado Olascoaga rebuilt La Familia from Luvianos and Arcelia and took the southern Tierra Caliente, fighting the Knights Templar around Tejupilco until 2014. Guerreros Unidos worked the Ixtapan de la Sal-Coatepec Harinas belt, the Cártel de Tláhuac the Chalco corridor, and La Unión Tepito Naucalpan and Tlalnepantla."},{"from":2017,"to":2020,"primary":"nueva_familia_michoacana","secondary":["cjng","tlahuac","union_tepito"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"The state logged about 3,000 homicides a year (3,061 in 2020, INEGI), concentrated in Ecatepec, Nezahualcóyotl, Chimalhuacán, Toluca and Naucalpan, driven by extortion, robbery and narcomenudeo gangs. CJNG cells entered Toluca, Tlalnepantla and the Chalco-Ixtapaluca corridor, where El Ojos' death (July 2017) had fragmented the Cártel de Tláhuac."},{"from":2021,"to":2023,"primary":"nueva_familia_michoacana","secondary":["cjng","guerreros_unidos","tlahuac"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"La Familia's ambush at Coatepec Harinas on 18 March 2021 killed 13 state police and prosecutors' agents, and its extortion of fava, pea and avocado farmers culminated in the Texcapilla (Texcaltitlán) uprising of 8 December 2023: villagers with machetes killed 10 gunmen including plaza chief Rigoberto de la Sancha 'El Payaso', losing four of their own. The group added Pemex pipeline theft in September 2023."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"nueva_familia_michoacana","secondary":["cjng","union_tepito","tlahuac"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Operativo Enjambre (from November 2024) arrested municipal officials and police serving La Familia in southern municipalities, Operación Bastión (March 2025) seized 21 Hurtado properties, and the US indicted (24 September 2024), designated as FTO (20 February 2025) and sanctioned (April 2025) the brothers, offering $5M and $3M rewards; state reports for April 2025-March 2026 still list La Familia in 27 municipalities including Toluca and Valle de Bravo, CJNG in Tlalnepantla, Toluca, Chalco and Ixtapaluca, and extortionist 'sindicatos' (USON, 22 de Octubre, Los 300) in the metro area. INEGI counted 3,277 homicides in 2024, second nationally; CJNG blockades reached the state on 22-23 February 2026 after El Mencho was killed."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Ecatepec de Morelos","lat":19.6019,"lng":-99.0509,"why":"Mexico's most populous municipality; the state's largest extortion, narcomenudeo and femicide hotspot on the northern edge of the capital.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2007,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2008,"to":2011,"cartel":"familia_michoacana"},{"from":2012,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Nezahualcóyotl","lat":19.4006,"lng":-98.9897,"why":"Dense eastern suburb bordering Iztapalapa: retail drug market and spillover zone for Unión Tepito and Tláhuac cells; recurring top-10 municipality for homicides.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Toluca","lat":19.2826,"lng":-99.6557,"why":"State capital with an international airport and industrial corridor (Lerma); BLO safe houses in the 2000s, now contested by La Familia and CJNG.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":1999,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2000,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2010,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2011,"to":2017,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2018,"to":2026,"cartel":"nueva_familia_michoacana"}]},{"city":"Tejupilco","lat":19.9056,"lng":-100.1528,"why":"Gateway to the Tierra Caliente and to Guerrero (Arcelia); La Familia's operational hub for extortion, mining and drug routes in southern Edomex.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2005,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2006,"to":2011,"cartel":"familia_michoacana"},{"from":2012,"to":2026,"cartel":"nueva_familia_michoacana"}]},{"city":"Texcaltitlán","lat":18.9285,"lng":-99.9385,"why":"Farming municipality where the Texcapilla community rose against La Familia's extortion on 8 December 2023 (14 dead).","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2005,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2006,"to":2011,"cartel":"familia_michoacana"},{"from":2012,"to":2026,"cartel":"nueva_familia_michoacana"}]},{"city":"Chalco / Valle de Chalco","lat":19.2647,"lng":-98.8975,"why":"South-eastern corridor between Tláhuac and Puebla: Cártel de Tláhuac satellite, later CJNG cell territory (Chalco, Valle de Chalco, Ixtapaluca).","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2010,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2011,"to":2019,"cartel":"tlahuac"},{"from":2020,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]}],"facts":["INEGI recorded 3,061 homicides in Estado de México in 2020 and 3,277 in 2024, the second-highest absolute count in Mexico after Guanajuato (4,015).","Édgar Valdez Villarreal 'La Barbie', the Beltrán Leyva enforcer, was captured on 30 August 2010 at a residence in the Lerma area outside Mexico City.","On 18 March 2021, 13 state police officers and prosecutors' agents were killed in an ambush in Coatepec Harinas attributed to La Familia Michoacana.","In Texcapilla, Texcaltitlán, on 8 December 2023 villagers armed with machetes and sticks killed 10 La Familia gunmen, including plaza chief 'El Payaso', who had come to collect extortion from fava and pea growers; four villagers died and several residents remain missing.","The US Justice Department indicted Johnny 'El Pez' and José Alfredo 'El Fresa' Hurtado Olascoaga on 24 September 2024; La Nueva Familia Michoacana was designated a Foreign Terrorist Organization on 20 February 2025 and the State Department offers $5 million and $3 million for the brothers.","The Estado de México prosecutor's Operación Bastión (March 2025) seized 21 ranches, safe houses and lookout posts belonging to the Hurtado brothers in Amatepec, Luvianos, Sultepec, Tejupilco, Temascaltepec and Tlatlaya.","The bodies of the 13 Heaven bar victims abducted in Mexico City in May 2013 were found in a mass grave in Tlalmanalco, Estado de México, in August 2013."],"sources":["https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2024/12/09/revelan-placa-en-honor-a-pobladores-de-texcaltitlan-que-se-enfrentaron-a-la-familia-michoacana/","https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2025/03/31/estados/asegura-la-fgjedomex-21-inmuebles-pertenecientes-a-la-familia-michoacana","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/05/12/estos-eran-los-municipios-donde-operaba-el-cjng-la-familia-michoacana-y-los-sindicatos-criminales-del-edomex/","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/estados/el-pez-y-el-fresa-20-anos-de-operar-en-impunidad-familia-michoacana-domina-en-4-estados/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/04/15/eeuu-sanciona-al-pez-y-la-fresa-lideres-de-la-nueva-familia-michoacana/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/nacional/2025/08/01/tasa-de-homicidios-en-mexico-subio-en-ultimo-ano-de-amlo-reporta-el-inegi/"],"verified":false},{"state":"MOR","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Cuernavaca served as a weekend refuge for traffickers and politicians of the Guadalajara era; there were no organized plazas or turf fights."},{"from":1990,"to":1998,"primary":"juarez","secondary":["sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Amado Carrillo's Juárez organization enjoyed protection from state security under Governor Jorge Carrillo Olea (1994-1998), who left office in 1998 amid a scandal over police complicity with kidnappers and traffickers; Juan José Esparragoza 'El Azul' reputedly lived in the state. Violence stayed low."},{"from":1999,"to":2007,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["beltran_leyva","juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The Beltrán Leyva brothers, then the Sinaloa Federation's central-Mexico wing, made Cuernavaca their residential and operational base and bought protection from state and municipal police. Morelos functioned as a narco sanctuary with little visible violence."},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"primary":"beltran_leyva","secondary":["sinaloa","zetas"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"After the January 2008 split from El Chapo, the BLO allied with Los Zetas and turned Cuernavaca into its headquarters; marines raided a BLO party in Ahuatepec on 11 December 2009 and killed Arturo Beltrán Leyva in the Torres de Altitude apartments in Cuernavaca on 16 December 2009."},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"primary":"pacifico_sur","secondary":["rojos","guerreros_unidos","beltran_leyva"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"Héctor Beltrán Leyva's Cártel del Pacífico Sur (formed April 2010) fought La Barbie's faction and then its own splinters; CPS narcomantas triggered a de facto curfew panic in Cuernavaca in April 2010 and bodies were hung from bridges. Los Rojos (Santiago Mazari 'El Carrete') and Guerreros Unidos (the Casarrubias brothers) broke away around 2011 and split the state into two warring blocs."},{"from":2014,"to":2019,"primary":"rojos","secondary":["guerreros_unidos","cjng","pacifico_sur"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"Los Rojos held Cuernavaca, Jojutla, Puente de Ixtla and the Guerrero border while Guerreros Unidos ran the Cuautla-Yautepec-Yecapixtla corridor, despite Governor Graco Ramírez's Mando Único; CJNG cells arrived around 2017. INEGI counted 911 homicides in 2019, and El Carrete was captured on 1 August 2019 in Leonardo Bravo, Guerrero, after a ten-year hunt."},{"from":2020,"to":2023,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["nueva_familia_michoacana","guerreros_unidos","rojos"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"With Los Rojos decapitated, CJNG-aligned cells pushed into Cuernavaca, Jiutepec and Temixco (extortion of restaurants and transport), La Familia Michoacana advanced from Guerrero into the west (Puente de Ixtla, Miacatlán) and Guerreros Unidos remnants held Cuautla. INEGI counted 802 homicides in 2020 and 1,035 in 2021; from 2023 Morelos ranked second nationally in homicides per capita, behind only Colima, under Governor Cuauhtémoc Blanco."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"nueva_familia_michoacana","secondary":["cjng","sinaloa","guerreros_unidos"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"INEGI put Morelos' 2024 rate at 77 per 100,000 (second after Colima) and the state was third by homicide count in January-April 2026; 35 of 36 municipalities host criminal groups and Cuernavaca alone hosts ten. A May 2026 analysis counts La Familia Michoacana in 22 municipalities including the Cuernavaca metro, CJNG in 21 as its main rival, Mayiza-aligned Sinaloa cells in 17 centered on Cuautla, and a weakened Guerreros Unidos in 19 southern municipalities, while FGR files stress Sinaloa cells' ties to municipal officials; Operación Enjambre (May 2026) arrested municipal officials under Governor Margarita González Saravia."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Cuernavaca","lat":18.9242,"lng":-99.2216,"why":"State capital and the BLO's headquarters until Arturo Beltrán Leyva was killed here in December 2009; today hosts ten criminal groups and heavy extortion.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":1998,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":1999,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"pacifico_sur"},{"from":2014,"to":2019,"cartel":"rojos"},{"from":2020,"to":2023,"cartel":"cjng"},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"cartel":"nueva_familia_michoacana"}]},{"city":"Cuautla","lat":18.8121,"lng":-98.9552,"why":"Eastern hub on Highway 115 toward Puebla and Iguala; Guerreros Unidos' base and now the stronghold of Sinaloa-aligned cells; among Mexico's 30 most violent municipalities per capita in 2026.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":1998,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":1999,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2010,"to":2011,"cartel":"pacifico_sur"},{"from":2012,"to":2023,"cartel":"guerreros_unidos"},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Jojutla","lat":18.6147,"lng":-99.1806,"why":"Southern node on Highway 95 to Guerrero; Los Rojos' historic base and a recurring homicide hotspot.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":1998,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":1999,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2010,"to":2011,"cartel":"pacifico_sur"},{"from":2012,"to":2019,"cartel":"rojos"},{"from":2020,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Yautepec","lat":18.8833,"lng":-99.0667,"why":"Corridor between Cuernavaca and Cuautla fought over by Los Rojos and Guerreros Unidos; now within the Sinaloa-influenced eastern zone.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":1998,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":1999,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2010,"to":2011,"cartel":"pacifico_sur"},{"from":2012,"to":2019,"cartel":"rojos"},{"from":2020,"to":2023,"cartel":"guerreros_unidos"},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Jiutepec","lat":18.8828,"lng":-99.1793,"why":"Industrial suburb of Cuernavaca (CIVAC park) where CJNG-aligned cells built extortion and narcomenudeo networks from 2019-2020.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":1998,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":1999,"to":2007,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2008,"to":2009,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"pacifico_sur"},{"from":2014,"to":2019,"cartel":"rojos"},{"from":2020,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]}],"facts":["Arturo Beltrán Leyva, 'El Jefe de Jefes', was killed by Navy marines on 16 December 2009 in the Torres de Altitude apartment complex in Cuernavaca, five days after a raid on a BLO party in Ahuatepec.","Héctor Beltrán Leyva founded the Cártel del Pacífico Sur in April 2010; the BLO's collapse spawned Los Rojos, Guerreros Unidos and the Cártel Independiente de Acapulco, which fought over Morelos and Guerrero.","Los Rojos leader Santiago Mazari Hernández 'El Carrete' was captured on 1 August 2019 in Leonardo Bravo, Guerrero, after a ten-year search, and the group was subsequently considered virtually dismantled.","INEGI recorded 911 homicides in Morelos in 2019, 802 in 2020 and 1,035 in 2021; its 2024 rate of 77 per 100,000 was Mexico's second highest after Colima (123).","Since 2023 Morelos has ranked second nationally in homicides per capita; in January-April 2026 it was third by count (about 7% of national homicides), and five of its municipalities, including Cuautla and Cuernavaca, were among the country's 30 most violent per capita.","FGR and state investigations in 2026 counted 11-16 criminal organizations in Morelos, with presence in 35 of 36 municipalities and ten groups operating in Cuernavaca alone.","Highways 95, 95D and 115 make Morelos the main corridor between Guerrero's heroin and cocaine routes and Mexico City, which is why the BLO, Los Rojos and Guerreros Unidos all based leadership here."],"sources":["https://www.noroeste.com.mx/colaboraciones/la-pugna-del-crimen-organizado-por-morelos-HC22461152","https://politica.expansion.mx/estados/2026/05/25/morelos-es-disputado-por-11-organizaciones-criminales-corrupcion-de-autoridades-clave-para-su-operacion","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/nacional/2026/05/26/el-narco-se-disputa-las-calles-de-morelos-que-carteles-tienen-presencia-en-el-estado/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beltr%C3%A1n-Leyva_Cartel","https://www.infobae.com/america/mexico/2022/03/25/morelos-sexto-estado-con-mayor-numero-de-homicidios-dolosos-en-administracion-de-la-4t/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/nacional/2025/08/01/tasa-de-homicidios-en-mexico-subio-en-ultimo-ano-de-amlo-reporta-el-inegi/"],"verified":false},{"state":"PUE","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"none","secondary":["guadalajara"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Puebla was a transit and rest corridor between the port of Veracruz and Mexico City with no organized plazas; Guadalajara-era traffickers passed through or kept families here."},{"from":1990,"to":2005,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["juarez","sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The Gulf Cartel's Veracruz-Puebla-Mexico City corridor (Highway 150D) moved cocaine through the state while Juárez and Sinaloa figures used Puebla city as a low-profile residence and laundering site. The state was widely described as a criminal 'sanctuary' with negligible cartel violence."},{"from":2006,"to":2013,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["gulf","sinaloa","beltran_leyva"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"Los Zetas planted cells in Puebla city, Tehuacán, San Martín Texmelucan and Huauchinango, running kidnapping and extortion and pioneering pipeline theft along the Tepeaca-Acatzingo corridor; the 2010 Gulf-Zetas war spilled over. BLO fugitive Sergio Villarreal 'El Grande' was arrested in the Puebla metropolitan area on 12 September 2010."},{"from":2014,"to":2019,"primary":"fragmented","secondary":["zetas","rojos","cjng"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"The Triángulo Rojo (Tepeaca, Acatzingo, Quecholac, Palmar de Bravo, Tecamachalco, Acajete) became Mexico's fuel-theft epicenter in 2014-2017 under local bosses Roberto de los Santos 'El Bukanas', Antonio Martínez 'El Toñín' and 'La Negra'; the Palmarito Tochapan clash of 3 May 2017 killed four soldiers and six civilians. Los Rojos worked the Mixteca (Izúcar de Matamoros), CJNG cells arrived, and homicides climbed from 1,083 (2017) to 1,274 (2019) before the December 2018 federal anti-huachicol crackdown shut the valves."},{"from":2020,"to":2023,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["sinaloa","nueva_familia_michoacana","other"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"CJNG cells took over narcomenudeo in Puebla city, Cholula and the San Martín Texmelucan valley and pressed into the Triángulo Rojo, while Sinaloa cells ran synthetic-drug labs and La Familia Michoacana probed the Mixteca from Guerrero. Huachicol clans (El Toñín remained wanted) shifted to cargo robbery, kidnapping and cobro de piso; homicides fell from 1,031 in 2020 to a 2024 rate of 9 per 100,000."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["sinaloa","nueva_familia_michoacana","other"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"CJNG remained the most documented organization, with cells such as 'Operativa Barredora' blamed for a February 2026 triple killing outside an Angelópolis bar and a Puebla-Tlaxcala border group called La Barredora (unrelated to Tabasco's) linked to six severed heads in August 2025; Mayiza-aligned Sinaloa cells were tied to clandestine labs. A reshuffle of CJNG-linked cells raised Triángulo Rojo killings even as statewide homicides fell 21.1% in the first half of 2026 versus 2025; El Mencho's death on 22 February 2026 caused no major blockades in Puebla."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Puebla (city and Angelópolis-Cholula)","lat":19.0414,"lng":-98.2063,"why":"Fourth-largest metro area in Mexico: narcomenudeo, laundering and nightlife market where BLO fugitives hid in 2010 and CJNG cells fight today.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1990,"to":2005,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2006,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2019,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2020,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Tepeaca (Triángulo Rojo)","lat":18.9631,"lng":-97.9014,"why":"Hub of the Triángulo Rojo on the Minatitlán-Mexico City pipeline and the Amozoc-Perote and Arco Norte highways; controlled by huachicol clans (El Bukanas, El Toñín) that CJNG now courts or displaces.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2005,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2006,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2023,"cartel":"other"},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"San Martín Texmelucan","lat":19.2842,"lng":-98.4386,"why":"Industrial corridor on the Mexico City-Puebla highway bordering Tlaxcala; huachicol, cargo theft and narcomenudeo disputed by Zetas remnants, CJNG and Sinaloa cells.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2005,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2006,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2023,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Tehuacán","lat":18.4617,"lng":-97.3928,"why":"Gateway to Oaxaca and southern Veracruz; a Zetas kidnapping base in 2008-2013 and later a CJNG-Sinaloa transit node.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2005,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2006,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2019,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2020,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Izúcar de Matamoros","lat":18.6019,"lng":-98.4675,"why":"Mixteca gateway toward Guerrero and Morelos used by BLO successors, Los Rojos and now La Familia Michoacana.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2007,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2008,"to":2011,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2012,"to":2019,"cartel":"rojos"},{"from":2020,"to":2026,"cartel":"nueva_familia_michoacana"}]}],"facts":["Beltrán Leyva lieutenant Sergio Villarreal Barragán 'El Grande' was arrested by marines in the Puebla metropolitan area on 12 September 2010.","Between 2014 and 2017 the Triángulo Rojo (Tepeaca, Acatzingo, Quecholac, Palmar de Bravo, Tecamachalco, Acajete) concentrated Mexico's highest number of clandestine taps on Pemex pipelines, making Puebla the country's fuel-theft epicenter.","On 3 May 2017 a clash between soldiers and huachicoleros in Palmarito Tochapan (Quecholac) left four soldiers and six civilians dead; video of a soldier executing a captive caused a national scandal.","INEGI counted 1,083 homicides in Puebla in 2017, 1,249 in 2018, a peak of 1,274 in 2019 (rate 19 per 100,000) and 1,031 in 2020; by 2024 the rate had fallen to 9, among the country's lowest.","Fuel-theft boss Antonio Valente Martínez Fuentes 'El Toñín' remained a priority target in 2026, while Roberto de los Santos de Jesús 'El Bukanas' led the rival Triángulo Rojo faction.","In the first half of 2026 homicide victims in Puebla fell 21.1% year on year (47 in June 2026 versus 75 in June 2025), even as CJNG-linked cell reshuffles raised killings in the Triángulo Rojo.","A cell identified as 'Operativa Barredora', linked to CJNG, was blamed for killing three people outside a bar in Angelópolis in February 2026."],"sources":["https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/08/28/que-es-el-triangulo-rojo-en-puebla-ruta-de-huachicol-asaltos-y-extorsiones-en-la-mira-del-cjng/","https://puebla.quadratin.com.mx/investigacion/reduce-puebla-homicidios-pero-persisten-disputas-por-huachicol-y-narcomenudeo/","https://www.eluniversalpuebla.com.mx/estado/se-mantiene-elevada-incidencia-de-homicidios-dolosos-en-puebla-inegi/","https://www.lajornadadeoriente.com.mx/puebla/reacomodo-cjng-triangulo-rojo-homicidios-puebla/","https://justiceinmexico.org/huachicoleros-rising-mexico/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/nacional/2025/08/01/tasa-de-homicidios-en-mexico-subio-en-ultimo-ano-de-amlo-reporta-el-inegi/"],"verified":false},{"state":"TLA","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"primary":"none","secondary":["other"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"No cartel established plazas in Mexico's smallest state; the dominant criminal economy was sex trafficking by family clans from Tenancingo and San Pablo del Monte (Carreto, Granados, Meléndez, Rojas Romero), with roots in the 1970s, feeding La Merced in Mexico City and, from the 1990s, New York. The US Justice Department later called Tenancingo the leading source of trafficking victims to the United States."},{"from":2010,"to":2018,"primary":"fragmented","secondary":["zetas","other"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Zetas cells operating out of Puebla and Veracruz extorted and kidnapped around Apizaco, Huamantla and Calpulalpan in 2010-2013, and huachicol spread along the Tuxpan-Azcapotzalco pipeline through Calpulalpan and Nanacamilpa. Homicides stayed low (75 in 2012, 152 in 2018 per INEGI) while US federal prosecutions dismantled several Tenancingo trafficking families."},{"from":2019,"to":2023,"primary":"fragmented","secondary":["cjng","sinaloa","other"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Homicides rose to 189 in 2019 and held at 140-160 a year (rate 11-12 per 100,000) as CJNG and Sinaloa cells used Tlaxcala as a logistics and hideout zone next to Puebla's San Martín Texmelucan corridor; an industrial-scale synthetic-drug lab was dismantled in Santa Rosa de Lima, Ixtacuixtla, in December 2023. La Unión Tepito ran small narcomenudeo and extortion cells in the state."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"fragmented","secondary":["cjng","nueva_familia_michoacana","union_tepito"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"INEGI counted 174 homicides in 2024 (rate 12); six severed heads left at Ixtacuixtla's borders in August 2025 were attributed to La Barredora, a Puebla-Tlaxcala border group, and the state's only narcobloqueo after El Mencho's death (February 2026) occurred in Ixtacuixtla, where about 900 kg of cocaine were seized in June 2026. Federal operations arrested a La Familia Michoacana operator ('El Callejas', March 2026) and a Unión Tepito extortion boss hiding in the state (May 2026), while trafficking networks tied to local officials persisted."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Tenancingo","lat":19.1633,"lng":-98.3306,"why":"Town of about 10,000 identified by the US DOJ as the leading source of sex-trafficking victims to the United States; run by family clans, not cartels.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2026,"cartel":"other"}]},{"city":"Apizaco","lat":19.4131,"lng":-98.1437,"why":"Rail and industrial hub of central Tlaxcala; Zetas extortion base in 2010-2013 and site of recent narcomenudeo homicides.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Calpulalpan","lat":19.5875,"lng":-98.5697,"why":"Border municipality with Hidalgo and Estado de México on the Tuxpan-Azcapotzalco pipeline; the state's main fuel-theft zone.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Ixtacuixtla de Mariano Matamoros","lat":19.3167,"lng":-98.3667,"why":"Enclave bordering Puebla's San Martín Texmelucan corridor: synthetic lab (2023), six heads (2025), the state's only CJNG narcobloqueo (Feb 2026) and a 900 kg cocaine seizure (June 2026).","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2013,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2014,"to":2022,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2023,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"San Pablo del Monte","lat":19.1181,"lng":-98.1681,"why":"Southern border town with Puebla; second hub of the Tenancingo-style trafficking clans and a narcomenudeo crossing point.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2009,"cartel":"other"},{"from":2010,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]}],"facts":["Tenancingo, a town of roughly 10,000 people, has been identified by the US Department of Justice as the leading source of sex-trafficking victims to the United States, with estimates that around 1,000 residents are involved in trafficking networks whose roots date to the 1970s.","The US State Department's 2024 Trafficking in Persons Report ranked Tlaxcala among the three Mexican states with the highest trafficking prevalence; family networks named include Los Meléndez, Los Carreto and Los Rojas Romero.","Tlaxcala's prosecutors documented 53 sex-trafficking victims between January 2021 and June 2025, detained 27 traffickers in-state, and 35 Tlaxcala-born traffickers were arrested in other states; a Tenancingo trafficker was extradited to the US in September 2024.","INEGI homicide counts for Tlaxcala doubled in a decade: 75 (2012), 152 (2018), 189 (2019), 154 (2020), 160 (2021), 155 (2022), 140 (2023) and 174 (2024, a rate of 12 per 100,000).","In August 2025 six severed heads were found in a container at Ixtacuixtla's municipal limits, attributed by intelligence to La Barredora, a group active on the Tlaxcala-Puebla border.","After El Mencho was killed on 22 February 2026, the only narcobloqueo in Tlaxcala took place in Ixtacuixtla; in June 2026 about 900 kg of cocaine (1.8 million doses) were seized there at a vegetable-distributor front.","In December 2023 authorities dismantled an industrial-scale synthetic-drug laboratory with dozens of drums of precursors in Santa Rosa de Lima, Ixtacuixtla."],"sources":["https://www.excelsior.com.mx/nacional/tlaxcala-complice-en-la-crisis-de-trata-de-personas/1742438","https://gentetlx.com.mx/2025/08/03/sumo-tlaxcala-174-homicidios-durante-2024/","https://laprensadetlaxcala.com/2026/07/ixtacuixtla-tlaxcala-se-convierte-en-enclave-estrategico-del-crimen-organizado-y-el-narcotrafico/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tenancingo,_Tlaxcala","https://intoleranciadiario.com/tlax/articles/2026/05/26/1053287-operativos-federales-exhiben-presencia-de-grupos-criminales-en-tlaxcala.html","https://sintesis.com.mx/tlaxcala/2025/07/30/trata-de-personas-en-tlaxcala/"],"verified":false},{"state":"GRO","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"guadalajara","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Guerrero's Sierra Madre del Sur (Tierra Caliente, Sierra of Tecpan and Heliodoro Castillo, Montaña) was already a poppy and marijuana heartland supplying Félix Gallardo's Guadalajara federation, with Acapulco as a money-laundering and retail hub. Violence was mostly caciquismo and dirty-war residue rather than cartel wars."},{"from":1990,"to":2004,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["beltran_leyva","juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"After the Guadalajara cartel broke up, the Sinaloa federation, with the Beltrán Leyva brothers as its Guerrero operators and Amado Carrillo's Juárez cartel using Acapulco and Sierra airstrips, monopolized opium-gum purchases and the Acapulco plaza. Homicide rates were high by national standards but largely rural and political; the state was effectively a single-cartel territory (Sinaloa monopoly pre-2005)."},{"from":2005,"to":2009,"primary":"beltran_leyva","secondary":["sinaloa","zetas","gulf"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Arturo Beltrán Leyva's organization, then still the Sinaloa federation's arm with Edgar Valdez 'La Barbie' as enforcer, fought the Gulf Cartel's Zetas for Acapulco in 2005-2006 (the first police decapitations, April 2006). The BLO broke with Sinaloa in 2008 and Arturo was killed in Cuernavaca on 16 December 2009, setting up a succession war; the state rate rose from 25 to 42 per 100,000 (SESNSP 2006-2009)."},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"primary":"fragmented","secondary":["cida","pacifico_sur","guerreros_unidos"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"The BLO's collapse split Acapulco between La Barbie's Cártel Independiente de Acapulco and Héctor Beltrán Leyva's Cártel del Pacífico Sur, with the Sinaloa-backed La Barredora also fighting (15 headless bodies at Plaza Sendero, 8 January 2011); Guerreros Unidos (Iguala, 2011) and Los Rojos (Chilpancingo) emerged from BLO remnants while La Familia/Templarios held Tierra Caliente. Guerrero tied Chihuahua for Mexico's highest homicide rate in 2012 (77 per 100,000, 2,783 murders, INEGI) and Acapulco was among the world's most violent cities."},{"from":2014,"to":2018,"primary":"fragmented","secondary":["guerreros_unidos","rojos","ardillos"],"contested":true,"intensity":5,"note":"Ayotzinapa: 43 students abducted in Iguala on 26 September 2014 by police working with Guerreros Unidos. Rojos vs Ardillos made Chilapa Mexico's most violent municipality by 2018, Rojos fought Guerreros Unidos in the Norte, Los Tequileros and La Familia Michoacana held Tierra Caliente, CJNG entered the Costa Grande and Los Tlacos formed in Heliodoro Castillo (May 2017). Acapulco logged 918 murders in 2016 and the army disarmed its police in September 2018; the state rate peaked at 64.3 per 100,000 in 2017."},{"from":2019,"to":2023,"primary":"fragmented","secondary":["nueva_familia_michoacana","tlacos","cjng"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"Homicides eased (34 per 100,000 in 2020) but fragmentation deepened: Los Tlacos pushed from the Sierra into Chilpancingo, Eduardo Neri and Iguala against Ardillos, Rojos and Guerreros Unidos; the Hurtado brothers' Familia Michoacana dominated Tierra Caliente (Los Tequileros massacred 20 in San Miguel Totolapán, 5 October 2022) and clashed with CJNG in the Costa Grande sierra; Acapulco's extortion-driven gang economy was upended by Hurricane Otis (25 October 2023). Some 40 criminal networks operated statewide."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"fragmented","secondary":["ardillos","tlacos","nueva_familia_michoacana"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"Chilpancingo mayor Alejandro Arcos was beheaded on 6 October 2024 and 11 bodies were dumped on the Chilpancingo highway on 6 November amid the Ardillos-Tlacos war; La Familia Michoacana fought CJNG in the Coyuca de Catalán sierra until a July 2025 truce and besieged Guajes de Ayala, prompting armed self-defense (2026); Ardillos drone-bomb attacks displaced 800-1,000 families in Chilapa (May 2026); CJNG cells staged roadblocks after El Mencho's death on 22 February 2026. Guerrero still produced 5.6% of national homicides in January-July 2026 (sixth among states)."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Acapulco","lat":16.8531,"lng":-99.8237,"why":"Pacific port and tourist economy: cocaine landing point, laundering hub and Mexico's largest extortion market","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2004,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2005,"to":2009,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2010,"to":2013,"cartel":"cida"},{"from":2014,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Chilpancingo","lat":17.5514,"lng":-99.5006,"why":"State capital on the Mexico City-Acapulco highway; Rojos base, then the Ardillos-Tlacos battleground","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2004,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2005,"to":2009,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2010,"to":2019,"cartel":"rojos"},{"from":2020,"to":2026,"cartel":"tlacos"}]},{"city":"Iguala","lat":18.3449,"lng":-99.5387,"why":"Northern hub for heroin moving to Morelos and Mexico City, gold mining and the Ayotzinapa case","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2004,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2005,"to":2010,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2011,"to":2019,"cartel":"guerreros_unidos"},{"from":2020,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Zihuatanejo","lat":17.6417,"lng":-101.5519,"why":"Costa Grande resort and gateway to the poppy sierra bordering Michoacán; CJNG's coastal stronghold","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2004,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2005,"to":2009,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"templarios"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Chilapa de Álvarez","lat":17.5947,"lng":-99.1772,"why":"Gateway to the Montaña poppy zone; Rojos-Ardillos war made it Mexico's most violent municipality (2018)","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2004,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2005,"to":2009,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"rojos"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"ardillos"}]},{"city":"Ciudad Altamirano","lat":18.3583,"lng":-100.6667,"why":"Tierra Caliente hub on the Michoacán border; La Familia Michoacana's Guerrero base","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2004,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2005,"to":2008,"cartel":"beltran_leyva"},{"from":2009,"to":2013,"cartel":"familia_michoacana"},{"from":2014,"to":2026,"cartel":"nueva_familia_michoacana"}]}],"facts":["Guerrero is Mexico's principal opium-poppy producer; its Sierra (Tierra Caliente, Sierra of Heliodoro Castillo, Montaña) supplied heroin gum as national poppy cultivation rose 21% between 2015 and 2017 (UNODC), making Mexico the world's third-largest producer.","In 2012 Guerrero tied Chihuahua for Mexico's highest homicide rate at 77 per 100,000 (2,783 murders, INEGI); the SESNSP rate peaked again at 64.3 per 100,000 in 2017 before easing to 34.2 in 2020.","Acapulco recorded 918 murders in 2016 (about 103 per 100,000), 863 in 2017 and 874 in 2018, ranking among the world's most violent cities; the armed forces took over and disarmed the municipal police in September 2018.","On 26 September 2014, 43 students from the Ayotzinapa rural teachers' college were abducted in Iguala by municipal police working with Guerreros Unidos, a Beltrán Leyva splinter founded in 2011.","Los Tequileros killed 20 people including the mayor in San Miguel Totolapán on 5 October 2022; Los Tlacos (formed May 2017 in Heliodoro Castillo) and Los Ardillos (Ortega family, Quechultenango-Chilapa, active since about 2000) have fought for Chilpancingo and Chilapa since 2020.","Chilpancingo mayor Alejandro Arcos was murdered and beheaded on 6 October 2024, six days after taking office; 11 bodies were dumped on the Chilpancingo highway on 6 November 2024 amid the Tlacos-Ardillos war.","Hurricane Otis (Category 5, 25 October 2023) devastated Acapulco, killing 52 and damaging over 85% of hotels; in May 2026 Ardillos drone-bomb attacks in Chilapa displaced 800-1,000 families, and Guerrero accounted for 5.6% of national homicides in January-July 2026."],"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/news/criminal-violence-paralyzes-guerrero-mexico/","https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/mayor-killed-chilpancingo-guerrero/","https://www.cbsnews.com/amp/news/bodies-found-dumped-highway-guerrero-mexico","https://fortune.com/2026/05/10/mexican-cartel-los-ardillos-explosives-drones-attacks-guerrero-rural-communities-families-flee/","https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/civilians-that-were-under-siege-by-a-mexican-cartel-fight-back-with-ak-47s-and-grenades","https://www.scielo.org.mx/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0188-25032024000100077"],"verified":false},{"state":"OAX","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"oaxaca","secondary":["guadalajara"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Pedro Díaz Parada's Isthmus organization (Cártel del Istmo) grew and moved marijuana from the Isthmus and Sierra Sur for the Guadalajara federation; Pedro was jailed in 1985 (33-year sentence), escaped in 1987 and had the sentencing federal judge murdered. Oaxaca's killings were overwhelmingly agrarian and communal rather than cartel-driven."},{"from":1990,"to":2006,"primary":"oaxaca","secondary":["tijuana","juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"After escaping again in January 1992, the Díaz Parada brothers (Pedro, 'Don Chuy', 'Don Cheto') shifted to Colombian cocaine through Salina Cruz and Isthmus ranches, aligning with the Arellano Félix organization (formally from 2003) and the Juárez cartel, while Sinaloa maritime routes touched the Costa (Puerto Escondido, Huatulco). The 28.7 per 100,000 rate of 2006 reflected land and political conflict, not cartel wars."},{"from":2007,"to":2012,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["oaxaca","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"Pedro Díaz Parada was recaptured in Oaxaca City in January 2007, the first major arrest of the Calderón presidency, and Los Zetas took over the Isthmus (Juchitán, Matías Romero, Ixtepec rail junction) and the Central American migrant kidnapping economy, publicly executing rivals outside Santo Domingo temple in Oaxaca City in October 2010. The homicide rate actually fell to 12 per 100,000 in 2012 under Zetas dominance."},{"from":2013,"to":2017,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["zetas","cjng"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"The Zetas' 2013 collapse left remnant cells in the Isthmus while the Sinaloa Cartel consolidated the Valles Centrales and Costa and CJNG pushed into the Costa Chica and Mixteca from Guerrero. Homicides climbed from 13.5 to 22.6 per 100,000 as extortion and fuel theft along the Salina Cruz pipelines spread."},{"from":2018,"to":2022,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","other"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Peak years: more than 1,180 victims a year in 2018-2019 (24.7 per 100,000 in 2019). Killings concentrated in the Isthmus (Juchitán, Santo Domingo Tehuantepec, Salina Cruz) and Costa (Pinotepa Nacional, Jamiltepec, San Pedro Mixtepec), where Sinaloa cells, CJNG franchises and Guerrero-based groups such as Guerreros Unidos competed; the Isthmus alone supplied 26% of state homicides in January-April 2022."},{"from":2023,"to":2026,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","guerreros_unidos"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"CJNG announced its arrival in the Isthmus by video on 18 January 2023, targeting the Interoceanic Corridor and huachicol; Juchitán became the state's most violent municipality (103 homicides in 2024) and Pinotepa Nacional hit 77 per 100,000. The Navy dismantled the Sinaloa cell 'Los Cabrera' in Ixtepec (May 2025) and CJNG-linked cells ('Los Cromo') staged Isthmus roadblocks on 22 February 2026 after El Mencho's death; Oaxaca logged 898 victims in 2024, 894 in 2025 and 495 in January-July 2026 (ninth nationally)."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Salina Cruz","lat":16.1667,"lng":-95.2,"why":"Pacific port and Pemex refinery: cocaine entry, fuel theft and terminus of the Interoceanic Corridor","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2006,"cartel":"oaxaca"},{"from":2007,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Juchitán de Zaragoza","lat":16.4333,"lng":-95.0167,"why":"Commercial capital of the Isthmus and wind-energy corridor; Oaxaca's most violent municipality since 2020","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2006,"cartel":"oaxaca"},{"from":2007,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2022,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2023,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Matías Romero","lat":16.8797,"lng":-95.0389,"why":"Rail junction on the Isthmus migrant route toward Veracruz; Zetas kidnapping hub, now Interoceanic rail node","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2006,"cartel":"oaxaca"},{"from":2007,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Puerto Escondido","lat":15.862,"lng":-97.0767,"why":"Costa tourism pole and maritime cocaine landing zone (San Pedro Mixtepec)","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Pinotepa Nacional","lat":16.3411,"lng":-98.0536,"why":"Costa Chica corridor into Guerrero; highest per-capita homicide rate in Oaxaca (77 per 100,000 in 2024)","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"guadalajara"},{"from":1990,"to":2016,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2017,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Oaxaca de Juárez","lat":17.0732,"lng":-96.7266,"why":"State capital and Valles Centrales retail-drug market; site of Díaz Parada's 2007 capture and 2010 Zetas executions","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2006,"cartel":"oaxaca"},{"from":2007,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]}],"facts":["Pedro Díaz Parada, 'El Cacique de Oaxaca', built the Isthmus-based Oaxaca Cartel from the late 1970s; arrested in 1985 and sentenced to 33 years, he escaped in 1987 and January 1992 and was recaptured in Oaxaca City in mid-January 2007, the first major capture of the Calderón presidency.","Oaxaca's homicide rate fell from 28.7 per 100,000 in 2006 to 12.0 in 2012 during the Zetas' Isthmus dominance, then rose to 24.7 in 2019 (SESNSP); annual victims exceeded 1,180 in 2018-2019.","In 2024 Oaxaca recorded 898 homicide victims; Juchitán de Zaragoza was the most violent municipality with 103, followed by San Pedro Mixtepec (44), Salina Cruz (42) and Pinotepa Nacional (41), which ranked 28th nationally at 77 per 100,000.","CJNG announced its arrival in the Isthmus with a video on 18 January 2023 ('Comandante Sierra'); the state security secretary confirmed its presence on 6 February 2023, with the Interoceanic Corridor and fuel theft as targets.","The DEA's 2025 National Drug Threat Assessment lists Oaxaca among states where the Sinaloa Cartel dominates transit, with CJNG present but not dominant; the Navy arrested the Sinaloa cell 'Los Cabrera' in Ixtepec in May 2025.","On 22 February 2026 CJNG-linked cells staged roadblocks and arson in Isthmus towns as part of the nationwide reaction to El Mencho's killing (252 roadblocks across Jalisco and 19 other states).","Oaxaca closed 2025 with 894 homicide victims and registered 495 in January-July 2026 (ninth most violent state); the mayors of Santiago Amoltepec (15 May 2025) and San Mateo Piñas (16 June 2025) were assassinated."],"sources":["https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna16681695","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oaxaca_Cartel","https://oaxaca.eluniversal.com.mx/tendencias/cartel-jalisco-nueva-generacion-desde-cuando-opera-en-el-istmo-de-oaxaca/","https://elindependiente.mx/estados/2025/02/21/juchitan-el-municipio-mas-violento-de-oaxaca/","https://imparcialoaxaca.mx/oaxaca/concentran-6-municipios-los-homicidios-dolosos/","https://imparcialoaxaca.mx/nacional/controla-el-cartel-de-sinaloa-oaxaca-hay-presencia-del-cjng-indica-la-dea/"],"verified":false},{"state":"CHP","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"none","secondary":["oaxaca","guadalajara"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The border was defined by Guatemalan refugee flows and counterinsurgency, not trafficking; only marginal Soconusco smuggling tied to Díaz Parada's Isthmus network and the first Colombian cocaine flights for the Guadalajara federation. No cartel plaza structure existed."},{"from":1990,"to":1999,"primary":"juarez","secondary":["sinaloa","oaxaca"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Amado Carrillo Fuentes' Juárez cartel landed Colombian cocaine jets on clandestine strips in the Selva and Soconusco and moved loads north by road through Tuxtla and the Isthmus. The 1994 Zapatista uprising militarized the state but was unrelated to trafficking; homicide levels were low."},{"from":2000,"to":2006,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["juarez","gulf"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The Sinaloa federation inherited the Guatemala corridor (La Mesilla-Frontera Comalapa, Suchiate and Puerto Chiapas) through local operators later led by the Rivera family in Tuxtla Gutiérrez; the Gulf Cartel's Zetas began scouting Tapachula around 2005-2006. Homicide rate about 7-8 per 100,000."},{"from":2007,"to":2012,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["sinaloa","gulf"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"Los Zetas seized Tapachula and the Soconusco and the migrant route (Arriaga-Ixtepec train, Comitán), kidnapping and extorting Central Americans, while Sinaloa kept the sierra routes. The rate peaked at 12.3 per 100,000 in 2011, still among Mexico's lowest."},{"from":2013,"to":2020,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["zetas","cjng"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"After Z-40's 2013 arrest the Zetas structure collapsed and Sedena documents show Sinaloa hegemony under Ramón Gilberto Rivera Estrada 'El Junior' (Tuxtla Gutiérrez) until 2021-2022; CJNG made its first Pacific-coast incursions around 2018-2019. Rate 7.5-10 per 100,000."},{"from":2021,"to":2024,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","chiapas_guatemala","other"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"CJNG gunmen killed 'El Junior' in Tuxtla (2021) and, through the allied Cártel de Chiapas y Guatemala (founded 2022 by the Calderón family around the La Mesilla crossing) and 'El Maíz' cells, fought Sinaloa for Frontera Comalapa, Chicomuselo, Motozintla and La Concordia: 3,000+ fled Frontera Comalapa in May 2023, 15,780 people were displaced between January 2023 and June 2024, and CJNG massacred 11 in Nuevo Morelia, Chicomuselo (12-13 May 2024). Los Machetes vs Los Herrera in Pantelhó-Chenalhó and the Tila displacement (June 2024) added highland fronts."},{"from":2025,"to":2026,"primary":"cjng","secondary":["chiapas_guatemala","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Governor Eduardo Ramírez's Fuerza Pakal (December 2024) reopened roads and commerce in Frontera Comalapa and Motozintla by March 2025, but CJNG and the CCyG consolidated the border and Soconusco (Acapetahua, Mapastepec, Escuintla, Tapachula) as Sinaloa, weakened by its own civil war, held the northern sierra. Five state police were killed in a Frontera Comalapa ambush (June 2025), Chiapas police raided La Mesilla, Guatemala (8 June 2025), CCyG founder 'Don Balde' was killed (July 2025), and 2026 investigations alleged Pakal officers on the CJNG payroll."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Tapachula","lat":14.9,"lng":-92.2667,"why":"Soconusco hub and Mexico's main southern border city; migrant bottleneck and Zetas' 2007-2012 stronghold","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1990,"to":1999,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2000,"to":2006,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2007,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2021,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2022,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Frontera Comalapa","lat":15.6614,"lng":-92.1425,"why":"La Mesilla crossing on the Pan-American Highway: main land route for cocaine and migrants from Huehuetenango; epicenter of the 2021-2025 war","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1990,"to":1999,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2000,"to":2006,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2007,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2021,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2022,"to":2026,"cartel":"chiapas_guatemala"}]},{"city":"Tuxtla Gutiérrez","lat":16.7528,"lng":-93.1156,"why":"State capital and command/laundering node; Sinaloa boss 'El Junior' killed here in 2021","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1990,"to":1999,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2000,"to":2006,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2007,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2021,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2022,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Comitán de Domínguez","lat":16.25,"lng":-92.1333,"why":"Highway junction linking La Mesilla and Frontera Comalapa to San Cristóbal; Sinaloa rear base","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1990,"to":1999,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2000,"to":2006,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2007,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Ciudad Hidalgo (Suchiate)","lat":14.6789,"lng":-92.1503,"why":"Suchiate river crossing to Tecún Umán and nearby Puerto Chiapas: contraband, cocaine and migrant gateway","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1990,"to":1999,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2000,"to":2006,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2007,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2021,"cartel":"sinaloa"},{"from":2022,"to":2026,"cartel":"cjng"}]},{"city":"Chicomuselo","lat":15.7433,"lng":-92.2822,"why":"Sierra Mariscal mining town and Sinaloa stronghold contested by CJNG; site of the May 2024 Nuevo Morelia massacre","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1990,"to":1999,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2000,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]}],"facts":["Chiapas' homicide rate stayed between 6.9 and 12.3 per 100,000 from 2006 to 2020 (SESNSP, peak 2011), among Mexico's lowest, even as cocaine and migrants transited La Mesilla-Frontera Comalapa and the Suchiate.","In 2021 CJNG gunmen killed the Sinaloa Cartel's Chiapas boss Ramón Gilberto Rivera Estrada 'El Junior' in Tuxtla Gutiérrez; Sedena documents show Sinaloa hegemony until 2022, when the CJNG-aligned Cártel de Chiapas y Guatemala was founded by Baldemar Calderón Carrillo 'Don Balde' (killed July 2025) and his son Walfre.","In May 2023 more than 3,000 residents fled Frontera Comalapa and at least 60 people were killed in two weeks; the federal government deployed over 1,500 troops on 29 May 2023.","Between January 2023 and June 2024, 20 violent events displaced 15,780 people in Frontera Comalapa, Chicomuselo, Pantelhó, Chenalhó, La Trinitaria and La Concordia; the Tila exodus of June 2024 (4,000+) was the largest in Chiapas since 1997.","Chicomuselo massacre: on 12-13 May 2024 CJNG gunmen killed 11 civilians (five men, five women and a 15-year-old boy) from a family of Catholic catechists who opposed illegal mining in Nuevo Morelia.","Governor Eduardo Ramírez created the Fuerza de Reacción Inmediata Pakal in December 2024; in June 2025 five state police were killed in an ambush between Las Champas and Ciudad Cuauhtémoc, and on 8 June 2025 Chiapas police crossed into La Mesilla, Guatemala, killing four gunmen.","Chiapas recorded over 3,200 homicides between 2020 and early 2026; SESNSP preliminary counts were 631 victims in 2023 and 662 in 2024."],"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/news/chiapas-bleeds-cjng-sinaloa-cartel-guatemala-trafficking-routes/","https://insightcrime.org/news/behind-criminal-conflict-raging-chiapas-mexico/","https://smallwarsjournal.com/2025/02/20/armed-confrontations-and-forced-evacuation-from-villages-in-chiapas-mexico-the-uncontrolled-southern-border-with-guatemala/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/03/21/guerra-entre-el-cartel-de-sinaloa-y-cjng-provoca-15-mil-desplazamientos-forzados-en-chiapas/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicomuselo_massacre","https://www.razon.com.mx/opinion/2026/03/18/el-clan-que-dejo-crecer-al-cjng/"],"verified":false},{"state":"TAB","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"primary":"none","secondary":["gulf"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"An oil-boom state with no cartel plaza structure; late in the decade Juan García Ábrego's Gulf organization began using the Villahermosa-Coatzacoalcos corridor and the Frontera coast for Colombian cocaine moving toward Tamaulipas."},{"from":1990,"to":2006,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["juarez","sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Gulf Cartel corridor (García Ábrego, then Osiel Cárdenas) from the Guatemalan border via Tenosique and Villahermosa to Veracruz and Tamaulipas, alongside Juárez cartel cocaine flights in the 1990s; homicide rate roughly 5-9 per 100,000."},{"from":2007,"to":2013,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["gulf","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"Los Zetas took Tabasco after breaking from the Gulf Cartel (open war from 2010), running Villahermosa, Cárdenas (plaza chief Alfredo Ceballos 'El Gel' arrested 2011) and the Tenosique migrant corridor from the Petén; state Zetas chief Santos Ramírez 'Santos Sapo' was arrested in 2011. Kidnapping and extortion rose but the homicide rate stayed at 5-7 per 100,000."},{"from":2014,"to":2018,"primary":"fragmented","secondary":["zetas_vieja_escuela","cjng","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"Zetas fragmentation (Zetas Vieja Escuela and Cártel del Noreste remnants), CJNG's entry from Veracruz around 2016-2017 and Sinaloa cells competed over huachicol on the Cárdenas-Villahermosa pipelines and retail drug sales; the rate doubled from 9.8 (2015) to 19.0 per 100,000 (2018)."},{"from":2019,"to":2023,"primary":"barredora","secondary":["cjng","sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"La Barredora, a CJNG-aligned structure, dominated extortion, retail drugs, fuel theft and migrant smuggling under its alleged leader Hernán Bermúdez Requena ('Comandante H'), state security secretary from 11 December 2019 to 5 January 2024 under governors Adán Augusto López and Carlos Merino; leaked Sedena intelligence (October 2022) exposed the nexus. The rate peaked at 22.6 per 100,000 in 2019 and then eased under a protected criminal order."},{"from":2024,"to":2025,"primary":"barredora","secondary":["cjng","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"Bermúdez's exit (January 2024) and the rupture between La Barredora and CJNG-loyal cells, with Sinaloa-linked groups also present, produced Tabasco's worst violence on record: 107 killed in November 2024, 92 in January 2025, 34 in five days in February 2025 and coordinated arson and shootings across Villahermosa on 1-2 April 2025 (Centro, Cárdenas, Macuspana and Nacajuca hardest hit). A federal surge (300 more troops from February 2025) cut daily homicides from 3.21 to 0.81 by December 2025, and Bermúdez was arrested in Paraguay on 12 September 2025."},{"from":2026,"to":2026,"primary":"fragmented","secondary":["cjng","barredora","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"With La Barredora's leadership jailed and 2025 homicides down 77% year on year, remaining CJNG-aligned cells and Barredora remnants fight at lower intensity in Macuspana ('Luki' arrested January 2026), Cárdenas and Villahermosa's Centro municipality; Tabasco supplied 2.9% of national homicides in 2025."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Villahermosa","lat":17.9892,"lng":-92.9475,"why":"State capital (Centro municipality) and Pemex hub; epicenter of the 2024-2025 La Barredora-CJNG war","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1990,"to":2006,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2007,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2018,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2019,"to":2025,"cartel":"barredora"},{"from":2026,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Cárdenas","lat":17.9833,"lng":-93.375,"why":"Western hub on the Coatzacoalcos highway; pipeline fuel-theft zone and Zetas plaza","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1990,"to":2006,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2007,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2018,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2019,"to":2025,"cartel":"barredora"},{"from":2026,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Tenosique","lat":17.4753,"lng":-91.4225,"why":"Guatemalan (Petén) border crossing and origin of the migrant train route; Zetas kidnapping corridor","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1990,"to":2006,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2007,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2018,"cartel":"zetas_vieja_escuela"},{"from":2019,"to":2025,"cartel":"barredora"},{"from":2026,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Paraíso (Dos Bocas)","lat":18.3961,"lng":-93.2136,"why":"Gulf port and the Dos Bocas refinery (2022): maritime smuggling and construction extortion","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1990,"to":2006,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2007,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2018,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2019,"to":2025,"cartel":"barredora"},{"from":2026,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Macuspana","lat":17.7614,"lng":-92.5958,"why":"Eastern crossroads toward Chiapas and Campeche with Pemex fields; violence hotspot 2025-2026","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1989,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1990,"to":2006,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2007,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2018,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2019,"to":2025,"cartel":"barredora"},{"from":2026,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]}],"facts":["Tabasco's homicide rate stayed at 5-9 per 100,000 from 2006 to 2015 (SESNSP), among Mexico's lowest, then climbed to 22.6 in 2019 as fuel theft and extortion spread.","Los Zetas controlled Tabasco from about 2007 to 2013; 2011 arrests included Santos Ramírez Morales 'Santos Sapo', the Zetas' leader in Tabasco, and Alfredo Ceballos Ortiz 'El Gel', plaza chief of Cárdenas.","Hernán Bermúdez Requena served as Tabasco's Secretary of Security from 11 December 2019 to 5 January 2024; leaked military intelligence reported in October 2022 identified him as 'Comandante H', leader of La Barredora, described as CJNG's operational arm in drug retail, extortion, fuel theft and migrant smuggling.","2024 was Tabasco's most violent year on record: November 2024 alone had 107 homicide victims, January 2025 had 92 (the worst January ever) and the first quarter of 2025 had 252 (+66% year on year), the most violent first quarter since 2015.","In February 2025, 34 people were killed in five days plus seven in a Villahermosa prison riot, prompting 300 additional army troops; on 1-2 April 2025 coordinated arson and shootings in Villahermosa neighborhoods (Primero de Mayo, Indeco, Carrizal, Villa Parrilla) triggered a 'código rojo'.","Bermúdez fled Mexico via Mérida in early 2025, was arrested on 12 September 2025 in Mariano Roque Alonso (Asunción area), Paraguay, expelled to Mexico on 17 September 2025 and held at the Altiplano prison on organized crime, kidnapping and extortion charges.","Homicides fell 77% in 2025 versus 2024 by SESNSP daily averages (3.21 per day in early 2025 to 0.81 in December 2025); November 2025 had 41 victims against 107 in November 2024, and Tabasco was 2.9% of national homicides in 2025."],"sources":["https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/former-tabasco-security-minister-arrested-paraguay-organized-crime-charges/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hern%C3%A1n_Berm%C3%BAdez_Requena","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/04/02/violencia-en-tabasco-quema-de-autos-balaceras-y-narcomantas-activan-codigo-rojo-en-villahermosa/","https://www.telemundo.com/shows/al-rojo-vivo/crimen-y-violencia/violencia-en-tabasco-asesinan-a-34-personas-en-5-dias-rcna191255","https://es-us.noticias.yahoo.com/sinaloa-tabasco-registran-m%C3%A1xima-cifra-000037963.html","https://la-verdad.com.mx/homicidios-dolosos-tabasco-cayeron-75-2025-152533.html"],"verified":false},{"state":"CAM","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1991,"primary":"none","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Campeche saw only sporadic Colombian cocaine airdrops and boat landings on the Laguna de Términos and Champotón coast, handled by transient brokers; Ciudad del Carmen boomed as Pemex's offshore hub without any resident cartel structure."},{"from":1992,"to":2001,"primary":"juarez","secondary":["gulf"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The Juárez Cartel's southeastern logistics network, run from Cancún under Governor Villanueva's protection, reportedly used Campeche's jungle airstrips and coast for Colombian cocaine, and the Gulf Cartel moved loads through the peninsula toward Tamaulipas. Presence was purely logistical and the homicide rate stayed below 4 per 100,000."},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["zetas","sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The Gulf Cartel and its Zetas arm expanded from Tabasco into Campeche, using the Escárcega-Candelaria corridor from Guatemala's Petén and Ciudad del Carmen's oil economy; Sinaloa cells kept a foothold in the northern municipalities toward Yucatán."},{"from":2008,"to":2013,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["gulf","sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"Los Zetas turned Ciudad del Carmen into a cash source by extorting Pemex contractors and stealing fuel in the Sonda de Campeche; a Carmen cell was arrested in April 2011 and alleged operator Jorge Luis Martínez Rodríguez 'El Taz' was captured there in August 2012. The state homicide rate rose from 5.6 (2008) to about 8 per 100,000 (2012), still among Mexico's lowest."},{"from":2014,"to":2018,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["sinaloa","cdn","other"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"As the Zetas fragmented, Ciudad del Carmen's retail market split between the ex-Zetas splinter 'Pura Gente Nueva' and the Cártel del Noreste, while Zetas remnants held Calakmul, Hopelchén, Escárcega and Candelaria and the Cártel del Pacífico (Sinaloa) operated in Calkiní, Hecelchakán, Tenabo, Campeche, Champotón and Palizada. The homicide rate peaked at 9.1 per 100,000 in 2016."},{"from":2019,"to":2023,"primary":"fragmented","secondary":["sinaloa","cdn","cjng"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"The UIF's 2019-2020 national risk assessment mapped Zetas remnants, the Pacífico cartel and a CDN-versus-'Pura Gente Nueva' dispute over Carmen retail worth more than one million pesos a week; CJNG cells began arriving via Tabasco and Escárcega and clandestine narco-flights landed across the peninsula. Executions rose from 53 in 2022 to 57 in 2023 amid retail turf killings in Carmen, Escárcega and Champotón."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","other","cdn"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"The DEA's 2025 threat assessment credits the Sinaloa Cartel with 'significant presence' and CJNG with operations in Campeche, while executions climbed to 61 (2024) and 73 (2025) and 16 in the first quarter of 2026. In Ciudad del Carmen the state prosecutor attributed nine executions in January-May 2026 to a retail turf war dominated by the local cell 'Pura Gente Nueva' and ruled out an established CJNG operation; Champotón, Seybaplaya, Calakmul, Calkiní and Escárcega also recorded targeted killings."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Ciudad del Carmen","lat":18.6511,"lng":-91.8073,"why":"Pemex's offshore hub on Laguna de Términos: extortion of oil contractors, fuel theft in the Sonda de Campeche and the state's largest retail drug market ('Pura Gente Nueva' vs CDN).","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1991,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1992,"to":2001,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2008,"to":2015,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2016,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"San Francisco de Campeche","lat":19.8301,"lng":-90.5349,"why":"State capital and Gulf port; Cártel del Pacífico (Sinaloa) financial and retail presence per UIF mapping.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1991,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1992,"to":2001,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2008,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Escárcega","lat":18.61,"lng":-90.7381,"why":"Highway crossroads linking Villahermosa, Chetumal and Mérida: transit node for cocaine, migrants and stolen fuel between Tabasco and the Caribbean.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1991,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1992,"to":2001,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2008,"to":2018,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2019,"to":2026,"cartel":"fragmented"}]},{"city":"Candelaria","lat":18.1858,"lng":-91.0459,"why":"Border municipality with Guatemala's Petén: jungle crossings for cocaine and migrants feeding the Escárcega corridor.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1991,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1992,"to":2001,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2008,"to":2018,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2019,"to":2023,"cartel":"fragmented"},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Champotón","lat":19.35,"lng":-90.7167,"why":"Coastal town south of the capital used for maritime cocaine landings; Pacífico cartel presence per UIF and repeated targeted killings in 2025-2026.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1991,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1992,"to":2001,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2008,"to":2013,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2014,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]}],"facts":["Campeche has long been among Mexico's least violent states: homicide rate 3.6 per 100,000 in 2006, a peak of 9.1 in 2016 and roughly 7-8 through 2020.","Ciudad del Carmen, base of Pemex's offshore Sonda de Campeche fields, was the Zetas' main cash source: contractors were extorted around 2010-2012, a Carmen cell arrested in April 2011 was sentenced in 2016, and alleged Zetas fuel-and-drug operator 'El Taz' was captured there in August 2012.","The UIF's 2019-2020 national risk assessment placed Zetas remnants in Calakmul, Hopelchén, Escárcega and Candelaria, the Cártel del Pacífico in Calkiní, Hecelchakán, Tenabo, Campeche, Champotón and Palizada, and a Cártel del Noreste dispute with the ex-Zetas splinter 'Pura Gente Nueva' over Carmen retail worth more than one million pesos a week.","Violent deaths rose from 53 in 2022 to 57 in 2023, 61 in 2024 and 73 in 2025; 16 homicides were recorded in the first quarter of 2026.","The DEA's 2025 National Drug Threat Assessment lists the Sinaloa Cartel with 'significant presence' and CJNG with operational activity in Campeche.","In May 2026 state prosecutor Jackson Villacís attributed nine executions in Ciudad del Carmen that year to a retail turf war led by the local cell 'Pura Gente Nueva' and ruled out an established CJNG operation.","Candelaria and Calakmul border Guatemala's Petén, and Escárcega's highway junction links Tabasco with Chetumal and Mérida, making the state a transit corridor rather than a production or consumption hub."],"sources":["https://www.poresto.com/campeche/2020/9/22/cartel-del-pacifico-los-zetas-con-presencia-financiera-en-campeche.html","https://www.poresto.com/campeche/2026/2/24/carteles-que-operan-en-campeche-segun-la-dea-y-los-riesgos-en-el-sureste-tras-la-muerte-de-el-mencho.html","https://www.poresto.com/campeche/ciudad-carmen/2026/5/8/narcomenudeo-deja-nueve-ejecuciones-en-ciudad-del-carmen-durante-2026-fiscalia-de-campeche.html","https://www.poresto.com/campeche/2026/3/22/campeche-registra-16-homicidios-en-primer-trimestre-de-2026.html","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/2012/8/10/capturan-el-taz-presunto-operador-de-los-zetas-en-la-sonda-de-campeche-106882.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mexican_states_by_homicides"],"verified":false},{"state":"YUC","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1991,"primary":"none","secondary":[],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Yucatán was untouched by cartel structures: Mérida was a quiet regional capital, Progreso a minor port, and only occasional cocaine overflights crossed the peninsula toward the Gulf."},{"from":1992,"to":2001,"primary":"none","secondary":["juarez","gulf"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Juárez Cartel and Gulf Cartel logistics through peninsula airstrips brushed the state, and Mérida began to be used as a discreet residence and money-laundering base, but no organization held a meaningful share of a tiny criminal economy; the homicide rate stayed near 2 per 100,000, the lowest in Mexico."},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["zetas","sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The Gulf Cartel and its Zetas arm extended retail sales, kidnapping and extortion cells into Mérida and along the Valladolid corridor to Cancún, while Sinaloa-linked figures settled families and assets in Mérida's northern suburbs."},{"from":2008,"to":2012,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["sinaloa","gulf"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"On 28 August 2008 twelve decapitated bodies were found (eleven at Chichí Suárez in Mérida, one in Buctzotz) with a message to state security chief Luis Felipe Saidén Ojeda; alleged Zetas members were arrested the next day. Zetas extortion and retail cells operated in Mérida, Kanasín and Tizimín through 2012, yet the homicide rate never exceeded 2.5 per 100,000."},{"from":2013,"to":2019,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","zetas"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"With the Zetas dismantled, Mérida consolidated as a refuge and laundering hub (real estate, restaurants, casinos) for Sinaloa-linked traffickers and their families, and CJNG cells appeared from about 2017 for retail and logistics; Yucatán posted the country's lowest homicide rate every year (1.5-2.5 per 100,000)."},{"from":2020,"to":2023,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","cdn"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Pandemic-era migration of capital to Mérida deepened its laundering role while Sinaloa and CJNG cells ran low-profile retail in Mérida, Kanasín and Progreso; Cártel del Noreste operators began using the city for luxury-vehicle and financial laundering."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","cdn"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"The DEA's 2025 assessment lists Sinaloa with 'significant' presence and CJNG with 'presence' via retail, logistics and laundering cells; Yucatán closed 2025 with 33 homicides (1.03 per 100,000 versus 12.85 nationally). Cártel del Noreste surfaced publicly: 'El Shamir' was arrested on 21 December 2025 for killing businessman José Ángel Dávila Bernal at a north Mérida mall, and alleged CDN financial operator Justo Aurelio 'N' was detained in Mérida on 19 July 2026 with assets worth over 145 million pesos."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Mérida","lat":20.9674,"lng":-89.5926,"why":"State capital and Mexico's 'safest city' brand: refuge for trafficking families and laundering through real estate, restaurants and casinos rather than a fought-over plaza.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2001,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2008,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Progreso","lat":21.2818,"lng":-89.6631,"why":"Yucatán's Gulf of Mexico container and cruise port, 30 km from Mérida; maritime contraband and cocaine transit toward the US Gulf coast.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2001,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2008,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Valladolid","lat":20.6896,"lng":-88.2011,"why":"Highway hub midway between Mérida and Cancún on the route that links Yucatán's calm interior to the Riviera Maya's drug markets.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2001,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2008,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Kanasín","lat":20.9347,"lng":-89.5578,"why":"Mérida's fast-growing working-class suburb where most of the metropolitan area's retail drug points and rare shootings are concentrated.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2001,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2008,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Tizimín","lat":21.1425,"lng":-88.1519,"why":"Northeastern cattle-ranching town near Buctzotz and the Quintana Roo line; ranch airstrips and the site of the 2008 Zetas decapitation message.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2001,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2008,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]}],"facts":["Yucatán has had Mexico's lowest homicide rate for decades: between 1.2 and 2.5 per 100,000 every year from 2006 to 2020, 1.77 in 2024 and 1.03 in 2025 (33 homicides, versus 12.85 per 100,000 nationally).","On 28 August 2008 twelve decapitated bodies were found, eleven at Chichí Suárez on Mérida's outskirts and one in Buctzotz, with a message addressed to state security chief Luis Felipe Saidén Ojeda; three alleged Zetas were arrested the next day and two Cuban nationals later.","The 2008 massacre remains the deadliest cartel episode in the state's history; the homicide rate that year (2.44 per 100,000) was still the lowest in the country.","The DEA's 2025 National Drug Threat Assessment lists the Sinaloa Cartel with 'significant' presence and CJNG with 'presence' in Yucatán, mainly cells devoted to retail sales, logistics and money laundering.","On 21 December 2025 authorities arrested 'El Shamir', an alleged Cártel del Noreste member, for the murder of businessman José Ángel Dávila Bernal at a shopping plaza in northern Mérida.","On 19 July 2026 federal forces detained alleged Cártel del Noreste financial operator Justo Aurelio 'N' in Mérida, seizing 15 luxury vehicles, weapons, methamphetamine and assets valued at more than 145 million pesos.","Diario de Yucatán reported in March 2026 that foreign networks, including the Albanian-origin Hysa family, used casinos and restaurants in the peninsula to launder money for the Sinaloa Cartel, illustrating Mérida's role as a financial rather than violent plaza."],"sources":["https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/2008/8/29/yucatan-12-decapitados-27418.html","https://www.poresto.com/yucatan/2026/1/9/yucatan-cerro-el-2025-como-el-estado-con-menos-homicidios-dolosos-del-pais-registro-33-casos.html","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/07/19/cae-en-yucatan-presunto-operador-financiero-de-grupo-criminal-autoridades-federales-aseguran-bienes-por-mas-de-145-millones-de-pesos/","https://www.yucatan.com.mx/mexico/2026/02/24/cjng-el-mencho-asi-opera-en-yucatan-campeche-quintana-roo.html","https://www.yucatan.com.mx/merida/2026/01/02/caen-en-yucatan-lideres-criminales-quienes-son-que-hacian-sonados-casos-2025.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mexican_states_by_homicides"],"verified":false},{"state":"ROO","periods":[{"from":1980,"to":1986,"primary":"other","secondary":["guadalajara"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Cancún (opened 1974) served mainly as a Caribbean transshipment and meeting point for Colombia's Medellín Cartel, whose boats and aircraft moved cocaine along the coast and through the Cozumel channel. Ex-DFS agent Rafael Aguilar Guajardo, part of the Guadalajara-era federation, negotiated with Medellín emissaries in Cancún luxury hotels; there was no resident Mexican cartel structure and virtually no violence."},{"from":1987,"to":1993,"primary":"juarez","secondary":["other","gulf"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Aguilar Guajardo led the Juárez organization from 1987 and used Cancún as its Caribbean base; the cartel formally set up operations in Quintana Roo around 1992 as Amado Carrillo Fuentes rose. On 12 April 1993 Aguilar Guajardo was shot dead at a Cancún dock (a US tourist also died), leaving Carrillo in sole command; Colombian suppliers and the Gulf Cartel also used the state's routes."},{"from":1994,"to":2001,"primary":"juarez","secondary":["gulf","sinaloa"],"contested":false,"intensity":1,"note":"Amado Carrillo's operator Alcides Ramón Magaña 'El Metro' ran the Cancún plaza under the protection of Governor Mario Villanueva (1993-1999), accused by US prosecutors of taking up to US$500,000 per cocaine shipment. Villanueva fled on 27 March 1999 and was captured near Cancún on 24 May 2001; 'El Metro' was arrested in Villahermosa in June 2001, dismantling the Juárez network while Gulf and Sinaloa/Beltrán Leyva figures kept a low-profile presence."},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["zetas","sinaloa","juarez"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"With the Juárez network broken, the Gulf Cartel and its armed wing Los Zetas moved into Cancún, Playa del Carmen and the Chetumal-Belize corridor, taking over retail drug sales, kidnapping and extortion of nightlife. Sinaloa-aligned Beltrán Leyva cells also operated; after Osiel Cárdenas's 2007 extradition the Gulf factions began fighting over Cancún and the homicide rate climbed above 14 per 100,000."},{"from":2008,"to":2012,"primary":"zetas","secondary":["gulf","beltran_leyva","sinaloa"],"contested":true,"intensity":2,"note":"Los Zetas, allied with the Beltrán Leyva organization whose hit squad 'Los Pelones' was sent to Cancún, dominated the state; retired Gen. Mauro Enrique Tello Quiñones was murdered in Cancún in February 2009 days after taking an anti-drug post, and a Zetas cell was arrested. After the 2010 Gulf-Zetas split the Zetas kept Cancún and Chetumal, and the August 2010 arson of the Castillo del Mar bar (8 dead) showed the extortion economy behind the violence; homicide rate 15-19 per 100,000."},{"from":2013,"to":2016,"primary":"gulf","secondary":["sinaloa","zetas","cjng"],"contested":false,"intensity":2,"note":"In April 2013 the state attorney general said the Gulf Cartel had driven the Zetas out and that only the Gulf Cartel and 'Los Pelones' (who announced they had joined the Gulf in a December 2012 banner) remained in Cancún and the Riviera Maya. Ex-federal police officer Leticia Rodríguez Lara 'Doña Lety', then linked to the Sinaloa Cartel, took over bar extortion and hotel-zone retail with defectors from the Zetas and Gulf, while CJNG scouts arrived; Cancún recorded only 76 murders in 2013-2016."},{"from":2017,"to":2019,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","gulf","zetas_vieja_escuela"],"contested":true,"intensity":4,"note":"War for the tourist corridor: on 16 January 2017 gunmen killed five at the Blue Parrot club during the BPM festival in Playa del Carmen and the next day attacked the state prosecutor's office in Cancún (4 dead), as Doña Lety's 'Cártel de Cancún' fought an alliance of Zetas, Gulf, Pelones and CJNG cells ('Los Combos'); she was arrested in 2017. Homicide rate jumped from 10.2 per 100,000 (2016) to 21.6 (2017), 42.6 (2018) and 38.2 (2019), with Cancún alone logging 193 murders in 2017 and CJNG blamed for killing the state police commander in September 2019."},{"from":2020,"to":2023,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","gulf","zetas_vieja_escuela"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"Sinaloa and CJNG cells fought over retail markets in Cancún, Playa del Carmen, Tulum and Chetumal alongside Gulf-linked 'Los Pelones', Zetas-linked 'Los Sureños' and 'Los Independientes' on Cozumel and Isla Mujeres; INEGI counted 628 homicides in 2020, 677 in 2021 and 635 in 2022. Tourist-zone shootings marked the period: Tulum (20 Oct 2021, two foreign tourists killed), Puerto Morelos (4 Nov 2021, gunmen landed by boat and killed two dealers in front of the Hyatt Ziva) and Hotel Xcaret (21 Jan 2022, two Canadians killed), while 13,012 extortion reports were filed 2019-Oct 2022, 41.5 percent from Cancún."},{"from":2024,"to":2026,"primary":"sinaloa","secondary":["cjng","gulf"],"contested":true,"intensity":3,"note":"The DEA's 2025 threat assessment lists both the Sinaloa Cartel and CJNG with wide presence in Quintana Roo (retail drugs, fuel theft, extortion, kidnapping); CJNG sent reinforcements from Jalisco, Zacatecas and Michoacán in late 2025 and its cell was blamed for killing an investigative police officer on 16 November 2025 in Playa del Carmen. Federal figures show homicides down 61 percent between October 2024 and August 2025 and roughly 85 percent by June 2026, yet extortion of bars and hotels persists and gunmen attacked a restaurant and a boutique hotel in Tulum in June 2026; El Mencho's death on 22 February 2026 reshuffled CJNG cells on the coast."}],"plazas":[{"city":"Cancún","lat":21.1619,"lng":-86.8515,"why":"Mexico's largest tourist hub: hotel-zone retail drug market worth roughly 100 million pesos a month, bar extortion, laundering and an international airport.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1986,"cartel":"other"},{"from":1987,"to":2001,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2008,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2016,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2017,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Playa del Carmen","lat":20.6296,"lng":-87.0739,"why":"Quinta Avenida nightlife and the Cozumel ferry make it the Riviera Maya's most contested retail-drug and extortion market (Blue Parrot 2017, Xcaret 2022).","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1991,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1992,"to":2001,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2008,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2016,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2017,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Tulum","lat":20.2114,"lng":-87.4654,"why":"Boutique party-tourism boomtown whose beach clubs and cenote-road bars became a cocaine retail battleground (October 2021 crossfire, June 2026 attacks).","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":2001,"cartel":"none"},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2008,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2016,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2017,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Chetumal","lat":18.5036,"lng":-88.3055,"why":"State capital on the Belize border next to the Corozal free zone: crossing point for cocaine, weapons, migrants and contraband from Central America.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1991,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1992,"to":2001,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2002,"to":2009,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2010,"to":2014,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2015,"to":2026,"cartel":"sinaloa"}]},{"city":"Cozumel","lat":20.5083,"lng":-86.9458,"why":"Mexico's busiest cruise port; island retail market run since the late 2010s by the local group 'Los Independientes', with ferry links to Playa del Carmen.","controllers":[{"from":1980,"to":1991,"cartel":"none"},{"from":1992,"to":2001,"cartel":"juarez"},{"from":2002,"to":2007,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2008,"to":2012,"cartel":"zetas"},{"from":2013,"to":2016,"cartel":"gulf"},{"from":2017,"to":2026,"cartel":"other"}]}],"facts":["Juárez Cartel leader Rafael Aguilar Guajardo was shot dead at a Cancún dock on 12 April 1993; US tourist Georgina Knafel was also killed and Amado Carrillo Fuentes is believed to have ordered the hit.","Governor Mario Villanueva (5 April 1993-4 April 1999) was accused of taking up to US$500,000 per Juárez Cartel shipment; he fled on 27 March 1999, was arrested near Cancún on 24 May 2001, extradited to the US on 8 May 2010, and a Mexican sentence of 36 years 9 months was reaffirmed in August 2026.","Retired Gen. Mauro Enrique Tello Quiñones was kidnapped and murdered in Cancún in February 2009, days after being hired as the municipality's anti-drug chief; the army arrested a Zetas cell for the killing.","On 16 January 2017 gunmen killed five people at the Blue Parrot club during the BPM festival in Playa del Carmen; the next day an attack on the state prosecutor's office in Cancún left four dead.","The state homicide rate rose from 10.2 per 100,000 in 2016 to 42.6 in 2018 and 38.2 in 2019; INEGI recorded 628 homicides in 2020, 677 in 2021 and 635 in 2022.","Tourist-zone shootings: Tulum, 20 October 2021 (two foreign tourists killed in crossfire); Puerto Morelos, 4 November 2021 (about 15 gunmen landed by boat and killed two dealers in front of the Hyatt Ziva); Hotel Xcaret, 21 January 2022 (two Canadians killed).","Authorities logged 13,012 anonymous extortion reports between 2019 and October 2022, 41.5 percent from Cancún; the federal security cabinet reported homicides down 61 percent between 1 October 2024 and 11 August 2025."],"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/news/brief/gulf-cartel-now-in-control-of-cancun/","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/articulo/estados/2017/01/23/dos-carteles-se-disputan-quintana-roo/","https://www.excelsior.com.mx/nacional/2017/03/31/1155151","https://www.eluniversal.com.mx/nacion/quien-es-mario-villanueva-gobernador-ligado-al-cartel-de-juarez/","https://www.nacion321.com/seguridad/radiografia-extorsiones-y-violencia-asechan-a-quintana-roo/","https://www.adn40.mx/seguridad/2025-08-15/bajan-61-homicidios-quintana-roo-que-otros-avances-hay-materia-seguridad"],"verified":false}];
